INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT 'technical-seo-audit-checklist', 'Technical SEO Audit Checklist: A Practical Guide for Small Sites', 'A prioritized technical SEO audit checklist you can run in an afternoon: crawlability, indexing, status codes, redirects, sitemaps, duplicates, speed and mobile.', '<p>You publish good content, you fix the obvious things, and your traffic still sits flat. Often the problem is not the writing. It is something underneath it: a page Google cannot crawl, a template that adds <code>noindex</code> to half the blog, or a redirect that loops back on itself.</p><p>These problems are invisible when you browse your own site. Everything looks fine in a browser, so nobody checks. Meanwhile search engines quietly skip the pages that matter most.</p><p>This technical SEO audit checklist gives you a prioritized, step-by-step process you can run on a small site in an afternoon. You will learn what to check, in what order, what a problem looks like, and how to decide which fixes come first. If you are new to the three pillars of SEO, start with <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/dont-let-your-website-turn-invisible-a-beginner-guide">our beginner''s guide to how search works</a> and come back.</p><h2>What a technical SEO audit actually covers</h2><p>A technical audit answers one question: <strong>can search engines find, crawl, understand and index the pages you want to rank?</strong> It does not judge whether your content is good. That comes later.</p><p>Google publishes a short list of minimum requirements a page must meet to be eligible for indexing. Per its <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/essentials/technical">technical requirements for Google Search</a>, Googlebot must not be blocked, the page must return an HTTP 200 status, and it must have indexable content. Almost every step below is a way of checking those three things at scale.</p><p>For example, say you run a bakery site with 40 pages. A technical audit would tell you that your new "Wedding cakes" page returns a 404 from the menu, that two old URLs redirect three times before landing anywhere, and that your sitemap still lists a page you deleted last year.</p><h2>Before you start: tools you need</h2><p>You do not need an expensive stack. Three things cover most small sites:</p><ul><li><strong>Google Search Console</strong>, verified for your domain. It shows what Google has actually crawled and indexed.</li><li><strong>A site crawler</strong> that walks your pages the way a search engine does and lists status codes, redirects, titles and directives. A free desktop crawler like <a href="https://www.scaryspiderseo.com">Scary Spider SEO</a> flags these issues in a sortable table and exports them to CSV.</li><li><strong>A spreadsheet</strong> to log each issue, the affected URLs, the fix and who owns it.</li></ul><p>Run a full crawl first, then work through the checklist with the crawl data and Search Console open side by side.</p><h2>The technical SEO audit checklist, step by step</h2><p>Work through these in order. Each early step can hide problems in later ones, so there is no point tuning page speed on a page Google is not allowed to crawl.</p><h3>1. Crawlability: check robots.txt</h3><p>Open <code>yoursite.com/robots.txt</code> in a browser. You are looking for rules that block pages or folders you want in search.</p><p>The classic disaster is a leftover <code>Disallow: /</code> from a staging site, which blocks everything. A quieter one is blocking the CSS or JavaScript folders Google needs to render your pages.</p><ul><li>Confirm no rule blocks your key sections.</li><li>Confirm the file lists your sitemap URL.</li><li>Crawl once with robots.txt rules respected and compare against a crawl that ignores them, so you can see exactly which URLs are blocked.</li></ul><p>For syntax and safe examples, see <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/robots-txt-guide">what to block in robots.txt</a>.</p><h3>2. Indexability: noindex, canonicals and Search Console</h3><p>A page can be crawlable and still be excluded from the index. The usual causes are a <code>noindex</code> meta robots tag, an <code>X-Robots-Tag</code> HTTP header, or a canonical tag that points at a different URL.</p><p>In your crawl, filter for pages marked noindex and for pages whose canonical is not themselves. Then open the <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/7440203">Page indexing report in Search Console</a>, which lists the reasons Google gives for each URL it has not indexed.</p><p><strong>Not every non-indexed page is a problem.</strong> A thank-you page or a cart page should stay out. What matters is whether any page you want to rank is on that list.</p><h3>3. Status codes: find 4xx and 5xx errors</h3><p>Sort your crawl by status code. Anything other than 200 on a page linked from your own site needs a look:</p><ul><li><strong>404 and 410</strong>: the page is gone. Fix or remove the links pointing to it.</li><li><strong>5xx</strong>: your server failed. If this happens on real pages, treat it as urgent, because Google cannot index a page it cannot fetch.</li><li><strong>Broken images</strong> count too, since they hurt the page experience.</li></ul><p>For a full process, including when to restore a page versus redirect it, read <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/find-and-fix-broken-links">how to find and fix broken links</a>.</p><h3>4. Redirects: chains, loops and internal redirects</h3><p>Redirects are fine when used on purpose. Problems start when they pile up. A redirect chain (A to B to C) slows users down and makes crawlers do extra work. A loop (A to B to A) means the page never loads at all.</p><p>In your crawl, look for chains of more than one hop and for internal links that point at a redirecting URL instead of the final destination. Update those links to point straight at the final URL.</p><p>Also check the type: a permanent move should use a 301 or 308, not a 302. The details are in <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/301-vs-302-redirects">our guide to 301 and 302 redirects</a>.</p><h3>5. XML sitemap: does it match reality?</h3><p>Your sitemap should be a clean list of the URLs you want indexed: canonical, indexable and returning 200. Compare it against your crawl and look for three mismatches:</p><ul><li>URLs in the sitemap that redirect, 404 or are noindexed.</li><li>Indexable pages your crawl found that are missing from the sitemap.</li><li>Pages in the sitemap that no internal link points to (orphan pages).</li></ul><p>If any of these show up, follow the steps to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/xml-sitemap-guide">clean up your XML sitemap</a>.</p><h3>6. Duplicates: titles, descriptions and URL variants</h3><p>Check that each page has a unique title and meta description, and that your site loads on only one version of its address. Type <code>http://</code>, <code>https://</code>, with and without <code>www</code>, into the browser. All four should end on the same final URL.</p><p>Duplicate titles across pages usually point to a template problem, such as every product page using the shop name as its title. Near-duplicate pages, such as several location pages with only the town name changed, compete with each other instead of supporting each other.</p><h3>7. Internal links and click depth</h3><p>Important pages should be reachable within a few clicks of the home page. Pages buried more than three clicks deep, or linked from only one other page, tend to get crawled less often and send weaker signals about their importance.</p><p>Sort your crawl by depth and by number of inlinks. If a key service page sits at depth 5 with one inlink, link to it from the main navigation or from related posts.</p><h3>8. Speed and Core Web Vitals</h3><p>Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/core-web-vitals">Core Web Vitals guidance</a> recommends a Largest Contentful Paint within 2.5 seconds, an Interaction to Next Paint under 200 milliseconds and a Cumulative Layout Shift under 0.1. Check the Core Web Vitals report in Search Console for real-user data, and PageSpeed Insights for individual URLs.</p><p>For a small site, the usual quick wins are oversized images, images without width and height attributes, and slow server response times. Treat speed as a tiebreaker, not a replacement for relevance.</p><h3>9. Mobile and HTTPS</h3><p>Google uses the mobile version of your content for indexing and ranking, according to its <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/mobile/mobile-sites-mobile-first-indexing">mobile-first indexing best practices</a>. So check that every page has a viewport meta tag and that your mobile layout shows the same content, links and structured data as desktop.</p><p>Then check security basics: every page should load over HTTPS, HTTP URLs should redirect to HTTPS, and pages should not load images or scripts over plain HTTP (mixed content).</p><h2>How to prioritize what you find</h2><p>A first audit on even a small site can produce a long list. <strong>Rank every issue by how many important pages it affects and how badly it blocks them</strong>, not by how alarming it sounds.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Priority</th><th>What it looks like</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fix today</td><td>Sitewide <code>Disallow: /</code>, noindex on key pages, 5xx errors, HTTPS not working</td><td>Pages cannot be crawled or indexed at all</td></tr><tr><td>Fix this week</td><td>Broken internal links, redirect chains, sitemap listing dead URLs, duplicate titles from a template</td><td>Wastes crawling and splits signals across URLs</td></tr><tr><td>Fix this month</td><td>Deep pages, pages with one inlink, slow pages, missing image dimensions</td><td>Real gains, but only once the basics work</td></tr><tr><td>Polish</td><td>Uppercase in URLs, single long redirect on an old campaign link</td><td>Low impact on a small site</td></tr></tbody></table><p>One fix often clears dozens of rows. If 200 pages share a duplicate title because of one template setting, that is one task, not 200.</p><h2>Turn the audit into a fix list</h2><p>An audit only helps if the fixes ship. Keep it simple:</p><ol><li>Export the crawl to CSV and group issues by cause, not by URL.</li><li>Write one row per cause with the fix, the owner and a target date.</li><li>Fix the "today" items first, then re-crawl to confirm they are gone.</li><li>For indexing issues, use the Validate Fix button in the Page indexing report so Google rechecks the affected URLs.</li><li>Save the crawl export. Next time, compare the two and see what changed.</li></ol><h2>How often to run a site audit</h2><p>For a small, stable site, a full audit every quarter is usually enough. Run an extra one after any big change: a redesign, a CMS or theme switch, a domain or HTTPS move, or a bulk URL change.</p><p>Between audits, glance at the Page indexing report once a month. A sudden jump in non-indexed pages is the earliest warning you will get.</p><h2>The bottom line</h2><p>A technical SEO audit checklist is not about chasing every warning. It is about confirming that Google can crawl and index your important pages, then removing the friction that wastes crawling and splits your signals.</p><p>Work top to bottom: robots.txt, indexability, status codes, redirects, sitemap, duplicates, internal links, speed, mobile and HTTPS. Fix what blocks pages first, then what dilutes them, then polish.</p><p><strong>Your next step:</strong> crawl your site today, open robots.txt and the Page indexing report, and write down the first three issues that affect pages you actually want to rank.</p>', '["A technical audit checks whether search engines can find, crawl and index the pages you want to rank.", "Work in order, starting with robots.txt and indexability, because early problems hide later ones.", "Compare your XML sitemap against a crawl to catch dead, redirected and orphaned URLs.", "Prioritize issues by how many important pages they affect, and group fixes by root cause.", "Re-crawl after fixing and use Search Console''s Validate Fix to confirm the problems are gone."]', '[{"question": "How long does a technical SEO audit take?", "answer": "For a small site of a few hundred pages, a crawl plus a first pass through the checklist usually fits in an afternoon. Fixing what you find can take longer, depending on whether the causes are in templates, content or server settings."}, {"question": "Can I do a technical SEO audit for free?", "answer": "Yes. Google Search Console is free, PageSpeed Insights is free, and there are free desktop crawlers that list status codes, redirects, titles and directives. A spreadsheet covers the rest."}, {"question": "What is the difference between a technical audit and a content audit?", "answer": "A technical audit checks whether search engines can access and index your pages. A content audit judges whether those pages are useful, accurate and match what searchers want. Run the technical audit first, because great content cannot rank if it is not indexed."}, {"question": "Should I fix every warning an audit tool reports?", "answer": "No. Many warnings are low impact on a small site. Fix anything that blocks crawling or indexing of important pages first, then issues that waste crawling or split signals, and leave cosmetic items for last."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'Technical SEO', 'Technical SEO Audit Checklist for Small Sites', 'Use this technical SEO audit checklist to find crawl, indexing, redirect and sitemap problems on a small site, then fix them in order of real impact.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = 'technical-seo-audit-checklist');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT 'robots-txt-guide', 'Robots.txt Explained: What to Block and What to Leave Alone', 'How robots.txt really works: the syntax you need, safe examples for WordPress and shops, the mistakes that wipe out traffic, and how to test the file before launch.', '<p>A new site goes live, everything looks perfect, and a few weeks later organic traffic is close to zero. Someone finally opens the robots.txt file and finds two lines left over from the staging server: <code>User-agent: *</code> and <code>Disallow: /</code>. Every crawler that respects the file has been told to stay away from the whole site.</p><p>That is the dramatic version. The everyday version is quieter: a rule that blocks the folder holding your CSS, or a pattern that matches far more URLs than anyone intended.</p><p>This guide explains how robots.txt works, the syntax you actually need, safe examples for common setups, the mistakes that cost the most traffic, and how to test the file before it hurts you. It is one step in a wider <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/technical-seo-audit-checklist">technical SEO audit checklist</a>, and probably the quickest one to check.</p><h2>What robots.txt does, and what it does not</h2><p>Robots.txt is a plain text file at the root of your site that tells crawlers which URLs they may request. <strong>It controls crawling, not indexing.</strong> That difference causes most of the confusion around it.</p><p>Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/robots/intro">introduction to robots.txt</a> says the file is mainly for managing crawler traffic and is not a mechanism for keeping a page out of Google. A disallowed URL can still be indexed if other pages link to it. It just shows up without a description, because Google never read the page.</p><p>So if your goal is "keep this page out of search results", robots.txt is the wrong tool. Use a <code>noindex</code> directive or password protection instead. One catch: Google can only see a <code>noindex</code> on a page it is allowed to crawl, so do not block the same URL in robots.txt.</p><p>Robots.txt is also voluntary. Well-behaved crawlers like Googlebot and Bingbot follow it. Scrapers and bad bots can ignore it completely.</p><h2>Where the file lives</h2><p>The file must sit at the top level of the host, for example <code>https://example.com/robots.txt</code>. A file in a subfolder is ignored.</p><p>Each host and protocol gets its own file. According to Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/crawling/docs/robots-txt/robots-txt-spec">robots.txt specification notes</a>, a file at <code>https://example.com/robots.txt</code> does not apply to <code>https://shop.example.com/</code> or to <code>http://example.com/</code>. If you run a blog on a subdomain, it needs its own robots.txt.</p><p>The same page also documents a size limit: Google reads up to 500 KiB of the file and ignores anything after that. A small site will never get close, but auto-generated files with thousands of lines can.</p><h2>Robots.txt syntax in plain English</h2><p>The file is made of groups. Each group starts with one or more <code>User-agent</code> lines naming the crawler, followed by rules for that crawler. Google supports four fields:</p><ul><li><strong><code>User-agent</code></strong>: which crawler the group applies to. <code>*</code> means any crawler that has no more specific group.</li><li><strong><code>Disallow</code></strong>: a path the crawler should not request.</li><li><strong><code>Allow</code></strong>: a path it may request, used to make exceptions inside a disallowed folder.</li><li><strong><code>Sitemap</code></strong>: the full URL of your XML sitemap. This line is independent of the groups and can go anywhere.</li></ul><p>Here is a simple, annotated example:</p><pre><code># Rules for every crawler
User-agent: *
Disallow: /cart/
Disallow: /search
Allow: /search/help

Sitemap: https://example.com/sitemap.xml</code></pre><p>A few rules of the format trip people up:</p><ul><li><strong>Rules are prefix matches.</strong> <code>Disallow: /search</code> blocks <code>/search</code>, <code>/search?q=cake</code> and also <code>/searching-for-venues</code>. Add a trailing slash if you only mean the folder.</li><li><strong>Paths are case-sensitive.</strong> <code>Disallow: /Private/</code> does not block <code>/private/</code>.</li><li><strong>Wildcards are limited.</strong> <code>*</code> matches any sequence of characters and <code>$</code> marks the end of the URL. So <code>Disallow: /*.pdf$</code> blocks URLs ending in .pdf.</li><li><strong>The most specific rule wins.</strong> When an allow and a disallow both match, Google uses the one with the longer path. If they are equally specific, it uses the less restrictive one.</li><li><strong>A crawler follows only one group.</strong> If you add a <code>User-agent: Googlebot</code> group, Googlebot ignores the <code>*</code> group entirely, so repeat any shared rules inside it.</li><li><strong>Some fields are ignored.</strong> Google does not support <code>crawl-delay</code>, even though some other search engines do.</li></ul><p>The underlying protocol is standardized as <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc9309/">RFC 9309, the Robots Exclusion Protocol</a>, though individual search engines can differ on details such as <code>crawl-delay</code>.</p><h2>Safe robots.txt examples for common setups</h2><p>For most small sites, the safest robots.txt is a short one. Start from the examples below and only add rules you can explain.</p><h3>Allow everything (a good default)</h3><pre><code>User-agent: *
Disallow:

Sitemap: https://example.com/sitemap.xml</code></pre><p>An empty <code>Disallow</code> blocks nothing. If your site is a brochure site or a blog with no private areas, this is all you need.</p><h3>WordPress</h3><p>WordPress serves a virtual robots.txt by default that blocks <code>/wp-admin/</code> and allows <code>/wp-admin/admin-ajax.php</code>, because some themes and plugins load front-end content through that file. That default is sensible. Do not add rules for <code>/wp-content/</code> or <code>/wp-includes/</code>, since those folders hold the CSS, JavaScript and images your pages need to render.</p><h3>Online shops</h3><p>Shops often generate thousands of near-identical URLs from internal search, filters and sorting. Blocking those can save crawling for your real product and category pages:</p><ul><li><code>Disallow: /cart/</code> and <code>Disallow: /checkout/</code> for pages with no search value.</li><li><code>Disallow: /search</code> for internal search results.</li><li><code>Disallow: /*?sort=</code> for sorted duplicates of the same category.</li></ul><p>Before blocking a parameter, check that none of your canonical URLs use it. If <code>?page=2</code> is how products deep in a category get discovered, blocking it hides those products from crawlers.</p><h3>Hosted platforms</h3><p>On hosted builders, you may not be able to edit robots.txt directly. The platform manages it, usually through a "hide from search engines" setting. Check that setting is off once the site launches.</p><h2>The robots.txt mistakes that cost the most traffic</h2><p>These are the problems worth checking every time you audit a site, roughly in order of damage.</p><ol><li><strong>Staging rules left in place.</strong> <code>Disallow: /</code> under <code>User-agent: *</code> blocks the entire site. Add "check robots.txt" to your launch checklist and look again after every deployment that touches server config.</li><li><strong>Blocking CSS and JavaScript.</strong> Google renders pages much like a browser does. Google''s intro to robots.txt warns that if missing resources make a page harder to understand, you should not block them. A page that renders as unstyled text, or as an empty shell when the content is loaded by JavaScript, is judged on what Google can see.</li><li><strong>Using disallow to remove a page.</strong> As covered above, a blocked page can stay in the index. Worse, if the page has a <code>noindex</code> tag, the block stops Google from ever reading it.</li><li><strong>Blocking pages that carry redirects or canonicals.</strong> If an old URL redirects to a new one but robots.txt blocks the old URL, crawlers never see the redirect.</li><li><strong>Listing secret paths.</strong> Robots.txt is public. Anyone can read it, so a line like <code>Disallow: /new-pricing-draft/</code> advertises the very page you wanted to hide. Protect private content with a login.</li><li><strong>Overly broad patterns.</strong> <code>Disallow: /p</code> meant for <code>/p/</code> also blocks <code>/products/</code> and <code>/pricing</code>. Always test patterns against real URLs.</li><li><strong>A robots.txt that errors.</strong> If the file returns a server error, Google''s documentation says it stops crawling the site for the first 12 hours while it retries. A 404 for robots.txt, by contrast, is treated as "no restrictions".</li></ol><h2>How to test your robots.txt</h2><p><strong>Never publish a robots.txt change without testing it against real URLs.</strong> Three checks cover most cases:</p><ol><li><strong>Read the live file.</strong> Open <code>/robots.txt</code> in a private browser window and confirm it is the version you expect, returns a 200 and is plain text.</li><li><strong>Use Search Console.</strong> Google''s guide to <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/crawling/docs/robots-txt/create-robots-txt">creating and testing a robots.txt file</a> points to the robots.txt report in Search Console, which shows the version Google last fetched and any parsing problems. For a specific page, the URL Inspection tool tells you whether it is blocked.</li><li><strong>Crawl your site both ways.</strong> Run one crawl that respects robots.txt and one that ignores it, then compare. Any important URL that appears only in the second crawl is blocked. A free desktop crawler like <a href="https://www.scaryspiderseo.com">Scary Spider SEO</a> has optional robots.txt compliance, which makes this comparison easy.</li></ol><p>Say you run a small events site and want to block internal search. You add <code>Disallow: /search</code>, then crawl with robots.txt respected. If your "Searching for a venue? Read this" guide at <code>/searching-for-a-venue</code> has vanished from the results, your rule is too broad. Change it to <code>Disallow: /search/</code> or <code>Disallow: /search?</code> to match how your search URLs are actually built.</p><h2>Robots.txt and your sitemap</h2><p>The <code>Sitemap:</code> line is the one part of robots.txt that helps crawlers find more, not less. Listing your sitemap there means any search engine that reads robots.txt can discover it, not only the ones where you submitted it by hand.</p><p>Keep the two files consistent. If a URL is in your sitemap, it should not be blocked in robots.txt. That contradiction tells Google you both want and do not want the page crawled. Learn how to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/xml-sitemap-guide">build a clean XML sitemap</a> that only lists pages worth indexing.</p><p>If you are still getting to grips with crawling and indexing in general, our <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/dont-let-your-website-turn-invisible-a-beginner-guide">plain-English intro to SEO basics</a> explains how the pieces fit together.</p><h2>The bottom line</h2><p>Robots.txt is a small file with a big blast radius. It tells crawlers where not to go, but it does not remove pages from Google, and it cannot hide anything, because anyone can read it.</p><p>For most small sites, the best robots.txt is short: block genuinely useless crawl paths like carts and internal search, never block CSS or JavaScript, list your sitemap, and test every change against real URLs.</p><p><strong>Your next step:</strong> open <code>yoursite.com/robots.txt</code> right now, check every <code>Disallow</code> line against a real URL, and confirm nothing important is blocked.</p>', '["Robots.txt controls which URLs crawlers may request, not which pages appear in Google.", "A disallowed page can still be indexed if other pages link to it, just without a description.", "Rules are case-sensitive prefix matches, and the most specific matching rule wins.", "Never block the CSS and JavaScript files Google needs to render your pages.", "Test every change with Search Console and a crawl before and after the rule takes effect."]', '[{"question": "Does every website need a robots.txt file?", "answer": "No. If there is no robots.txt file, crawlers assume they can crawl everything, which is fine for many small sites. A short file is still useful because it can list your sitemap location."}, {"question": "How do I block Google from a page completely?", "answer": "Do not rely on robots.txt for that. Add a noindex meta tag or X-Robots-Tag header and make sure the page is crawlable so Google can see it, or put the content behind a login."}, {"question": "How long does it take Google to notice a robots.txt change?", "answer": "Google generally caches robots.txt for up to 24 hours, so a change usually takes effect within a day. If you need Google to pick up an urgent change sooner, Google documents how to submit an updated robots.txt file through Search Console."}, {"question": "Can I block AI crawlers with robots.txt?", "answer": "You can add groups for specific user agents that a crawler operator documents, and crawlers that respect robots.txt will follow them. Check each operator''s own documentation for the exact user-agent name, and remember that robots.txt is voluntary."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'Technical SEO', 'Robots.txt Guide: Syntax, Examples and Mistakes', 'Learn how robots.txt works, see safe disallow examples, avoid the mistakes that block a whole site, and test your file before it costs you traffic.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = 'robots-txt-guide');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT 'xml-sitemap-guide', 'XML Sitemaps: How to Build One Google Actually Uses', 'What belongs in an XML sitemap, what to leave out, how to handle lastmod and size limits, how to submit it to Google, and how to audit it against a crawl of your site.', '<p>Most sites have an XML sitemap these days, usually generated by a plugin nobody has looked at since launch. Open one and you may find a surprise. Picture a site with 120 real pages whose sitemap lists 900 URLs, including tag archives, redirected posts, test pages and URLs marked <code>noindex</code>.</p><p>That sitemap is still "working" in the sense that it exists. But it is sending Google a muddled message about which pages matter, and it hides the pages that genuinely need attention.</p><p>This guide covers what belongs in an XML sitemap, what to leave out, how to handle dates and size limits, how to submit it to Google, and how to audit it against a crawl of your site. It is one step in our <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/technical-seo-audit-checklist">technical SEO audit checklist for small sites</a>.</p><h2>What an XML sitemap is, and what it is not</h2><p>An XML sitemap is a file that lists the URLs on your site you want search engines to crawl and index, optionally with the date each one last changed meaningfully.</p><p><strong>A sitemap is a hint, not an order.</strong> It helps Google discover URLs and understand which ones you consider important, but it does not guarantee that every listed page will be crawled or indexed. Pages still have to earn their place on quality and technical grounds.</p><p>Do you even need one? Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/sitemaps/overview">overview of sitemaps</a> says you might not if your site is small (about 500 pages or fewer that you want in search), well linked internally, and has no images, videos or news content that need to show up in search. In practice, a clean sitemap costs almost nothing, so most sites should still have one.</p><h2>What belongs in your XML sitemap</h2><p>The rule is simple. <strong>Only list URLs that you want to appear in search, exactly as you want them to appear.</strong> Every URL in the file should be:</p><ul><li><strong>Returning a 200 status</strong>, not a redirect or an error.</li><li><strong>Indexable</strong>, with no <code>noindex</code> in a meta robots tag or <code>X-Robots-Tag</code> header.</li><li><strong>Canonical</strong>, meaning the page''s <code>rel="canonical"</code> points to itself, not to another URL.</li><li><strong>Crawlable</strong>, so not blocked by robots.txt.</li><li><strong>Absolute and consistent</strong>, using the same protocol, host and trailing slash style as your live URLs.</li></ul><p>Google''s guide to <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/sitemaps/build-sitemap">building and submitting a sitemap</a> asks for fully qualified, absolute URLs, so <code>https://www.example.com/cakes/</code> rather than <code>/cakes/</code>. A minimal sitemap looks like this:</p><pre><code>&lt;?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?&gt;
&lt;urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9"&gt;
  &lt;url&gt;
    &lt;loc&gt;https://www.example.com/wedding-cakes/&lt;/loc&gt;
    &lt;lastmod&gt;2026-09-14&lt;/lastmod&gt;
  &lt;/url&gt;
&lt;/urlset&gt;</code></pre><h2>What to leave out</h2><p>Most sitemap problems come from listing URLs that contradict other signals on your site. Leave these out:</p><ul><li><strong>Redirected URLs.</strong> List the final destination instead. If you recently moved pages, our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/301-vs-302-redirects">permanent versus temporary redirects</a> explains how to clean up after the move.</li><li><strong>404 and 410 pages.</strong> Deleted pages should drop out of the sitemap at the same time they go offline.</li><li><strong>Noindexed pages.</strong> Listing a page and telling Google not to index it sends opposite signals.</li><li><strong>Non-canonical duplicates</strong>, such as URLs with tracking parameters, sort orders or print versions.</li><li><strong>URLs blocked by robots.txt.</strong> If you are unsure which paths your file blocks, check them against <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/robots-txt-guide">how robots.txt rules match URLs</a>.</li><li><strong>Low-value archive pages</strong> you would not want as a search result, such as empty tag pages or attachment pages.</li></ul><p>Say you run a bakery site with a blog. Your plugin lists 40 tag archives, each with one or two posts. None of them are pages you would want a searcher to land on. Exclude them from the sitemap (and consider noindexing them) so the file points Google at your recipes and product pages instead.</p><h2>Be honest with lastmod</h2><p>The optional <code>&lt;lastmod&gt;</code> field tells search engines when a page last changed. It is only useful if it is true.</p><p>According to the same Google guide, Google uses <code>lastmod</code> when it is consistently and verifiably accurate, and it should reflect the last significant change, such as an update to the main content, structured data or links. Changing the copyright year in the footer does not count.</p><p>The same page says Google ignores <code>&lt;priority&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;changefreq&gt;</code>. You can leave them out entirely.</p><p><strong>Do not set every lastmod to today''s date on each rebuild.</strong> If every URL claims to have changed every day, the field stops carrying any information, and Google is likely to stop trusting it for your site.</p><h2>Size limits and sitemap index files</h2><p>A single sitemap can hold up to 50,000 URLs or 50 MB uncompressed, whichever comes first. Larger sites split their URLs across several sitemaps and list them in a sitemap index file.</p><p>Google''s page on <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/sitemaps/large-sitemaps">managing sitemaps with sitemap index files</a> explains the format: a <code>&lt;sitemapindex&gt;</code> element containing one <code>&lt;sitemap&gt;</code> entry per child sitemap. You then submit only the index file.</p><p>Small sites rarely hit the limits, but splitting can still help diagnosis. Separate sitemaps for pages, posts and products let you see in Search Console which group has indexing problems, instead of one blended number.</p><h2>How to submit your sitemap to Google</h2><p>There are two ways to tell Google where your sitemap lives, and you can use both:</p><ol><li><strong>Search Console.</strong> Open the <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/7451001">Sitemaps report in Search Console</a>, paste the sitemap URL into "Add a new sitemap" and submit. You need owner permissions on the property to do this.</li><li><strong>Robots.txt.</strong> Add a line such as <code>Sitemap: https://www.example.com/sitemap.xml</code>. Any crawler that reads your robots.txt can then find it.</li></ol><p>After submitting, check that the status reads Success. If it shows "Couldn''t fetch", load the sitemap URL in a private browser window. Common causes are a login requirement, a robots.txt block, or the sitemap returning an error page.</p><p>Then open the Page indexing report and filter by your submitted sitemap. That view shows how many of the URLs you asked Google to index actually made it in, and why the rest did not.</p><h2>Audit your sitemap against a crawl</h2><p>A sitemap tells you what you think your site contains. A crawl tells you what is actually reachable. <strong>The useful findings live in the gap between the two.</strong></p><p>Crawl your site with sitemap seeding turned on, so the crawler starts from both your home page and the sitemap URLs. A free desktop crawler like <a href="https://www.scaryspiderseo.com">Scary Spider SEO</a> flags all three mismatches below automatically.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Finding</th><th>What it usually means</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Non-200 or non-indexable URLs in the sitemap</td><td>The sitemap is stale or the generator ignores redirects and noindex</td><td>Remove them, or fix the generator setting</td></tr><tr><td>Indexable pages missing from the sitemap</td><td>Custom pages or post types the generator skips</td><td>Include that content type, or add the URLs</td></tr><tr><td>Orphan pages: in the sitemap but not linked internally</td><td>Old landing pages, or pages dropped from the menu</td><td>Link to them from relevant pages, or retire them</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Orphan pages deserve a second look. If a page matters enough to be in your sitemap, it matters enough to be linked from somewhere on your site. If it does not, ask whether it should exist at all.</p><h2>Common sitemap problems in popular CMS setups</h2><p>Most sitemaps are generated automatically, so problems tend to come from settings rather than typos. Watch for:</p><ul><li><strong>Two sitemaps from two plugins</strong>, for example the CMS''s built-in one and an SEO plugin''s, both submitted with overlapping URLs. Pick one and remove the other.</li><li><strong>Every content type included by default</strong>, including tags, authors, media attachments or custom types that have no search value.</li><li><strong>Staging or development URLs</strong> leaking in after a migration because the site address setting was not updated.</li><li><strong>A cached sitemap</strong> that a caching plugin or CDN serves long after pages change.</li></ul><p>The fix is usually one settings change, then a fresh crawl to confirm the sitemap matches reality.</p><h2>The bottom line</h2><p>A good XML sitemap is a short, honest list: canonical, indexable URLs that return 200, with lastmod dates that only change when the content does. Everything else adds noise.</p><p>Submit it in Search Console and robots.txt, then audit it against a crawl regularly. The gaps between your sitemap and your real site structure point straight at the pages that need fixing.</p><p><strong>Your next step:</strong> open your sitemap in a browser, pick ten URLs at random, and check that each one loads, is indexable and points its canonical at itself.</p>', '["An XML sitemap should list only canonical, indexable URLs that return a 200 status.", "A sitemap is a hint that helps discovery, not a guarantee that pages will be indexed.", "Google uses lastmod only when it is consistently accurate and ignores priority and changefreq.", "One sitemap holds up to 50,000 URLs or 50 MB uncompressed, and larger sites use a sitemap index.", "Comparing your sitemap against a crawl reveals stale URLs, missing pages and orphan pages."]', '[{"question": "Where do I find my website''s sitemap?", "answer": "Try yoursite.com/sitemap.xml or yoursite.com/sitemap_index.xml, and check the Sitemap line in yoursite.com/robots.txt. Your CMS or SEO plugin settings will also show the sitemap URL it generates."}, {"question": "How often should I update my XML sitemap?", "answer": "Whenever pages are added, removed or meaningfully changed. Most CMS sitemaps update automatically, so the real task is checking that the generator settings exclude redirects, noindexed pages and low-value archives."}, {"question": "Do I need to resubmit my sitemap after every change?", "answer": "No. Once Google knows where your sitemap is, it fetches it again on its own. Resubmitting is useful after a major change, such as a site migration or a new sitemap URL."}, {"question": "Should images be in my XML sitemap?", "answer": "You can add image entries using Google''s image sitemap extension, which can help Google find images it might otherwise miss. For most small sites with images in normal img tags, it is optional."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'Technical SEO', 'XML Sitemap Guide: Build, Submit and Audit', 'Build an XML sitemap Google can trust: list only canonical, indexable URLs, keep lastmod honest, submit it in Search Console and audit it against a crawl.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = 'xml-sitemap-guide');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT 'find-and-fix-broken-links', 'How to Find Broken Links and Fix Them Before They Cost You Traffic', 'Learn how to find broken links with a site crawl and Search Console, then fix each one: update the link, restore the page, or redirect to the closest match.', '<p>You click a link in your own navigation and land on a "Page not found" message. Or a customer emails to say the "Download our price list" button goes nowhere. Either way, you have just met a broken link, and it is rarely the only one. The quickest way to find broken links is to stop waiting for visitors to report them and check the whole site at once.</p><p>Broken links build up quietly. Pages get renamed, products get retired, a blog post you linked to five years ago disappears, and nobody updates the links that pointed at them. On a site of any size, a few dead links per year turns into dozens.</p><p>This guide explains the different kinds of broken links, why they matter for visitors and search engines, how to find them with a crawl and in Google Search Console, and a simple decision tree for fixing each one. It is the status code step of our <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/technical-seo-audit-checklist">technical SEO audit for small sites</a>, covered in full.</p><h2>What counts as a broken link</h2><p>A broken link is any link whose destination does not return a working page. <strong>The status code the server sends back is what tells you whether a link is broken, not how the page looks.</strong> The usual suspects:</p><ul><li><strong>404 Not Found</strong>: the URL does not exist. By far the most common.</li><li><strong>410 Gone</strong>: the page was removed on purpose.</li><li><strong>5xx server errors</strong>: the server failed to produce the page, either briefly or every time.</li><li><strong>Timeouts and DNS failures</strong>: the domain no longer exists or never answers, which is common with links to old external sites.</li><li><strong>Soft 404s</strong>: the page says "not found" or is empty, but the server still returns 200 OK. These look fine to a basic checker and are easy to miss.</li></ul><p>It also helps to separate links by where they live:</p><ul><li><strong>Internal broken links</strong> point from one page on your site to another page on your site. You control both ends, so these are the easiest to fix and the most important.</li><li><strong>Outbound broken links</strong> point from your site to someone else''s. You cannot fix their page, but you can fix your link.</li><li><strong>Inbound links to dead pages</strong> are links on other sites that point at a URL you removed. You cannot edit their link, but you can decide what that URL does now.</li></ul><p>Broken images, scripts and stylesheets follow the same logic. A missing image file is a broken link inside your page, even if no visitor ever clicks it.</p><h2>Why broken links matter</h2><p>A single dead link will not sink a site. Google is clear that 404s on some URLs do not count against the rest. Its post on whether <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/blog/2011/05/do-404s-hurt-my-site">404 errors hurt your site</a> says the fact that some URLs return 404 does not affect how your other, working URLs perform in search results. So this is not about a penalty. It is about three practical costs.</p><h3>1. Broken crawl paths</h3><p>Search engines find pages by following links. Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/links-crawlable">link best practices</a> describe links as a way to find new pages to crawl and a signal of relevance. When your only internal link to a page is broken, you have cut the path to it. When a menu link is broken, every page that uses that menu repeats the dead end.</p><h3>2. Lost visitor trust</h3><p>A visitor who hits a dead end on your site usually does not hunt for the right page. They hit back. On a checkout path, a booking flow or a "Contact us" link, that is a lost lead, not just a bad impression.</p><h3>3. Wasted link value</h3><p>If other sites link to a page you deleted, those links now point at nothing. The visitors they send land on an error, and any value those links carried is not reaching a live page. Earning those links in the first place is the hard part, as our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/offpage-seo/off-page-seo-guide">earning backlinks and authority</a> explains, so letting them go to waste is an expensive mistake.</p><p>Say you run a garden center site and you retire a popular "How to plant spring bulbs" page during a redesign. Local newsletters and a gardening forum link to it. After the redesign, every one of those links ends on a 404, and the internal "Seasonal tips" box on twenty product pages does too.</p><h2>How to find broken links on your site</h2><p>Use two sources, because each catches things the other misses. A crawl shows you what is broken right now and which page links to it. Search Console shows you what Google has actually tried to fetch, including old URLs that only external sites still link to.</p><h3>Crawl your site</h3><p>A site crawler starts at your home page, follows every link the way a search engine does, and records the status code of each URL it finds. <strong>The useful output is not just the broken URL but the pages that link to it</strong>, because that is where you make the fix.</p><p>A free desktop crawler like <a href="https://www.scaryspiderseo.com">Scary Spider SEO</a> flags 4xx and 5xx pages, broken links and images, and pages that link to non-200 URLs, then exports the list to CSV. Whatever tool you use, check a few things:</p><ul><li>Turn on checking for external URLs if your tool supports it, so outbound links are tested too.</li><li>Sort by status code and group the results by broken URL. One dead page linked from 40 places is one fix in a template, not 40.</li><li>Recheck outbound "broken" links by hand before you change them. Some sites return 403 or 429 to automated requests while working fine in a browser.</li></ul><h3>Check the Page indexing report in Search Console</h3><p>In Search Console, open Indexing, then Pages, and look under the reasons pages are not indexed. Two statuses matter here, and Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/7440203">Page indexing report documentation</a> explains both:</p><ul><li><strong>Not found (404)</strong>: Google requested the URL and got a 404. Google says it may have found the URL through a link from another page, or the page existed and was deleted. It also notes that a 404 is not necessarily a problem if the page was removed with no replacement, and that a moved page should get a 301.</li><li><strong>Soft 404</strong>: the page looks like an error to Google but returns 200. Google recommends returning a real 404 status for pages that truly do not exist.</li></ul><p>Click a URL in either list and use URL Inspection to see more detail, including how Google discovered it. Expect old URLs here that your own site no longer links to. Those usually come from external links or old sitemaps, which is exactly what a crawl of your own site cannot show you.</p><h3>Find external links pointing at dead pages</h3><p>The Links report in Search Console lists your top linked pages from other sites. Export that list and check the status code of each URL. Any URL that has external links but now returns 404 is a page worth redirecting, because people may still be arriving there.</p><h2>How to fix broken links: a decision tree</h2><p>Every broken link has two ends: the link and the destination. <strong>Ask these questions in order and stop at the first "yes".</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Is the link simply wrong?</strong> A typo, a missing slash, or an old URL after a rename. Update the link so it points straight at the correct live URL.</li><li><strong>Should the page still exist?</strong> If it was deleted by accident, or still gets traffic and links, restore it at its original URL.</li><li><strong>Is there a close equivalent?</strong> If the content moved or merged into another page, add a 301 redirect from the old URL to that page, then update your internal links to point at the new URL directly. Our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/301-vs-302-redirects">choosing between 301 and 302 redirects</a> covers which code to use and how to avoid chains.</li><li><strong>Is it gone for good, with no replacement?</strong> Let the URL return 404 or 410 and remove or replace every internal link to it.</li></ol><p>Two rules keep this honest. First, <strong>do not redirect every dead URL to your home page.</strong> A visitor who wanted a specific article and lands on the home page is no better off than with a 404, and an unrelated destination does not tell Google where the content went.</p><p>Second, 404 and 410 are close enough that you do not need to worry much about which one to use. Google''s documentation on <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/crawling/docs/troubleshooting/http-status-codes">how status codes affect its crawlers</a> says all 4xx errors except 429 are treated the same, and that URLs already in the index are removed once they return a 4xx.</p><p>For outbound links the tree is shorter, because you only control your end:</p><ul><li>Search the other site for the new location of the page and update your link.</li><li>If it is gone, link to another reliable source that backs the same point.</li><li>If no replacement exists and the sentence still makes sense without it, remove the link.</li></ul><h2>Fix the source, not just the symptom</h2><p>A redirect catches old bookmarks and links from other sites. It should not be how your own navigation works. If your menu links to a URL that redirects, every visit and every crawl takes an extra hop to reach a page you control.</p><p>After any fix, update the places that point at the old URL:</p><ul><li>Navigation, footer and sidebar templates, where one change fixes every page.</li><li>Body links in posts and pages.</li><li>Canonical tags that reference the old URL.</li><li>Your XML sitemap. A sitemap that still lists deleted pages sends Google back to them, so <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/xml-sitemap-guide">keep your sitemap in sync</a> with what actually exists.</li></ul><p>Then recrawl. Your list of broken links and internal redirects should shrink to zero, apart from anything you deliberately left as a 404.</p><h2>Build a custom 404 page that helps</h2><p>Some 404s are unavoidable. People mistype URLs and old links live on for years. A good custom 404 page turns that dead end into a detour.</p><p>A useful 404 page:</p><ul><li>Says plainly that the page could not be found, without jargon.</li><li>Uses the same header, navigation and styling as the rest of the site.</li><li>Offers a search box and links to your main sections or most popular pages.</li><li>Gives a way to report the problem if the visitor followed a link on your site.</li></ul><p>The technical catch: <strong>a custom 404 page must still return a 404 status code.</strong> If your server sends 200 with a "not found" message, you have created soft 404s. Check with a terminal by requesting a URL you know does not exist:</p><pre><code>curl -I https://example.com/this-page-does-not-exist

HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found</code></pre><p>If you see <code>HTTP/1.1 200 OK</code> instead, fix the server or CMS setting so missing pages return a real 404. Also watch for "page not found" templates that some themes serve with a 200 for empty category or search pages.</p><h2>Keep broken links from coming back</h2><p>Fixing broken links once is satisfying. Keeping the count near zero takes a small habit, not a big project.</p><ul><li><strong>Before you delete or rename a page</strong>, check which pages link to it and decide on the redirect at the same time.</li><li><strong>After a redesign, migration or CMS change</strong>, crawl the site the same day and compare against the previous crawl.</li><li><strong>On a schedule</strong>, crawl monthly for an active site or quarterly for a small, stable one, and check the Page indexing report in between.</li><li><strong>For outbound links</strong>, prefer linking to stable pages, such as official documentation, over pages likely to move.</li></ul><p>Broken links on other people''s sites can also be an opportunity. Finding dead links on relevant pages and offering your own content as a replacement is a link building technique in its own right, but that is outreach, and a separate job from cleaning up your own site.</p><h2>The bottom line</h2><p>To find broken links, combine a crawl of your site with the Page indexing report in Search Console. The crawl shows you which of your pages contain dead links. Search Console shows you which old URLs Google and other sites still try to reach.</p><p>Fix each one in order: correct the link, restore the page, redirect to the closest equivalent, or let it 404 cleanly. Then update your templates and sitemap so your own site never relies on a redirect, and make sure your 404 page returns a real 404.</p><p><strong>Your next step:</strong> crawl your site today, export every URL that returns a 4xx or 5xx along with the pages that link to it, and fix the broken links in your navigation and templates first.</p>', '["A broken link is any link whose destination returns a 4xx, 5xx, timeout or soft 404 instead of a working page.", "Google says 404s on some URLs do not hurt how your other pages perform, but broken links still cut crawl paths, frustrate visitors and waste backlinks.", "Combine a site crawl, which shows which of your pages contain dead links, with the Search Console Page indexing report, which shows old URLs Google still tries to fetch.", "Fix each broken link in order: correct the link, restore the page, 301 redirect to the closest equivalent, or let it return 404 or 410 cleanly.", "A custom 404 page should help visitors find their way while still returning a real 404 status code, never a 200."]', '[{"question": "Do broken links hurt SEO?", "answer": "Not directly as a penalty. Google has said that 404s on some URLs do not affect how your working URLs perform. The real costs are indirect: broken internal links cut the paths crawlers use to find pages, visitors leave, and backlinks pointing at dead pages stop reaching a live page."}, {"question": "How often should I check my site for broken links?", "answer": "A monthly crawl suits an active site that publishes or changes pages often, and a quarterly crawl is usually enough for a small, stable site. Always run an extra crawl straight after a redesign, migration or bulk URL change, because that is when broken links appear in large numbers."}, {"question": "Should I redirect all 404 pages to my home page?", "answer": "No. Redirect a dead URL only when there is a genuinely close equivalent page. Sending every missing page to the home page leaves visitors no better off than a 404 and does not tell Google where the content went. If there is no replacement, let the URL return 404 or 410."}, {"question": "What is the difference between a 404 and a soft 404?", "answer": "A 404 is a real HTTP status code telling browsers and crawlers that the page does not exist. A soft 404 is a page that looks like an error or is empty but returns a 200 OK status. Google reports soft 404s in Search Console and recommends returning a real 404 status for pages that do not exist."}, {"question": "Is a 410 better than a 404 for deleted pages?", "answer": "For Google it makes little practical difference. Google''s documentation says it treats all 4xx errors except 429 the same way and removes already indexed URLs that return them. Use 410 if you want to state clearly that removal was deliberate, but a 404 is perfectly fine."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'Technical SEO', 'How to Find Broken Links and Fix Them for Good', 'Find broken links with a crawl and Search Console, then fix each one the right way: update the link, restore the page, 301 it, or let it 404 cleanly.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = 'find-and-fix-broken-links');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT '301-vs-302-redirects', '301 vs 302 Redirects: Which to Use and How to Fix Chains', 'Learn the real difference between 301 and 302 redirects, when 307 and 308 fit, and how to clean up redirect chains, loops and meta refresh after a migration.', '<p>You changed a URL, added a redirect, and moved on. Months later the old address still turns up in search results, or a crawl shows that half your internal links pass through two or three hops before they reach a real page. Nothing is technically broken, but nothing is clean either.</p>
<p>Most of these problems trace back to one decision made in a hurry: the <strong>301 vs 302 redirect</strong> choice. A 301 says "this page has moved for good." A 302 says "this page is somewhere else for now." Google treats those two messages differently, so the code you pick changes which URL ends up in search results.</p>
<p>In this guide you will learn what each redirect code tells Google, when 307 and 308 make more sense, how redirect chains and loops form, why meta refresh is a fallback rather than a plan, and how to clean everything up after a site migration. If you are working through a full <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/technical-seo-audit-checklist">technical SEO audit checklist</a>, this is the redirects step in detail.</p>

<h2>What a redirect actually tells Google</h2>
<p>A redirect is an HTTP response that says "the thing you asked for lives at another URL." The browser or crawler then requests the new address. Visitors rarely notice which type of redirect you used. Google does.</p>
<p>According to <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/301-redirects">Google''s documentation on redirects</a>, <strong>a permanent redirect is a signal that the target URL should become canonical, while a temporary redirect is not.</strong> In plain terms, a permanent redirect tends to put the new URL in search results, and a temporary one tends to keep the old URL there.</p>
<p>Say you rename <code>/services/web-design</code> to <code>/services/website-design</code>. With a 301, Google is told the new URL is the one to keep, so over time it swaps the address shown in results. With a 302, Google is told the move is temporary, which gives it a reason to keep showing the old address.</p>
<p>What to do:</p>
<ul>
<li>Decide whether the move is permanent before you choose a code.</li>
<li>If you will never switch back, use a permanent redirect.</li>
<li>If the original URL will return, use a temporary one.</li>
</ul>

<h2>301 vs 302 redirect: the comparison table</h2>
<p>There are four redirect codes worth knowing. Two are permanent, two are temporary, and within each pair the difference is about how the browser treats the request method.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Type</th><th>How Google treats it</th><th>Request method</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>301 Moved Permanently</td><td>Permanent</td><td>Target is a strong canonical candidate</td><td>GET stays GET, other methods may switch to GET</td><td>Changed slugs, HTTPS moves, domain changes, merged pages</td></tr>
<tr><td>308 Permanent Redirect</td><td>Permanent</td><td>Same as 301</td><td>Method and body preserved</td><td>Permanent moves where forms or APIs send POST requests</td></tr>
<tr><td>302 Found</td><td>Temporary</td><td>Not used as a canonical signal</td><td>GET stays GET, other methods may switch to GET</td><td>Short-term detours, maintenance notices</td></tr>
<tr><td>307 Temporary Redirect</td><td>Temporary</td><td>Same as 302</td><td>Method and body preserved</td><td>Temporary moves where non-GET requests must survive</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The method column comes from the HTTP specification and is summarized clearly in <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/Redirections">MDN''s guide to HTTP redirections</a>. On an ordinary content site, where browsers and crawlers mostly send GET requests, 301 and 308 behave the same for SEO, and so do 302 and 307. Google groups 301 and 308 as permanent, and 302, 303 and 307 as temporary.</p>
<p><strong>For most site owners the rule is simple: use a 301 for permanent moves and a 302 for genuinely temporary ones.</strong> Reach for 308 or 307 when a developer needs the request method preserved, such as a form endpoint that moved.</p>

<h2>When to use a 301 (or 308)</h2>
<p>Use a permanent redirect whenever the old URL is not coming back. Common cases:</p>
<ul>
<li>You changed a page''s slug or folder structure.</li>
<li>You moved from HTTP to HTTPS, or picked www over non-www.</li>
<li>You merged two thin pages into one stronger page.</li>
<li>You retired a product and have a direct replacement.</li>
<li>You moved the whole site to a new domain.</li>
</ul>
<p>Say you run a bakery site with 40 pages, and you merge "Wedding Cakes" and "Custom Wedding Cakes" into one page. The URL that disappears gets a 301 to the one that stays, so anyone following an old link or bookmark lands on the merged page.</p>
<p>One caution: <strong>redirect to the closest equivalent page, not to your home page.</strong> Sending every retired URL to the home page confuses visitors who expected something specific. If a page is gone with no replacement, a 404 or 410 is the honest answer. If you are unsure which applies, here is how to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/find-and-fix-broken-links">decide between restoring, redirecting, or letting a page 404</a>.</p>

<h2>When to use a 302 (or 307)</h2>
<p>A temporary redirect is right when you want visitors sent elsewhere for a while, but you want the original URL to keep its place in search. Google''s own example is a service that is temporarily unavailable: you redirect to a page explaining the situation without giving up the original URL in results.</p>
<p>Other fair uses include a booking page sent to a "back soon" notice during a system upgrade, or a product page sent to a holding page while stock is replaced. In each case the original URL will serve real content again.</p>
<p>The far more common mistake runs the other way: <strong>a permanent move that was set up as a 302 by accident.</strong> Some tools default to temporary. Apache''s <code>Redirect</code> directive returns a 302 unless you specify otherwise, and so does PHP''s <code>header(''Location: ...'')</code> on its own. Many CMS redirect plugins let you pick the type, and it is easy to leave the wrong option selected.</p>
<p>Check the actual status code rather than trusting the settings screen. From a terminal:</p>
<pre><code>curl -I https://example.com/old-page

HTTP/1.1 301 Moved Permanently
Location: https://example.com/new-page</code></pre>
<p>If you see <code>302 Found</code> on a move you meant to be permanent, change the rule to a 301 and recheck.</p>

<h2>Redirect chains and loops</h2>
<p>A redirect chain is when one redirect leads to another before reaching a page that returns 200. Chains usually build up over years, one sensible change at a time. Here is a typical one:</p>
<ol>
<li><code>http://example.com/blog/post</code> redirects to the HTTPS version.</li>
<li><code>https://example.com/blog/post</code> redirects to the www version.</li>
<li><code>https://www.example.com/blog/post</code> redirects to the version with a trailing slash.</li>
<li><code>https://www.example.com/blog/post/</code> finally returns the page.</li>
</ol>
<p>Each hop is another round trip for the visitor''s browser and another request for a crawler. Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/site-move-with-url-changes">site move guidelines</a> say Googlebot can follow up to 10 hops, but advise redirecting straight to the final destination and, if a chain is unavoidable, keeping it to no more than 3 hops and fewer than 5. They also note that chains add latency and that not every browser handles long chains.</p>
<p>A redirect loop is worse: A redirects to B, and B redirects back to A. The page never loads for anyone. Loops often appear when two rules disagree, for example a server rule that adds a trailing slash and a CMS setting that removes it. Search Console reports both long chains and loops as a "Redirect error" in the Page indexing report.</p>
<p>How to fix them:</p>
<ul>
<li>List every redirect source and its final destination.</li>
<li>Rewrite each rule so the source points directly at the final 200 URL.</li>
<li>Order your rules so protocol and host fixes happen in a single hop, not three.</li>
<li>Test a sample of old URLs and confirm one 301 followed by a 200.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Internal links that point at redirects</h2>
<p>Even a single, correct 301 is a detour when it sits behind your own internal links. If your navigation links to <code>/services/web-design</code> and that URL redirects, every visit and every crawl takes an extra step to reach a page you control.</p>
<p><strong>Your own links should point at the final URL, not at a redirect.</strong> Redirects are there to catch old bookmarks and links from other sites, not to patch your own navigation.</p>
<p>Say your footer, three blog posts and a services menu all link to an old URL. Update all five links to the new address and the redirect becomes a safety net that your own site never needs. Apply the same rule to your sitemap: list only final URLs, never redirecting ones, and <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/xml-sitemap-guide">keep your XML sitemap clean</a> after any URL change.</p>
<p>Finding these by hand is slow on anything bigger than a handful of pages. A free desktop crawler like <a href="https://www.scaryspiderseo.com">Scary Spider SEO</a> flags internal redirects, long redirect chains and meta refresh redirects in one crawl, so you can export the list and fix the links at the source.</p>

<h2>Meta refresh and JavaScript redirects</h2>
<p>Sometimes you cannot touch server settings, for example on a locked-down hosting plan. Two fallbacks exist, and both are second best.</p>
<p>A meta refresh is an HTML tag in the page head, such as <code>&lt;meta http-equiv="refresh" content="0; url=https://example.com/new"&gt;</code>. Google interprets an instant meta refresh (0 seconds) as a permanent redirect and a delayed one as a temporary redirect. MDN also recommends keeping the delay at 0 for accessibility, because a page that jumps after a few seconds can disorient visitors.</p>
<p>A JavaScript redirect sets <code>window.location</code> in a script. Google says to use it only if server-side and meta refresh redirects are not possible, because Google only sees it after rendering the page, and rendering can fail.</p>
<p><strong>Use a server-side 301 whenever you can, and treat meta refresh and JavaScript as fallbacks.</strong> If a crawl shows meta refresh redirects you did not create on purpose, they often come from old landing page builders or a theme setting, and are worth replacing.</p>

<h2>A redirect cleanup process after a site migration</h2>
<p>Migrations are where redirects go wrong at scale: a new CMS, a new URL structure, or a new domain. Work through these steps in order.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Capture every old URL before you switch.</strong> Combine a full crawl, your current sitemap, and the top landing pages from Search Console and your analytics.</li>
<li><strong>Map each old URL to its closest new equivalent.</strong> One to one wherever possible. Keep the map in a spreadsheet with columns for old URL, new URL and status.</li>
<li><strong>Implement the map as server-side 301s.</strong> Avoid sending large groups of unrelated URLs to the home page.</li>
<li><strong>Test the old URL list after launch.</strong> Every row should return a single 301 to a page that returns 200. Fix chains, loops and 404s now.</li>
<li><strong>Update everything you control.</strong> Internal links, canonical tags, your sitemap, and hreflang tags if you use them should all reference the new URLs directly.</li>
<li><strong>Leave the redirects in place.</strong> Google''s site move guidance says to keep them for as long as possible, generally at least one year, so signals and external links can be reassigned to the new URLs.</li>
<li><strong>Monitor Search Console.</strong> "Page with redirect" for old URLs is expected. "Redirect error" and a rising count of 404s are not, and need attention.</li>
</ol>
<p>Say a 60-page site moves from <code>/index.php?page=about</code> style URLs to clean paths like <code>/about</code>. The map has 60 rows, the test takes minutes with a crawler in list mode or a short script, and the payoff is that every old link, bookmark and search result still lands somewhere useful.</p>

<h2>The bottom line</h2>
<p>The 301 vs 302 redirect decision comes down to one question: is the old URL ever coming back? If not, use a 301 (or 308) so Google moves its attention to the new page. If it is, use a 302 (or 307) and keep the original URL in place.</p>
<p>After that, keep it tidy. One hop from old to new, internal links and sitemaps pointing at final URLs, no accidental meta refresh, and redirects that stay live for at least a year after a migration.</p>
<p><strong>Your next step:</strong> run <code>curl -I</code> on five URLs you have changed in the past year, confirm each returns a single 301 to a live page, and fix any that return a 302 or pass through more than one hop.</p>', '["Google treats 301 and 308 redirects as permanent and uses them as a signal that the target URL should be canonical.", "A 302 or 307 tells Google the move is temporary, so the original URL tends to stay in search results.", "Some server rules and code default to 302, so always check the actual status code of a permanent move.", "Redirect chains and loops waste requests and slow visitors down, so point every redirect straight at the final URL.", "After a migration, map old URLs one to one, update internal links and sitemaps, and keep redirects live for at least a year."]', '[{"question": "Do 301 redirects pass link equity?", "answer": "Google uses a permanent redirect as a strong signal that the target URL should be canonical, and signals for duplicate URLs are consolidated onto the canonical. In practice a clean, one-hop 301 to a closely matching page is the standard way to move a page without starting over. Chains and redirects to unrelated pages are where problems start."}, {"question": "How long should I keep a 301 redirect in place?", "answer": "Google''s site move guidance says to keep redirects for as long as possible, and generally at least one year. If old URLs still receive links or visits after that, there is little reason to remove them."}, {"question": "Will a 302 redirect hurt my rankings?", "answer": "A 302 is not a penalty. The risk is that a permanent move set up as a 302 tells Google the old URL should stay in results, which can leave the wrong address showing. If the move is permanent, switch it to a 301."}, {"question": "Is a 308 redirect better than a 301 for SEO?", "answer": "Google treats 301 and 308 the same way, as permanent redirects. The difference is technical: a 308 guarantees the browser keeps the original request method, which matters for forms and APIs. For normal page moves either works."}, {"question": "How can I tell which type of redirect a page uses?", "answer": "Run curl -I on the old URL in a terminal and read the status line, or open your browser''s developer tools and check the Network tab. A site crawler will also report the status code and final destination for every redirecting URL it finds."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'Technical SEO', '301 vs 302 Redirect: When to Use Each for SEO', '301 vs 302 redirect explained: what each tells Google, when to use 307 or 308, and how to find and fix redirect chains, loops and meta refresh redirects.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = '301-vs-302-redirects');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT 'why-google-is-not-indexing-your-pages', 'Page Not Indexed? Why Google Skips Pages and How to Fix It', 'Page not indexed by Google? Learn what each Search Console indexing status means, which ones you can ignore, and a step-by-step process to fix the pages that matter.', '<p>You published a new service page two weeks ago and it still does not show up in Google. You open Search Console and find a long list of URLs under "Why pages aren''t indexed," with labels like "Crawled - currently not indexed" and "Duplicate without user-selected canonical." You have a <strong>page not indexed</strong> by Google and no clear instruction on what to change.</p>
<p>This is one of the most common worries for small site owners, and one of the most misunderstood. Some of those statuses are real problems. Many are Google working exactly as intended. The trick is telling them apart.</p>
<p>This guide explains the difference between crawling, indexing and ranking, walks through the main Page indexing report statuses and what usually causes each one, and gives you a repeatable process to fix the pages that matter.</p>

<h2>Crawl, index, rank: three different steps</h2>
<p>Google describes Search as <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/how-search-works">three stages: crawling, indexing and serving results</a>. Crawling is Googlebot downloading your page. Indexing is Google analyzing that page and deciding whether to store it in its index. Serving (ranking) is choosing which indexed pages to show for a search.</p>
<p><strong>A page can be crawled and still not be indexed, and indexed and still not rank.</strong> Those are separate problems with separate fixes. Google also states plainly that indexing is not guaranteed: not every page it processes will be indexed, and low-quality content is one of the common reasons.</p>
<p>Say you run a plumbing business with 30 pages. If your "Boiler repair" page is indexed but sits on page five, that is a ranking question about content and competition. If it never appears for a search of its exact URL, that is an indexing question, and it is the one this guide covers.</p>

<h2>First, check whether you actually have a problem</h2>
<p>Not every URL on your site should be indexed. Redirected URLs, filtered category views, internal search results and duplicate versions of pages are supposed to stay out. A non-indexed count in the hundreds can be perfectly healthy if those URLs are all junk variants.</p>
<p>Google''s own <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/7440203">Page indexing report documentation</a> says that if your site has fewer than 500 pages, you probably do not need the report at all. It suggests starting with simple searches instead:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>site:example.com</code> to see a sample of what Google has indexed.</li>
<li><code>site:example.com/services/boiler-repair</code> to check one specific URL.</li>
</ul>
<p>What to do: write down the 10 to 20 pages that genuinely matter (home, services, products, top articles). <strong>Those pages are your indexing checklist.</strong> If they are indexed, most other "not indexed" entries can wait.</p>

<h2>Use the URL Inspection tool on your important pages</h2>
<p>For any important page that is missing, the <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/9012289">URL Inspection tool in Search Console</a> is the fastest way to a diagnosis. It shows whether the URL is indexed, which canonical Google selected, whether crawling and indexing are allowed, and when Google last crawled it.</p>
<p>Two features matter most:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Test live URL</strong> checks the current version of the page, so you can confirm a fix before asking Google to look again.</li>
<li><strong>Request indexing</strong> asks Google to crawl the URL. There is a daily limit per property, so save it for important pages after you have fixed something.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Page not indexed: what each status means and how to fix it</h2>
<p>Here are the statuses you are most likely to see on a small site, grouped by cause. The labels come from the Page indexing report.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Usual cause</th><th>First fix to try</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>URL blocked by robots.txt</td><td>A Disallow rule matches the URL</td><td>Remove or narrow the rule if the page should rank</td></tr>
<tr><td>URL marked ''noindex''</td><td>A meta robots tag or X-Robots-Tag header</td><td>Remove noindex if the page should rank</td></tr>
<tr><td>Alternate page with proper canonical tag</td><td>The page points to another URL as canonical</td><td>Usually nothing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Duplicate without user-selected canonical</td><td>Google found a near-identical page and picked the other one</td><td>Add a canonical, or make the pages genuinely different</td></tr>
<tr><td>Duplicate, Google chose different canonical than user</td><td>Your canonical conflicts with other signals</td><td>Align canonical, links, redirects and sitemap</td></tr>
<tr><td>Page with redirect</td><td>The URL redirects elsewhere</td><td>Usually nothing; update internal links</td></tr>
<tr><td>Not found (404), Soft 404, Server error (5xx)</td><td>Missing pages, empty pages, server trouble</td><td>Restore, redirect, or fix the server</td></tr>
<tr><td>Crawled - currently not indexed</td><td>Google fetched it and decided not to index it, for now</td><td>Improve the page and its internal links</td></tr>
<tr><td>Discovered - currently not indexed</td><td>Google knows the URL but has not crawled it yet</td><td>Strengthen internal links and sitemap signals</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>URL blocked by robots.txt</h3>
<p>Your robots.txt file tells Googlebot not to fetch the URL. This is the classic leftover from a staging site that launched with <code>Disallow: /</code> still in place. If the page should rank, find the matching rule and remove or narrow it. Our guide on <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/robots-txt-guide">writing safe robots.txt rules</a> covers the syntax and the common mistakes.</p>
<p>Watch for the related warning "Indexed, though blocked by robots.txt." Google can index a blocked URL without its content if other pages link to it, which usually means a poor search result with no description.</p>

<h3>URL marked ''noindex''</h3>
<p>The page carries a <code>noindex</code> directive, either in a meta robots tag or an <code>X-Robots-Tag</code> HTTP header. That is correct for thank-you pages and internal search results. It is a serious mistake on a service page, and it often comes from a CMS setting like "Discourage search engines" or a per-page SEO toggle left on.</p>
<p>Remove the directive, then use Test live URL to confirm it is gone. If you are unsure which pages deserve a noindex and which should be blocked instead, read <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/noindex-vs-disallow">when to use noindex versus disallow</a>.</p>

<h3>Duplicate and canonical statuses</h3>
<p>Three statuses relate to duplicates. "Alternate page with proper canonical tag" means your page correctly points to another URL as the main version, so there is nothing to do. "Duplicate without user-selected canonical" means Google found near-identical pages and chose one itself. "Duplicate, Google chose different canonical than user" means you declared a canonical but other signals disagreed.</p>
<p><strong>For the last two, check what Google selected in URL Inspection before changing anything.</strong> Sometimes Google''s choice is the better one. If it is not, align every signal: the canonical tag, internal links, redirects and the sitemap should all name the same URL. Learn <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/canonical-tags-explained">how canonical tags work</a> and how to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/duplicate-content-seo">fix duplicate content that splits signals</a>.</p>
<p>Say your shop sells a mug in four colors and each color has its own URL with identical text. Google will likely index one and mark the rest as duplicates. Either canonicalize the variants to one page, or give each variant enough unique content to stand on its own.</p>

<h3>Page with redirect, 404, soft 404 and 5xx</h3>
<p>"Page with redirect" is normal for old URLs that now point somewhere else. The fix is only needed if your own links or sitemap still use the old address. If you see "Redirect error" instead, you likely have a chain or loop; here is <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/301-vs-302-redirects">how to fix redirect chains</a>.</p>
<p>A 404 is fine for pages that are truly gone. A soft 404 means the page returns 200 but looks empty or like an error, often a thin category page or an out-of-stock product with no content. Server errors (5xx) mean Googlebot could not get the page at all; check with your host if these appear on pages that load fine for you.</p>

<h3>Crawled - currently not indexed</h3>
<p>Google fetched the page and chose not to index it, at least for now. Google says the page may or may not be indexed in the future, and that you do not need to resubmit it. <strong>On small sites this status most often points to pages that add little beyond what is already indexed.</strong></p>
<p>Look honestly at each affected page. Is it thin, a near copy of another page, or missing the detail a searcher would need? Say you have 12 location pages that differ only by town name. Merging them, or adding real local detail such as service areas, local projects and specific contact details, gives Google a reason to index each one.</p>

<h3>Discovered - currently not indexed</h3>
<p>Google knows the URL exists but has not crawled it yet. Google''s report documentation says the typical reason is that crawling was expected to overload the site, so it was rescheduled. On a small site, where server load is rarely the issue, it is still worth checking how the page is linked internally.</p>
<p>A page that appears only in the sitemap, with no internal links pointing to it, sends a weak signal about its importance. Find these <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/orphan-pages-internal-linking">orphan pages and link them</a> from relevant hub pages, and make sure your <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/xml-sitemap-guide">XML sitemap lists only indexable URLs</a> so it points Google at pages worth crawling.</p>

<h2>A repeatable process for fixing indexing problems</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>List your important pages.</strong> Ignore everything else at first.</li>
<li><strong>Inspect each one</strong> in the URL Inspection tool and note the status and the Google-selected canonical.</li>
<li><strong>Fix technical blocks first:</strong> robots.txt rules, noindex directives, redirects, 404s and server errors. These are the quickest wins.</li>
<li><strong>Fix duplicate signals next:</strong> canonical tags, internal links and sitemap entries should all agree.</li>
<li><strong>Improve quality and linking last:</strong> expand thin pages, merge near duplicates, and add internal links from related pages.</li>
<li><strong>Test live, then request indexing</strong> for the pages you changed.</li>
<li><strong>Validate the fix</strong> in the Page indexing report. Google says validation typically takes up to about two weeks, and sometimes longer.</li>
</ol>
<p>Steps 3 and 4 are easier with a full crawl of your own site, because Search Console only shows a sample of URLs per status. A free desktop crawler like <a href="https://www.scaryspiderseo.com">Scary Spider SEO</a> flags noindex and X-Robots-Tag directives, canonical problems, orphan pages, and non-indexable URLs in your sitemap across every page it crawls, so you can fix the causes in bulk.</p>

<h2>The bottom line</h2>
<p>A page not indexed by Google is a symptom, and the Page indexing report tells you which kind. Technical blocks like robots.txt rules and noindex are quick to fix. Duplicate and canonical statuses need consistent signals. "Crawled" and "Discovered" statuses usually come down to page quality and internal links.</p>
<p>Do not chase every non-indexed URL. Focus on the pages that should earn traffic, fix the cause, confirm it with a live test, and give Google time to recrawl.</p>
<p><strong>Your next step:</strong> list your ten most important URLs, run each through the URL Inspection tool today, and write down the status and Google-selected canonical next to each one.</p>', '["Crawling, indexing and ranking are separate steps, and Google does not guarantee that every crawled page will be indexed.", "Many non-indexed URLs, such as redirects and duplicates, are excluded on purpose and need no action.", "Start with a short list of important pages and inspect each one in the URL Inspection tool.", "Fix technical blocks like robots.txt rules and noindex first, then align canonical signals, then improve thin or poorly linked pages.", "After fixing, test the live URL, request indexing for key pages, and allow up to about two weeks for validation."]', '[{"question": "How long does it take Google to index a new page?", "answer": "There is no fixed timeframe. Some pages are indexed within days, others take weeks, and some are never indexed. Linking to the page from relevant pages on your site, listing it in your sitemap, and requesting indexing in the URL Inspection tool can help Google find it sooner."}, {"question": "Should I keep clicking Request Indexing until the page is indexed?", "answer": "No. Requesting indexing asks Google to crawl the page, but it does not force indexing, and there is a daily limit per property. Request it once after a real change, then work on the cause if the page still is not indexed."}, {"question": "Is Crawled - currently not indexed a penalty?", "answer": "No. It means Google fetched the page and decided not to index it for now. It is often a signal that the page adds little compared to other pages, so improving the content and its internal links is the usual fix."}, {"question": "Why does Search Console show hundreds of non-indexed URLs on my small site?", "answer": "Most are usually variants that should not be indexed, such as redirects, parameter URLs, tag archives or duplicates. Check whether your important pages are indexed first. If they are, the rest may be harmless."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'Technical SEO', 'Page Not Indexed by Google? Causes and Fixes', 'Page not indexed by Google? Learn crawl vs index, what each Search Console status means, and how to fix robots.txt, noindex, duplicate and quality issues.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = 'why-google-is-not-indexing-your-pages');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT 'canonical-tags-explained', 'Canonical Tags Explained: Fix Duplicate URLs the Right Way', 'What a canonical tag really does, why every page needs a self-referencing one, the duplicate URLs small sites create by accident, and the canonical mistakes to fix first.', '<p>Your shop has one product page, but a crawl finds it at five addresses: with and without <code>www</code>, with a tracking parameter from a newsletter, with a sort parameter, and with a trailing slash. To you it is one page. To a search engine it is five URLs with the same content, and unless a canonical tag says otherwise, it has to pick one on its own.</p>
<p>The <strong>canonical tag</strong> is how you tell Google which one you prefer. Used well, it quietly consolidates duplicates into a single URL. Used badly, it can point Google at a broken page, or get ignored entirely.</p>
<p>This guide covers what <code>rel="canonical"</code> actually does, why every page should have a self-referencing canonical, the URL duplicates small sites create without noticing, the canonical mistakes that cause real problems, and how to audit your canonicals in an afternoon.</p>

<h2>What a canonical tag does (and does not do)</h2>
<p>A canonical tag is a line in the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> of a page that names the preferred URL for that content:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;link rel="canonical" href="https://www.example.com/mugs/blue-mug" /&gt;</code></pre>
<p>When Google finds several pages with the same or very similar main content, it groups them and picks one as the canonical. That is the version it crawls most often and usually shows in results. The others become duplicates that are crawled less.</p>
<p><strong>Your canonical tag is a strong hint, not a command.</strong> Google''s overview of <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/canonicalization">what canonicalization is</a> states this directly: indicating a canonical preference is a hint, not a rule, and Google may choose a different page. It weighs your tag alongside redirects, sitemap inclusion, HTTPS versus HTTP and other signals.</p>
<p>That is why canonical problems rarely come from one tag in isolation. <strong>They come from signals that disagree.</strong> If your canonical says one URL, your internal links point to another, and your sitemap lists a third, Google has to guess.</p>
<p>Canonical selection also explains several statuses in Search Console''s indexing report, such as "Duplicate, Google chose different canonical than user." If pages are missing from Google entirely, start with the wider guide on <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/why-google-is-not-indexing-your-pages">diagnosing pages Google is not indexing</a>.</p>

<h2>Why every page needs a self-referencing canonical tag</h2>
<p>A self-referencing canonical is a canonical tag that points to the page''s own URL. It looks redundant, but it does real work.</p>
<p>Say your blue mug page lives at <code>https://www.example.com/mugs/blue-mug</code>. A newsletter link adds <code>?utm_source=newsletter</code>. Without a canonical, that tagged URL is a separate address with identical content. With a self-referencing canonical on the page, every variant that loads the same template automatically declares the clean URL as the preferred one.</p>
<p>Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/consolidate-duplicate-urls">guide to specifying a canonical URL</a> recommends adding the self-referential canonical to the canonical page itself, and using absolute URLs rather than relative paths. A relative canonical like <code>/mugs/blue-mug</code> works, but if a staging copy of your site is ever crawled, it can end up pointing at the staging host.</p>
<p>What to do:</p>
<ul>
<li>Give every indexable page a canonical tag pointing at its own clean, absolute URL.</li>
<li>Make sure the URL uses your chosen protocol and host, for example <code>https://www.</code>.</li>
<li>Strip tracking and session parameters from the canonical URL.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The duplicate URLs small sites create without noticing</h2>
<p>Most duplicates are not copied content. They are the same page reachable through slightly different URLs. The common ones:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Duplicate type</th><th>Example</th><th>Best fix</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Protocol</td><td><code>http://</code> and <code>https://</code></td><td>301 redirect to HTTPS, plus canonical</td></tr>
<tr><td>Host</td><td><code>example.com</code> and <code>www.example.com</code></td><td>301 redirect to one host, plus canonical</td></tr>
<tr><td>Trailing slash</td><td><code>/about</code> and <code>/about/</code></td><td>301 redirect to one form, plus canonical</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tracking parameters</td><td><code>?utm_source=...</code>, <code>?gclid=...</code></td><td>Self-referencing canonical to the clean URL</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sort and filter parameters</td><td><code>?sort=price</code>, <code>?color=blue</code></td><td>Canonical to the main category URL</td></tr>
<tr><td>Product variants</td><td>One URL per color with identical text</td><td>Canonical to the main product, or unique content per variant</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>For site-wide variants like protocol, host and trailing slash, use redirects first and canonicals second.</strong> Google lists redirects as the strongest canonicalization signal and canonical tags as a strong one, and the signals stack when they agree. A redirect means visitors and crawlers never see the duplicate. For variants that must keep working, such as filtered views, the canonical tag is the right tool.</p>
<p>When you set up those site-wide redirects, use permanent ones. A temporary redirect is not treated as a canonical signal; this guide on <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/301-vs-302-redirects">choosing between 301 and 302 redirects</a> explains why.</p>

<h2>Canonical mistakes that cause real problems</h2>
<p>A missing canonical is usually harmless, because Google will pick a version itself. A wrong canonical is worse, because you are actively pointing Google at the wrong place.</p>

<h3>Canonical pointing to a redirect or a 404</h3>
<p>The canonical URL should be the final page, returning a 200 status. If it points to a URL that redirects, you are saying "prefer URL A" while URL A says "go to URL B." If it points to a 404, you are naming a page that does not exist. Both are common after a redesign, when templates keep old URLs.</p>
<p>Fix: update the canonical to the final, live URL.</p>

<h3>Canonical pointing to a noindexed page</h3>
<p>If page A canonicalizes to page B, and page B has a <code>noindex</code> directive, the signals contradict each other: "index B instead of me" and "do not index B." Google''s guidance also says not to use noindex as a way to choose a canonical within a site, because it blocks the page from Search completely. Our comparison of <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/noindex-vs-disallow">noindex and robots.txt disallow</a> explains when noindex is the right choice.</p>
<p>Fix: canonicalize only to indexable pages.</p>

<h3>Multiple canonical tags on one page</h3>
<p>This happens when a theme and an SEO plugin each add a canonical, or when a script injects a second one. Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/javascript/javascript-seo-basics">JavaScript SEO basics</a> warn that conflicting or multiple canonical tags may lead to unexpected results.</p>
<p>Fix: view the page source, search for <code>rel="canonical"</code>, and remove every copy but one.</p>

<h3>JavaScript changing the canonical</h3>
<p>Some single-page apps and tag managers rewrite the canonical after the page loads. Google says you should not use JavaScript to change the canonical to something other than the URL in the original HTML. If you must set it with JavaScript, leave it out of the HTML entirely so there is only one value.</p>
<p>Fix: compare the canonical in the raw HTML with the one in the rendered page. They should match.</p>

<h3>Canonicalizing different pages to one URL</h3>
<p>A canonical is meant for duplicate or near-duplicate pages. Pointing page two of a blog archive, or a different product, at a single URL does not merge them in any useful way. Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/7440203">Page indexing report help</a> is blunt about it: a duplicate page must be similar to the canonical, and if the declared canonical is not similar, Google will not choose it.</p>
<p>Fix: if pages are genuinely different, give each a self-referencing canonical. If they overlap heavily, decide whether to merge, rewrite or canonicalize, as covered in this guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/duplicate-content-seo">handling duplicate content</a>.</p>

<h2>Keep your canonical signals consistent</h2>
<p>Canonical tags work best when everything else on the site agrees with them. Check these four signals for each important page:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Canonical tag:</strong> points to the clean, live, indexable URL.</li>
<li><strong>Internal links:</strong> your menus and body links use that same URL. Google recommends linking to the canonical URL rather than a duplicate.</li>
<li><strong>Sitemap:</strong> lists only canonical URLs. Google counts sitemap inclusion as a weak canonical signal, so listing duplicates muddies it. Here is <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/xml-sitemap-guide">what belongs in your sitemap</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Redirects:</strong> any old or variant URL redirects to that same canonical URL.</li>
</ol>
<p>Say your canonical points to <code>https://www.example.com/services/</code>, but your main menu links to <code>https://example.com/services</code> without the trailing slash. Even if a redirect cleans it up, Google sees mixed messages from your own site. Fix the menu link and all four signals line up.</p>

<h2>How to audit your canonical tags</h2>
<p>For a small site, a canonical audit takes a few steps:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Crawl the site</strong> and export each URL with its canonical tag and status code.</li>
<li><strong>Find pages with no canonical</strong> and add a self-referencing one, ideally through your template or SEO plugin.</li>
<li><strong>Find canonicals that point elsewhere</strong> and confirm each target is a 200, indexable page with similar content.</li>
<li><strong>Find pages with more than one canonical</strong> and remove the extras.</li>
<li><strong>Spot-check rendered pages</strong> if your site uses JavaScript heavily, to see whether scripts change the canonical.</li>
<li><strong>Compare with Google''s choice</strong> in the URL Inspection tool for your key pages. Look at the user-declared canonical and the Google-selected canonical.</li>
</ol>
<p>A free desktop crawler like <a href="https://www.scaryspiderseo.com">Scary Spider SEO</a> covers steps 1 to 5 in one pass: it flags missing and multiple canonicals, canonicals pointing to broken, redirecting or non-indexable pages, and, with optional rendering, JavaScript that changes the canonical.</p>
<p>If Google still picks a different canonical after you fix the technical signals, Google''s guide to <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/canonicalization-troubleshooting">fixing canonicalization issues</a> is the next stop. It notes that pages may stay in a duplicate cluster for up to two weeks after a content change, and that the fix usually comes down to making clustered pages sufficiently different.</p>

<h2>The bottom line</h2>
<p>A canonical tag tells Google which URL you prefer for a piece of content. It is a strong hint, not a guarantee, and it works best when redirects, internal links and your sitemap all name the same URL.</p>
<p>Give every page a self-referencing canonical, redirect site-wide variants, and remove canonicals that point at redirects, 404s, noindexed pages or unrelated content. Most canonical problems are signal conflicts you created yourself, which means you can fix them.</p>
<p><strong>Your next step:</strong> open the source of your five most important pages, search for <code>rel="canonical"</code>, and confirm each one appears exactly once and points to a live, absolute URL.</p>', '["A canonical tag is a strong hint about your preferred URL, and Google can still choose a different one.", "Every indexable page should have a self-referencing canonical tag that uses an absolute URL.", "Fix site-wide duplicates like HTTP, non-www and trailing slash variants with permanent redirects as well as canonicals.", "Canonicals should never point to a redirect, a 404, a noindexed page or an unrelated page.", "Canonical tags, internal links, sitemaps and redirects should all name the same URL."]', '[{"question": "Does every page need a canonical tag?", "answer": "It is not required, because Google will choose a canonical on its own if you do not specify one. A self-referencing canonical on every indexable page is still good practice, since it covers tracking parameters and other accidental URL variants automatically."}, {"question": "Can a canonical tag point to a different domain?", "answer": "Yes, cross-domain canonicals are supported, for example when the same article is published on two sites you control. The content must be essentially the same, and Google still treats the tag as a hint. For syndication to third-party sites, Google recommends that partners block indexing of the copy instead."}, {"question": "What is the difference between a canonical tag and a 301 redirect?", "answer": "A 301 redirect sends visitors and crawlers to the new URL, so the old version is no longer seen. A canonical tag leaves both URLs working and only tells search engines which one to prefer. Use redirects when the duplicate should disappear and canonicals when it needs to stay usable."}, {"question": "Why is Google ignoring my canonical tag?", "answer": "Usually because other signals disagree, such as internal links, redirects or the sitemap pointing to a different URL, or because the pages are not similar enough to be duplicates. Check the Google-selected canonical in the URL Inspection tool and align your signals before changing anything else."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'Technical SEO', 'Canonical Tag Guide: How rel=canonical Works', 'Learn how the canonical tag works, why self-referencing canonicals matter, and how to fix canonicals pointing to redirects, 404s or noindexed pages.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = 'canonical-tags-explained');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT 'noindex-vs-disallow', 'Noindex vs Disallow: Which One Keeps a Page Out of Google?', 'Disallow stops crawling, noindex stops indexing. Learn when to use each, why combining them backfires, and how to remove a page from Google results fast.', '<p>You added <code>Disallow: /thank-you/</code> to your robots.txt to keep your form confirmation page out of Google. A month later, a search for your brand shows that exact URL in the results, with a note that no description is available. You blocked it. So why is it there?</p>
<p>Because blocking crawling and blocking indexing are two different things. The <strong>noindex vs disallow</strong> choice decides whether Google can read the page, whether it can list the page, or both, and mixing them up is one of the most common technical SEO mistakes on small sites.</p>
<p>In this guide you will see a side-by-side comparison, why using both on the same page backfires, when to use a meta robots tag versus an <code>X-Robots-Tag</code> header, which pages actually deserve a noindex, and how to get a page out of Google quickly.</p>

<h2>Noindex vs disallow: the short version</h2>
<p><strong>Disallow controls crawling. Noindex controls indexing.</strong> A <code>Disallow</code> rule in robots.txt asks crawlers not to fetch a URL. A <code>noindex</code> directive, in a meta tag or HTTP header, tells search engines not to show a page they have fetched.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Disallow (robots.txt)</th><th>Noindex (meta tag or header)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>What it controls</td><td>Whether Googlebot fetches the URL</td><td>Whether the page can appear in results</td></tr>
<tr><td>Where it lives</td><td>One robots.txt file at the site root</td><td>Each page''s HTML head, or its HTTP response</td></tr>
<tr><td>Keeps the URL out of Google?</td><td>No. The URL can still be indexed if linked from elsewhere</td><td>Yes, once Google recrawls the page</td></tr>
<tr><td>Google must crawl the page?</td><td>No, that is the point</td><td>Yes, or it never sees the noindex</td></tr>
<tr><td>Works for PDFs and images?</td><td>Yes, blocks crawling of any URL</td><td>Yes, via the X-Robots-Tag header</td></tr>
<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Saving crawl effort on low-value URLs</td><td>Keeping specific pages out of search results</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/robots/intro">introduction to robots.txt</a> is explicit that it is not a mechanism for keeping a page out of Google. If other pages link to a disallowed URL, Google can still index the address, without visiting it, and show it with no description. The full syntax is covered in our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/robots-txt-guide">robots.txt rules and common mistakes</a>.</p>

<h2>Why disallow plus noindex backfires</h2>
<p>This is the trap. You want a page gone, so you add a noindex tag and, to be extra safe, disallow it in robots.txt as well. The result is the opposite of what you wanted.</p>
<p>Google''s documentation on <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/block-indexing">blocking indexing with noindex</a> explains why: for noindex to work, the page must not be blocked by robots.txt. <strong>If Googlebot is not allowed to fetch the page, it never sees the noindex tag.</strong> The URL can then stay in results, especially if other pages link to it.</p>
<p>Say you run a 50-page agency site and you have an old "Spring offer" landing page you want removed from Google. The right sequence is:</p>
<ol>
<li>Add <code>&lt;meta name="robots" content="noindex"&gt;</code> to the page.</li>
<li>Make sure robots.txt does <em>not</em> block it.</li>
<li>Wait for Google to recrawl it, or request a recrawl in the URL Inspection tool.</li>
<li>Once it has dropped out, you can leave the noindex in place, or delete the page and return a 404 or 410.</li>
</ol>
<p>If you add a disallow rule later, do it only after the page has left the index, and only if you have a real reason to stop crawling it.</p>
<p>One more detail: a <code>noindex</code> line inside robots.txt does nothing in Google. Google''s documentation states that specifying noindex in robots.txt is not supported.</p>

<h2>Meta robots tag vs X-Robots-Tag header</h2>
<p>There are two ways to send a noindex, and Google says they have the same effect. Pick the one that fits the content type.</p>

<h3>The meta robots tag (for HTML pages)</h3>
<p>Place this in the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> of the page:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;meta name="robots" content="noindex"&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Most CMSs and SEO plugins expose this as a per-page checkbox, such as "Hide from search engines." That is usually the easiest route for HTML pages. Use <code>name="googlebot"</code> instead of <code>robots</code> only if you want to target Google alone.</p>

<h3>The X-Robots-Tag header (for PDFs, images and other files)</h3>
<p>A PDF has no HTML head, so it cannot carry a meta tag. Instead, the server sends the directive as an HTTP header: <code>X-Robots-Tag: noindex</code>.</p>
<p>Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/robots-meta-tag">robots meta tag and X-Robots-Tag specification</a> shows how to apply it to every PDF on an Apache server through <code>.htaccess</code>, using a <code>&lt;Files&gt;</code> block that sets the header for files ending in <code>.pdf</code>, with an equivalent <code>add_header</code> rule for NGINX.</p>
<p>Say your site hosts price lists as PDFs that you would rather people reach through the HTML pricing page. A header rule for those files keeps them out of results without touching each document. Check the header is actually sent by running <code>curl -I</code> on one of the PDF URLs.</p>

<h2>Which pages to noindex (and which to leave alone)</h2>
<p><strong>Noindex is for pages that should exist for visitors but have no value as a search result.</strong> Typical candidates on a small site:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Thank-you and confirmation pages</strong> after a form or purchase.</li>
<li><strong>Internal search results pages</strong>, such as <code>/search?q=</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Account, cart and checkout pages</strong>, if your platform does not already handle them.</li>
<li><strong>Thin tag or author archives</strong> that list a couple of posts and duplicate your categories.</li>
<li><strong>Printer-friendly or filtered versions</strong> of pages, if a canonical tag is not a better fit.</li>
</ul>
<p>Pages to leave alone: anything you want to rank, and duplicate URLs that should pass their signals to a main version. For those duplicates, a canonical tag is usually better than noindex. Our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/canonical-tags-explained">choosing the right canonical URL</a> covers that case.</p>
<p>Also keep noindexed URLs out of your sitemap. A sitemap says "please index these pages," so listing a noindexed URL sends a contradictory signal. Here is how to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/xml-sitemap-guide">build a sitemap with only indexable URLs</a>.</p>

<h3>What about staging sites?</h3>
<p>A staging or development copy of your site should not be in Google at all. A robots.txt <code>Disallow: /</code> is the usual approach, but for the reasons above it does not guarantee the URLs stay out. <strong>Password protection is the safest option</strong>, and Google lists it alongside noindex as a reliable way to keep pages out of search.</p>
<p>Whatever you use on staging, remove it on launch day. A leftover <code>Disallow: /</code> or site-wide noindex on a live site is one of the fastest ways to lose search traffic, and it is a common reason a <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/why-google-is-not-indexing-your-pages">page is not indexed by Google</a>.</p>

<h2>How to remove a page from Google fast</h2>
<p>A noindex only works after Google recrawls the page, which, according to Google, can take months for less important pages. When you need something gone quickly, combine a temporary block with a permanent fix.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Request a temporary removal.</strong> The <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/9689846">Removals tool in Search Console</a> hides a URL from Google results for about six months. It does not remove the page from your site or stop Google crawling it.</li>
<li><strong>Make the removal permanent within that window.</strong> Delete the page and return a 404 or 410, put it behind a password, or add a noindex tag. Google''s help page notes noindex is less secure than the other two, and warns against using robots.txt for this.</li>
<li><strong>Confirm the result.</strong> Use the URL Inspection tool to check that Google sees the 404, 410 or noindex on the live page.</li>
</ol>
<p>Say a staff member''s personal phone number was published on a contact page. Remove it from the page first. Then use the Removals tool''s "Clear snippet in search" option for that URL, so the outdated snippet stops showing while Google recrawls the corrected page.</p>
<h2>How to check which rule a page is using</h2>
<p>Before you change anything, confirm what is actually in place. Directives can come from a theme, a plugin, a server rule or a CDN, and the settings screen does not always match what Google receives.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Meta robots:</strong> view the page source and search for <code>name="robots"</code>.</li>
<li><strong>X-Robots-Tag:</strong> run <code>curl -I</code> on the URL and look for the header in the response.</li>
<li><strong>Robots.txt:</strong> open <code>/robots.txt</code> on your domain and check whether any Disallow rule matches the path.</li>
<li><strong>Google''s view:</strong> in the URL Inspection tool, check whether crawling and indexing are allowed, then run a live test after any change.</li>
</ul>
<p>To catch these issues across a whole site, a free desktop crawler like <a href="https://www.scaryspiderseo.com">Scary Spider SEO</a> flags noindex and nofollow directives, including those sent through the X-Robots-Tag header, and lists non-indexable URLs that still appear in your sitemap.</p>

<h2>The bottom line</h2>
<p>The noindex vs disallow decision comes down to one question: do you want Google to stop reading the page, or stop showing it? Disallow stops crawling but can leave the URL in results. Noindex removes the page from results, but only if Google is allowed to crawl it and see the directive.</p>
<p>Never combine the two on a page you want removed. Use meta robots for HTML, the X-Robots-Tag header for PDFs and other files, password protection for staging, and the Removals tool when you need a page gone today.</p>
<p><strong>Your next step:</strong> open your robots.txt and list every Disallow rule, then check whether any of those paths also carry a noindex tag. If they do, decide which one you actually need and remove the other.</p>', '["Disallow in robots.txt controls crawling, while noindex controls whether a page can appear in search results.", "A disallowed URL can still be indexed without its content if other pages link to it.", "Combining disallow and noindex backfires because Google cannot crawl the page to see the noindex.", "Use a meta robots tag for HTML pages and the X-Robots-Tag HTTP header for PDFs and other files.", "For urgent removals, use the Search Console Removals tool, then make it permanent with a 404, 410, password or noindex."]', '[{"question": "Can I put noindex in my robots.txt file?", "answer": "No. Google does not support a noindex rule inside robots.txt. Use a meta robots tag in the page''s HTML or an X-Robots-Tag HTTP header instead, and make sure the URL is not disallowed so Google can see it."}, {"question": "How long does it take for a noindexed page to disappear from Google?", "answer": "It disappears after Google recrawls the page and sees the noindex. For important pages that can be quick, but Google says it may take months for less important pages. Requesting a recrawl in the URL Inspection tool can speed it up."}, {"question": "Does noindex also stop Google from following links on the page?", "answer": "Not on its own. The nofollow rule controls whether links are followed, and you can combine them as noindex, nofollow. In most cases a plain noindex is enough."}, {"question": "Should I noindex category and tag pages?", "answer": "Only if they are thin or duplicate other archives. Category pages that group useful content and receive internal links are often worth indexing. Look at whether each archive offers something a searcher would want before adding noindex."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'Technical SEO', 'Noindex vs Disallow: Which One to Use and When', 'Noindex vs disallow explained: why robots.txt does not keep pages out of Google, when to use meta robots or X-Robots-Tag, and how to remove a page fast.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = 'noindex-vs-disallow');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT 'duplicate-content-seo', 'Duplicate Content SEO: What Actually Hurts and How to Fix It', 'There is no duplicate content penalty, but duplicates still split signals and waste crawling. Learn the common types and the right fix for each one.', '<p>Say you crawl your site for the first time and the report lights up: dozens of pages flagged as duplicate content, a long list of duplicate title tags, and the same meta description repeated across half the blog. The natural reaction is to search for "duplicate content penalty" and assume the worst.</p><p>Here is the good news: Google does not punish ordinary sites for having duplicate pages. The less good news is that duplicates still cost you, just in quieter ways that are easy to miss.</p><p>This guide covers duplicate content SEO from the practical side. You will learn what Google actually does with duplicates, the types you are likely to find on a small site, how to spot them, and which fix (consolidate, canonicalize, rewrite, or noindex) suits each one.</p><h2>The duplicate content penalty is mostly a myth</h2><p>Google tackled this directly in a Search Central blog post called <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/blog/2008/09/demystifying-duplicate-content-penalty">Demystifying the duplicate content penalty</a>, and its current documentation makes the same point: it says that <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/canonicalization">some duplicate content on a site is normal</a> and is not a violation of its spam policies.</p><p><strong>The real exception is copying with intent to manipulate.</strong> Scraping other sites and republishing their content without adding anything is a spam problem, and Google handles it very differently. If you wrote your own pages and your CMS happens to serve them on several URLs, you are not in that category.</p><p>So when a tool flags duplicates, read it as "housekeeping needed", not "site in trouble".</p><h2>What duplicate content actually costs you</h2><p>When Google finds several pages with the same or very similar main content, it groups them into a cluster and picks one as the canonical: the version it indexes and usually shows in results. The other URLs are crawled less often. That process creates a few real problems.</p><ul><li><strong>Google may pick the wrong version.</strong> Your preference is a hint, not a rule. If Google settles on the URL with a tracking parameter or an old HTTP address, that is the one searchers see.</li><li><strong>Signals get split.</strong> If some sites link to <code>/cakes/</code> and others to <code>/cakes/?sort=price</code>, those links point at two URLs. Telling Google which URL you prefer, as explained in its <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/consolidate-duplicate-urls">guide to consolidating duplicate URLs</a>, lets those signals count toward one page.</li><li><strong>Crawling time goes to copies.</strong> Every duplicate URL Googlebot fetches is a fetch not spent on a new or updated page. On a 40-page site this barely matters. On a store with thousands of filter combinations it can slow down how quickly new products get found.</li><li><strong>Your reporting gets messy.</strong> When one piece of content lives at five URLs, its traffic can be split across five rows in your analytics, which makes it harder to judge what is working.</li></ul><p>Duplication is also one of the common reasons a page never makes it into the index at all. If that is the symptom you are chasing, start with <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/why-google-is-not-indexing-your-pages">why Google is not indexing your pages</a>, which walks through each status in Search Console.</p><h2>The types of duplicate content you will actually find</h2><h3>URL variations of the same page</h3><p>This is the most common type, and it is almost always a technical accident. The same page answers on more than one address:</p><ul><li><code>http://</code> and <code>https://</code></li><li>with and without <code>www</code></li><li>with and without a trailing slash</li><li>with tracking or sorting parameters such as <code>?utm_source=newsletter</code> or <code>?sort=price</code></li><li>printer-friendly versions or old mobile subdomains</li></ul><p>Example: say you run a bakery site with 40 pages. If both HTTP and HTTPS resolve, and both the www and non-www hosts work, every page exists at four URLs. That is 160 URLs for 40 pages of content.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> pick one protocol and one host, then send everything else there with a server-side 301. If you are unsure which status code to use, see <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/301-vs-302-redirects">when to use a 301 redirect</a>. For parameter URLs that need to keep working, use a canonical tag that points to the clean URL.</p><h3>Near duplicates</h3><p>Near duplicates share most of their text with a few words swapped. Typical examples are location pages where only the town name changes ("Plumber in Leeds", "Plumber in York"), a separate page for each color of the same product, and two blog posts that answer the same question from slightly different angles.</p><p><strong>A page that differs from its sibling by one word gives Google no reason to show both.</strong> Expect Google to cluster them and index only one, and it may not be the one you want.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> either make each page genuinely different (local details, team members, service area, prices, photos taken on site) or merge them into one stronger page.</p><h3>Duplicate title tags and meta descriptions</h3><p>These are not duplicate pages, but they send a duplicate signal. They usually come from templates: every post titled "Blog - Site Name", or every product inheriting one store-wide description. Google''s guidance asks you to <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/snippet">create unique descriptions for each page</a>. Identical titles cause the same problem: when several of your pages show up for one search, nobody can tell them apart.</p><p>Duplicate titles are also a useful diagnostic clue. If two URLs share an identical title, check whether they are actually the same page on two addresses.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> rewrite the titles and descriptions for your most important pages by hand, and change the template so new pages pull in something specific, such as the product name and its main attribute.</p><h3>Thin pages full of boilerplate</h3><p>Tag archives with one post, empty category pages, and auto-generated pages where the header, navigation, and footer make up most of the text all fall here. Google looks at the main content of each page when it groups duplicates, so pages whose main content is two lines can end up looking interchangeable.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> add real content to the ones that deserve to rank, and noindex or remove the rest.</p><h3>Content copied across domains</h3><p>Manufacturer product descriptions used by dozens of retailers, articles republished on partner sites, or your own content scraped by someone else. A shop using the stock description is not penalized, but it gives search engines no reason to prefer that page over every other copy.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> write your own descriptions for the products that matter, using details only you know: how it fits, what it is good for, what is in the box. If a partner republishes your article, Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/canonicalization-troubleshooting">canonicalization troubleshooting guide</a> says the most effective option is for the partner to block indexing of their copy, so ask for that, or at least for a credit and a link to your original.</p><h2>How to find duplicate content on your site</h2><ol><li><strong>Crawl the site.</strong> A crawler compares every page it finds and groups identical text, titles, and descriptions. A free desktop crawler like <a href="https://www.scaryspiderseo.com">Scary Spider SEO</a> flags duplicate and near-duplicate content, duplicate titles and meta descriptions, and canonical problems in one table.</li><li><strong>Check the Page indexing report in Search Console.</strong> Look for "Duplicate without user-selected canonical" and "Duplicate, Google chose different canonical than user". The first means Google found copies and you have not said which one you prefer. The second means you did, and Google disagreed.</li><li><strong>Inspect key URLs.</strong> The URL Inspection tool shows the canonical you declared next to the one Google selected. If they differ on an important page, find out why.</li><li><strong>Search a sentence in quotes.</strong> Copy a distinctive sentence from one of your pages and search for it in quotation marks to find copies on other sites.</li></ol><p>If you are working through a full site review, duplicates are one step of a wider <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/technical-seo-audit-checklist">technical SEO audit</a>, and it pays to fix redirects and canonicals in the same pass.</p><h2>Choosing the right fix for duplicate content SEO</h2><p>There are four main fixes. <strong>The right one depends on whether visitors need the duplicate URL to keep working.</strong></p><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Best fix</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Same page on several hosts or protocols (http/https, www/non-www)</td><td>301 redirect</td><td>Users and crawlers all land on one URL</td></tr><tr><td>Sort, filter, or tracking URLs that must keep working</td><td><code>rel="canonical"</code> to the clean URL</td><td>The page still works and signals point to one version</td></tr><tr><td>Two old posts covering the same topic</td><td>Merge into one, 301 the other</td><td>One strong page instead of two weak ones</td></tr><tr><td>Location or product pages with real differences</td><td>Rewrite each to be unique</td><td>Each page earns its own place in results</td></tr><tr><td>Pages useful to visitors but not to searchers (internal search results, print versions)</td><td><code>noindex</code></td><td>Keeps them out of the index entirely</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Canonical tags come with their own traps, such as pointing at a redirect or having two on one page, so read up on <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/canonical-tags-explained">how to use canonical tags correctly</a> before rolling them out across a template.</p><p>Noindex takes a page out of search completely, which is why Google advises against using it to choose between duplicates on your own site. It is the right tool for pages that should never appear in results; see <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/noindex-vs-disallow">noindex vs disallow</a> for when and how to apply it.</p><p><strong>Do not use robots.txt to handle duplicates.</strong> Google may still index a blocked URL without its content, and it cannot see a canonical tag on a page it is not allowed to crawl.</p><h2>A worked example: cleaning up a small online store</h2><p>Say you run a store with 120 products, each sold in three colors. Every color has its own URL, category pages add <code>?sort=</code> parameters, and the product copy came straight from the supplier. A crawl shows hundreds of near duplicates. Here is a sensible order of work:</p><ol><li><strong>Fix the hosts first.</strong> Confirm that HTTP and the non-preferred host redirect to your main version. This one change often removes the largest share of duplicate URLs.</li><li><strong>Choose one URL per product.</strong> Point the color variant URLs at the main product with a canonical tag, unless people search for a specific color and you can give that variant its own photos and copy.</li><li><strong>Canonicalize sorted category URLs</strong> to the unsorted category page.</li><li><strong>Rewrite supplier copy</strong> for your 20 best sellers first, then work down the list.</li><li><strong>Change the title template</strong> so each product title includes the product name and its key attribute instead of a generic store phrase.</li><li><strong>Clean the sitemap</strong> so it lists only the canonical URLs.</li><li><strong>Recrawl and watch Search Console</strong> over the following weeks to confirm Google is picking the URLs you chose.</li></ol><p>Notice the order: the cheap, site-wide fixes come first, and the slow manual rewriting is focused on the pages that bring in money.</p><h2>The bottom line</h2><p>Duplicate content SEO is less about avoiding a penalty and more about housekeeping. Google will not punish you because your CMS serves a page on four URLs, but it will choose a version for you, split your signals, and spend crawl time on copies.</p><p>Fix technical duplicates with redirects and canonicals, merge or rewrite the near duplicates that matter, and give every important page its own title and meta description. That covers most of what a small site will ever run into.</p><p><strong>Your next step:</strong> crawl your site, sort the results by duplicate title, and check the first ten groups to see whether each one is the same page on different URLs or different pages that need their own titles.</p>', '["Google has no generic penalty for duplicate content on your own site; deliberate scraping of other sites is a separate spam issue.", "Duplicates make Google choose a canonical for you, split link signals across URLs, and spend crawl time on copies.", "Most duplication on small sites comes from URL variations such as protocol, www, trailing slashes, and parameters.", "Use 301 redirects when the duplicate URL does not need to work, and canonical tags when it does.", "Write unique titles and meta descriptions for important pages and fix the template so new pages do not repeat the problem."]', '[{"question": "Does Google penalize duplicate content?", "answer": "Not in the way most people fear. Google says some duplicate content on a site is normal and not a spam policy violation. Penalties are reserved for deceptive behavior such as scraping and republishing other sites'' content to manipulate rankings."}, {"question": "How much duplicate content is acceptable on a website?", "answer": "There is no published percentage. Shared headers, footers, and short legal notices are normal on every site. The problem starts when a page''s main content is the same as, or nearly the same as, another URL, because Google will usually index only one of them."}, {"question": "Is it OK to use manufacturer product descriptions?", "answer": "You will not be penalized for it, but your page will be one of many with identical text, so it has little reason to rank above the others. Rewrite descriptions for your most important products with details only you can provide, such as fit, use cases, and what is included."}, {"question": "Should I noindex duplicate pages or use a canonical tag?", "answer": "Use a canonical tag or a redirect when the pages are versions of the same content, because that consolidates signals into one URL. Google advises against using noindex to pick a canonical within your own site. Reserve noindex for pages that should not appear in search at all."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'Technical SEO', 'Duplicate Content SEO: What Hurts and How to Fix It', 'Duplicate content SEO explained: why there is no penalty, how duplicates split signals and waste crawling, and which fix suits each type of duplicate.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = 'duplicate-content-seo');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT 'orphan-pages-internal-linking', 'Orphan Pages and Internal Linking: A Simple Plan That Works', 'Orphan pages have no internal links pointing to them, so visitors and crawlers miss them. Learn how to find them and an internal linking routine to stop new ones.', '<p>You spend a week on a detailed guide, publish it, and it shows up in your XML sitemap. A month later it has almost no impressions. You check your site and realize nothing links to it: not the menu, not the blog index past page one, not a single related post.</p><p>That guide is one of your orphan pages. It exists, search engines may even know about it, but your own site gives no sign that it matters.</p><p>This post explains what orphan pages are, how to find them by comparing a crawl with your sitemap, and how to fix them. It also covers the wider internal linking habits that stop new orphans appearing: click depth, weakly linked pages, hub and spoke linking, and anchor text.</p><h2>What orphan pages are and why they hurt</h2><p>An orphan page is a page on your site that no other page on your site links to. Visitors cannot reach it by clicking around, and a crawler that starts at your home page will never find it.</p><p>Google is clear that links matter here. Its link guidance says <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/links-crawlable">every page you care about should have a link</a> from at least one other page on your site. A sitemap helps, but Google''s own sitemap documentation notes that listing a URL <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/sitemaps/overview">does not guarantee it will be crawled and indexed</a>.</p><p><strong>Internal links do two jobs a sitemap cannot: they tell search engines how important a page is, and the anchor text tells them what it is about.</strong> An orphan gets neither. If a page you care about is not making it into search results at all, our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/why-google-is-not-indexing-your-pages">fixing pages Google is not indexing</a> walks through every likely cause, orphaning included.</p><h3>How pages become orphans</h3><ul><li>A page is removed from the main menu during a redesign, and nothing else pointed to it.</li><li>Campaign landing pages are built for ads or email and never linked from the site.</li><li>Old blog posts drop off the paginated blog index and are only reachable through page 14.</li><li>Links are built with JavaScript click handlers instead of real <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code> elements, so crawlers cannot follow them.</li><li>Pages are created in the CMS, published, and forgotten.</li></ul><h2>How to find orphan pages</h2><p>You cannot find orphan pages with a crawl alone, because a crawl only sees pages that are linked. <strong>The method is to compare a list of every URL you know exists against the list of URLs a crawl can reach.</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Build the "known URLs" list.</strong> Combine your XML sitemap, an export of published pages from your CMS, and the landing pages from your analytics or the Search Console Performance report.</li><li><strong>Crawl the site from the home page</strong>, following internal links only.</li><li><strong>Compare the two lists.</strong> Any URL that is known but was not reached by the crawl is an orphan candidate. In a spreadsheet, a simple <code>MATCH</code> or <code>XLOOKUP</code> between the two columns does the job.</li><li><strong>Check each candidate by hand.</strong> Some will be deliberate, such as a thank-you page after a form.</li></ol><p>Some crawlers do the comparison for you. A free desktop crawler like <a href="https://www.scaryspiderseo.com">Scary Spider SEO</a> can seed the crawl from your sitemap and flag orphan pages (URLs in the sitemap that have no internal links), along with deep pages and pages that have only one inlink.</p><p>While you are comparing lists, look at the opposite gap too. Indexable pages missing from the sitemap, and sitemap URLs that redirect or return errors, both point to a sitemap that needs work. Our guide on how to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/xml-sitemap-guide">audit your XML sitemap</a> walks through that cleanup.</p><h2>Decide what to do with each orphan page</h2><p>Not every orphan deserves to be rescued. Sort each one into a bucket:</p><ul><li><strong>Valuable and current:</strong> add internal links to it from relevant pages (the rest of this post covers how).</li><li><strong>Outdated, but has backlinks or traffic:</strong> merge it into a better page and 301 redirect the old URL. If you are unsure which redirect to use, read about <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/301-vs-302-redirects">choosing between 301 and 302 redirects</a>.</li><li><strong>Deliberately unlinked</strong> (thank-you pages, ad landing pages): leave it unlinked, consider noindex, and take it out of the sitemap.</li><li><strong>No value at all:</strong> delete it, let it return 404 or 410, and remove it from the sitemap.</li></ul><p>Example: say you run a bakery site with 40 pages and find six orphans. Two are seasonal cake pages from past years, one is a catering menu the menu redesign dropped, and three are form confirmation pages. The catering menu gets linked from the home page and the contact page. The seasonal pages get merged into one evergreen "seasonal cakes" page. The confirmation pages stay as they are.</p><h2>Click depth: how far a page is from your home page</h2><p>Click depth is the number of clicks it takes to reach a page from the home page. The home page is depth 0, pages in the main menu are depth 1, and so on.</p><p>Google does not publish a depth limit. Still, a page buried six clicks deep usually has fewer internal links pointing to it and is harder for both people and crawlers to reach. <strong>A practical rule is to keep your important pages within about three clicks of the home page.</strong></p><p>The usual cause of deep pages is pagination. If your blog index shows ten posts per page, a post from two years ago may only be reachable at <code>/blog/page/15/</code>, which can leave it more than ten clicks from the home page.</p><p>Ways to bring pages closer to the surface:</p><ul><li>Add category or topic pages that list related posts, and link them from the menu.</li><li>Link to important older posts from newer ones in the body text.</li><li>Build hub pages that link to every post on a topic (more on this below).</li><li>Add a short "related posts" block, curated by hand where it matters.</li></ul><h2>Pages with only one internal link</h2><p>A page with a single inlink is one edit away from becoming an orphan. If that link lives on a page you later delete or rewrite, the page drops off the map.</p><p>Search Console''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/9049606?hl=en">Links report</a> shows which of your pages are the most linked from within your own site. Sort the other way in your crawl data and you will see the weakly linked ones. Product pages, service pages, and cornerstone guides should never be in that bottom group.</p><p><strong>Action:</strong> for every page that brings in leads or sales, aim for links from several relevant pages, not just one.</p><h2>Use hub and spoke linking to organize topics</h2><p>Hub and spoke linking (often called a topic cluster) is the simplest internal linking strategy that scales. You write one broad hub page on a topic and several focused spoke pages on subtopics. The hub links to every spoke, every spoke links back to the hub, and spokes link to each other where it helps the reader.</p><p>This blog uses the same pattern. Our <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/technical-seo-audit-checklist">technical SEO audit checklist</a> is the hub for the technical cluster, and each step links out to a deeper guide on that step.</p><p>Example for the bakery: a hub page on wedding cakes links to spokes on pricing, flavors, delivery, and tasting appointments. Each spoke links back to the hub and to the pricing page. A new visitor can move through the topic in any order, and a crawler finds every page within two clicks of the hub.</p><p>If you are newer to how links fit into SEO as a whole, our <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/dont-let-your-website-turn-invisible-a-beginner-guide">beginner''s guide to how search works</a> covers the basics first.</p><h2>Anchor text best practices for internal links</h2><p>Anchor text is the clickable text of a link. Google''s guidance is that good anchor text is descriptive, reasonably concise, and relevant to the page it points to. A useful test: read the anchor on its own, out of context. If you cannot tell what the linked page is about, rewrite it.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Weak anchor</th><th>Better anchor</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>click here</td><td>see our wedding cake prices</td></tr><tr><td>read more</td><td>how to store a fruit cake</td></tr><tr><td>this page</td><td>book a tasting appointment</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Two cautions. First, do not cram keywords into every anchor; natural variation reads better and keyword stuffing is against Google''s spam policies. Second, use real <code>&lt;a href="..."&gt;</code> links. Google can only reliably follow links that are anchor elements with an <code>href</code> attribute.</p><h2>A repeatable process for every new page</h2><p>Orphan pages are far easier to prevent than to find later. Run this short routine every time you publish:</p><ol><li><strong>Pick the hub.</strong> Decide which topic hub the new page belongs to, and add a link to it from the hub.</li><li><strong>Link out from the new page</strong> to the hub and to two or three closely related pages, using descriptive anchors.</li><li><strong>Link in from older pages.</strong> Search your own site for the topic (for example, <code>site:yourdomain.com wedding cake</code>) and add a contextual link from two or three relevant existing pages.</li><li><strong>Check the sitemap</strong> includes the new URL, which most CMS plugins handle automatically.</li><li><strong>Recrawl monthly</strong> and review orphan pages, deep pages, and pages with a single inlink.</li></ol><p>Step 3 is the one most people skip, and it is the one that stops orphan pages from appearing in the first place.</p><h2>The bottom line</h2><p>Orphan pages are pages your own site forgot about. They are easy to find once you compare your sitemap against a crawl, and most can be fixed in minutes by adding a few relevant internal links, merging them, or removing them.</p><p>The lasting fix is a habit: organize content into hubs and spokes, keep important pages within a few clicks of the home page, write anchors that describe the destination, and link new posts from old ones on the day you publish.</p><p><strong>Your next step:</strong> export your sitemap URLs, crawl your site from the home page, and list every URL that appears in the sitemap but not in the crawl. That list is your orphan pages to fix this week.</p>', '["An orphan page has no internal links pointing to it, so visitors and crawlers that follow links cannot reach it.", "Find orphan pages by comparing a list of known URLs, such as your sitemap and CMS export, against the URLs a crawl can reach.", "A sitemap helps discovery but does not replace internal links, which signal importance and context.", "Keep important pages within about three clicks of the home page and give key pages links from several relevant pages.", "Prevent new orphans by linking every new page to its hub and adding links to it from two or three older pages on publish day."]', '[{"question": "Are orphan pages bad for SEO?", "answer": "They are a missed opportunity rather than a penalty. Without internal links, search engines get no signal that the page matters and no anchor text describing it, and visitors cannot find it. Pages you care about should be linked from at least one other page on your site."}, {"question": "Can Google find a page that is only in the sitemap?", "answer": "Yes, Google can discover URLs from a sitemap. However, Google says a sitemap does not guarantee a page will be crawled and indexed, and a page with no internal links looks unimportant compared with well-linked pages."}, {"question": "How many internal links should a page have?", "answer": "There is no ideal number. Google says there is no magical count of links per page. Focus on linking each important page from several relevant pages, and keep links where they genuinely help the reader."}, {"question": "What is a good click depth for SEO?", "answer": "Google does not set a limit, but a common practical rule is to keep important pages within about three clicks of the home page. Pages that sit much deeper usually have fewer internal links and are slower for people and crawlers to reach."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'Technical SEO', 'Orphan Pages: How to Find and Fix Them With Internal Links', 'Find orphan pages by comparing your sitemap with a crawl, then fix them with a simple internal linking plan: click depth, topic hubs, and better anchors.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = 'orphan-pages-internal-linking');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT 'on-page-seo-checklist', 'On-Page SEO Checklist: 12 Things to Check Before You Publish', 'A practical on-page SEO checklist: 12 quick checks covering intent, titles, meta descriptions, headings, links, images, schema, and trust before you publish.', '<p>Most on-page SEO problems are not hard to fix. They are just easy to forget. A page goes live with the CMS default title, a stock meta description, a hero image called <code>IMG_4471.jpg</code>, and no links to or from the rest of the site. Nobody notices until the page fails to get traffic.</p><p>An on-page SEO checklist solves that by turning the important checks into a routine you run before you hit publish. None of the items take long, and together they cover what you control on the page itself.</p><p>Below are 12 checks in the order you would naturally work through them, from planning to final review. Each one explains what to look for, gives an example, and tells you exactly what to do. Where a topic needs more depth, there is a link to a dedicated guide.</p><h2>How to use this on-page SEO checklist</h2><p>Run it on every new page, and on old pages when you update them. Copy the 12 items into your CMS notes, a project board, or a printed sheet next to your desk.</p><p><strong>Do not treat it as a scoring system.</strong> Google''s own SEO Starter Guide is blunt about several popular myths: there is <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/seo-starter-guide">no magical word count target</a>, heading order does not matter to Google (it matters to screen reader users), and E-E-A-T is not a ranking factor in itself. The checklist exists to make pages clear and useful, not to tick boxes for an algorithm.</p><p>If you are new to SEO and want the bigger picture first, start with our <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/dont-let-your-website-turn-invisible-a-beginner-guide">beginner''s guide to SEO</a>, then come back here.</p><h2>Before you write</h2><h3>1. Match the search intent</h3><p>Search intent is what the searcher actually wants when they type a query. Look at the current top results for your main keyword before you write anything. Are they guides, product pages, comparison lists, or local listings?</p><p>Example: if everyone ranking for "sourdough starter" has a step-by-step guide, a product page selling starter kits will struggle for that query, however well optimized it is.</p><p><strong>Action:</strong> write one sentence describing what the searcher needs, and check your page delivers it in the first screen.</p><h2>In the search result</h2><h3>2. Write a specific title tag</h3><p>The title tag is the most likely source of the clickable headline in search results. Put the main topic near the start, keep it roughly 30 to 60 characters so it is less likely to be cut off, and make it different from every other title on your site.</p><p>Example: "Home" tells a searcher nothing. "Wedding Cakes in Bristol: Prices and Flavors" tells them exactly what they will get.</p><p><strong>Action:</strong> read the title on its own and ask whether a stranger would know what the page is about. Our guide on <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/how-to-write-title-tags">writing title tags that get clicked</a> covers length, branding, and why Google sometimes rewrites them.</p><h3>3. Write a meta description that earns the click</h3><p>The meta description does not affect rankings, but Google sometimes shows it as the snippet under your title. A good one summarizes the page, includes the main keyword naturally, and gives a reason to click.</p><p>Example: "Compare our three wedding cake sizes, see prices, and book a free tasting in central Bristol." That is specific and unique to one page.</p><p><strong>Action:</strong> aim for about 70 to 155 characters and never reuse a description across pages. See our <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/meta-descriptions-that-get-clicks">meta description examples and formula</a> for page-by-page templates.</p><h3>4. Keep the URL slug short and readable</h3><p>Google recommends <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/url-structure">readable words separated by hyphens</a> in URLs rather than long ID numbers or underscores. Short slugs are also easier to share and less likely to need changing later.</p><p>Example: <code>/wedding-cakes/</code> beats <code>/index.php?page_id=4471</code> and <code>/our-amazing-handmade-wedding-cakes-for-2025/</code>.</p><p><strong>Action:</strong> set the slug before publishing, leave out dates unless the content is truly time-bound, and avoid changing it afterward. If you must change it, redirect the old URL.</p><h2>On the page</h2><h3>5. Use one clear H1 and a logical heading structure</h3><p>The H1 is the main visible title of the page. Below it, H2s mark the main sections and H3s sit inside them. This outline helps readers skim, helps screen reader users jump between sections, and makes the page''s topic obvious.</p><p>Example: a common CMS mistake is a theme that wraps the logo in an H1, so every page has the same H1 and the real title is demoted to an H2.</p><p><strong>Action:</strong> check the page has one H1 that matches its topic and headings that do not skip levels. Our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/heading-tags-seo">structuring headings for SEO and readers</a> shows a full outline example.</p><h3>6. Answer the question in the introduction</h3><p>Readers decide within a few lines whether to stay. An intro that restates the problem and then says what the page delivers keeps them reading. An intro that warms up for four paragraphs loses them.</p><p>Example: a page on "how long to bake a sourdough loaf" should give the time range in the first few sentences, then explain what changes it.</p><p><strong>Action:</strong> make sure the main keyword and the main answer appear in the first paragraph or two.</p><h3>7. Add internal links in both directions</h3><p>Internal links help visitors find related pages and help search engines understand how your content fits together. A new page with no links to it is easy for both to miss.</p><p>Example: a new post on cake tasting appointments should link to the wedding cakes page, and the wedding cakes page should link back to it.</p><p><strong>Action:</strong> link out to two or three relevant pages with descriptive anchor text, then add a link to the new page from at least two existing pages.</p><h3>8. Optimize images and write alt text</h3><p>Images need descriptive file names, alt text that explains what the image shows, sensible file sizes, and width and height attributes so the layout does not jump while loading.</p><p>Example: <code>alt="three-tier lemon wedding cake with fresh flowers"</code> is useful. <code>alt="cake cake wedding cake bristol"</code> is keyword stuffing, and an empty alt is correct only for purely decorative images.</p><p><strong>Action:</strong> rename files before upload, compress them, and write alt text for every meaningful image. Our <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/image-seo-alt-text">image SEO and alt text guide</a> covers formats, compression, and lazy loading.</p><h3>9. Add structured data where it fits</h3><p>Structured data is code (usually JSON-LD) that describes the page to search engines in a standard format, such as Article, Product, or LocalBusiness. It can make a page eligible for rich results, though it never guarantees them.</p><p>Example: a bakery''s location page can describe its address, opening hours, and phone number with LocalBusiness markup.</p><p><strong>Action:</strong> only mark up information that is visible on the page; Google''s guidelines say not to <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/sd-policies">mark up content that is not visible</a> to readers. Start with our <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/structured-data-for-beginners">beginner''s guide to schema markup</a>, then validate with the Rich Results Test.</p><h2>Quality and trust</h2><h3>10. Cover the topic properly</h3><p>Depth is not word count. It means answering the follow-up questions a reader will have, with specifics they cannot get from the first three results.</p><p>Example: a page on wedding cake pricing that lists actual price ranges, what drives cost, and how far ahead to order is more useful than a longer page of general advice.</p><p><strong>Action:</strong> list the questions a customer has asked you about this topic and check each one is answered. If you drafted with AI, read our steps to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/ai-seo/5-steps-to-humanize-ai-content-save-seo">make AI drafts sound human</a> before publishing.</p><h3>11. Show who wrote it and why they know</h3><p>Google''s guidance on helpful content suggests asking "Who, How, and Why" about every page, and it <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/creating-helpful-content">encourages accurate bylines</a> where readers would expect them. These signals help readers decide whether to trust you.</p><p>Example: a recipe page with the baker''s name, a short bio, and photos of their own bakes is more convincing than an anonymous page with stock images.</p><p><strong>Action:</strong> add an author name linked to a real bio, include first-hand details only you could provide, and cite sources for any facts or figures.</p><h3>12. Keep it fresh, honestly</h3><p>Updating content keeps it accurate. Changing the date without changing the content does not help, and Google''s helpful content guidance lists it as a warning sign.</p><p>Example: a pricing page updated with this year''s prices deserves a new "last updated" date. A post where you fixed one typo does not.</p><p><strong>Action:</strong> set a review reminder for pages with prices, statistics, or yearly details, and update the date only when the content meaningfully changes.</p><h2>Check the page after it goes live</h2><p>A checklist catches most problems before launch, but templates and plugins can still undo your work. A quick crawl after publishing confirms what search engines actually see.</p><p>A free desktop crawler like <a href="https://www.scaryspiderseo.com">Scary Spider SEO</a> flags missing or duplicate titles and meta descriptions, H1 problems, missing alt text, missing structured data, and low word count across the whole site in one table, which makes it easy to spot pages that slipped through.</p><h2>The bottom line</h2><p>An on-page SEO checklist is less about tricks and more about consistency. Twelve quick checks, run every time, catch the forgotten titles, missing alt text, and unlinked pages that quietly hold sites back.</p><p>Start with intent and finish with trust. If the page answers the searcher''s question clearly, is easy to scan, and shows who stands behind it, the technical details mostly fall into place.</p><p><strong>Your next step:</strong> pick your most important page, run it through all 12 checks today, and note which items fail. Those failures are your template fixes for every page that follows.</p>', '["Start every page by checking the search intent behind the main keyword and making sure the page delivers it early.", "Give each page a specific, unique title tag and meta description, and a short, readable, hyphenated URL slug.", "Use one clear H1, logical headings, descriptive alt text, and internal links both to and from the new page.", "Add structured data only for information that is visible on the page, and validate it before publishing.", "Show who wrote the page, cite sources, and update dates only when the content has meaningfully changed."]', '[{"question": "What is on-page SEO?", "answer": "On-page SEO covers everything you control on the page itself: the content, title tag, meta description, headings, URL, images, internal links, and structured data. It differs from technical SEO, which deals with crawling and indexing, and off-page SEO, which deals with links and mentions from other sites."}, {"question": "How long should a page be for SEO?", "answer": "There is no ideal length. Google states that content length alone does not matter for ranking and that there is no magic word count. Write enough to fully answer the searcher''s question and stop there."}, {"question": "Do I still need meta keywords?", "answer": "No. Google Search does not use the keywords meta tag, so filling it in has no effect on Google rankings. Spend that time on the title tag and meta description instead."}, {"question": "How often should I review on-page SEO on old pages?", "answer": "Review pages whenever their information changes, and set a regular reminder for pages with prices, statistics, or yearly details. Run the same checklist you use for new pages, and only change the published date when the content has meaningfully changed."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'On-page SEO', 'On-Page SEO Checklist: 12 Checks Before You Publish', 'Use this on-page SEO checklist before you publish: 12 practical checks for intent, titles, meta descriptions, headings, links, images, schema, and trust.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = 'on-page-seo-checklist');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT 'how-to-write-title-tags', 'How to Write Title Tags That Rank and Get Clicked', 'How to write title tags that rank and earn clicks: length in characters and pixels, keyword placement, branding, why Google rewrites titles, and real examples.', '<p>Search for your own business and look at the blue headline Google shows for your home page. For many small sites it says something like "Home" or just the company name. That headline is usually your title tag, and it is often the first thing a potential customer reads about you.</p><p>The title tag is one of the smallest pieces of SEO work and one of the most visible. A few words decide whether a searcher understands what your page offers and whether they click it or the result below.</p><p>In this guide you will learn what a title tag does, how long it should be (and why pixels matter more than characters), where to put keywords and your brand, why Google sometimes rewrites titles, and how titles differ from H1s. It ends with before and after examples for common page types.</p><h2>What a title tag is and why it matters</h2><p>The title tag is the <code>&lt;title&gt;</code> element in the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> of a page. As <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/title">MDN''s reference for the title element</a> explains, it defines the document''s title, which browsers show in the title bar or the page''s tab.</p><pre><code>&lt;title&gt;Wedding Cakes in Bristol: Prices and Flavors - Rosie''s Bakery&lt;/title&gt;</code></pre><p>Google uses it as the main source for the clickable headline in search results, which it calls the title link. Google''s own documentation says the title link is <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/title-link">often the primary piece of information people use</a> to decide which result to click.</p><p><strong>A title has two jobs: tell search engines what the page is about, and persuade a person to click.</strong> The rest of this guide is about doing both at once. For where the title fits among all the other pre-publish checks, see our <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/on-page-seo-checklist">on-page SEO checklist</a>.</p><h2>Title tag length: characters vs pixels</h2><p>Google does not set a maximum. Its documentation says there is no limit on title length, but the title link is truncated in results as needed, typically to fit the device width.</p><p><strong>Truncation happens by pixel width, not by character count.</strong> Letters like W and M take far more space than i and l, so two titles of the same character length can be cut off in different places. On desktop, titles start getting cut at roughly 560 to 600 pixels. That figure comes from observation, not from Google, and it varies by device.</p><p>A practical rule that works for most pages:</p><ul><li><strong>Aim for about 30 to 60 characters.</strong> Under 30 usually wastes space you could use to describe the page. Over 60 risks a truncated title with an ellipsis.</li><li><strong>Put the important words first</strong>, so even a truncated title makes sense.</li><li><strong>Watch out for wide letters and capitals.</strong> A title written in all caps uses more pixels per character and gets cut off sooner.</li></ul><p>Example: "Emergency Plumber in Leeds: 24/7 Callouts and Fixed Prices" is 58 characters and front-loads the service and location. If it is truncated, the searcher still knows what you do and where.</p><h2>How to write title tags: keyword first, then the promise</h2><p>Good titles follow a simple pattern: the main topic first, then what makes this page worth clicking, then the brand if there is room.</p><ol><li><strong>Front-load the main keyword.</strong> Put the phrase people search for at or near the start. Searchers scan the first few words of each result.</li><li><strong>Add a specific benefit or detail.</strong> A price range, a location, a number of steps, a year if the content is truly time-bound.</li><li><strong>Write for a person.</strong> The title should read like a sentence fragment a human wrote, not a list of keywords.</li><li><strong>Make every title unique.</strong> Each page on your site should have its own title that describes that page.</li></ol><p><strong>Avoid keyword stuffing.</strong> Google''s guidance specifically calls out titles like "Foobar, foo bar, foobars, foo bars": repeating variations does not help users and can make your result look spammy.</p><p>Duplicate titles are one of the most common problems a site crawl turns up, usually because a template applies the same title to every page in a section. If you find groups of identical titles, our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/duplicate-content-seo">fixing duplicate titles and content</a> explains how to tell whether they are separate pages or the same page on several URLs.</p><h2>Where to put your brand name</h2><p>Google suggests branding titles concisely: include your site name at the beginning or end of each title, separated by a delimiter such as a hyphen, colon, or pipe. On the home page you can say a little more about the business.</p><p>For most small businesses, the brand works best at the end of inner pages. Searchers looking for "wedding cakes Bristol" care about the cakes first. On the home page, or for a well-known brand, putting it first is reasonable.</p><p>Keep the brand short. "Rosie''s Bakery" is fine. "Rosie''s Bakery and Cake Studio, Family Run Since 1998" eats most of your space on every single page. Google may also drop the site name from the title link when it repeats the site name already shown in the result.</p><h2>Why Google rewrites title tags (and how to reduce it)</h2><p>Google generates title links automatically and does not always use your title tag. When it rolled out its newer title system in 2021, Google said the HTML title tag was still <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/blog/2021/08/update-to-generating-page-titles">used more than 80% of the time</a>, but it also draws on the main visible heading, other prominent text, and anchor text of links pointing to the page.</p><p>Google''s documentation lists the most common reasons it replaces a title:</p><ul><li><strong>Half-empty titles</strong>, such as <code>| Site Name</code> with the page name missing.</li><li><strong>Obsolete titles</strong>, for example a page that says 2024 in the title but 2025 in the visible heading.</li><li><strong>Inaccurate titles</strong> that do not describe what is actually on the page.</li><li><strong>Boilerplate titles</strong> repeated across a set of pages, such as every season page of a TV show sharing one title.</li><li><strong>No clear main title</strong>, where several headings carry the same visual weight.</li><li><strong>Language mismatch</strong> between the title and the page content.</li></ul><p><strong>The best way to reduce rewrites is to make your title tag and your main visible heading agree.</strong> Write an accurate, specific title, keep it current, and make sure the page''s main heading clearly says the same thing. Remember that changes are not instant: Google has to recrawl and reprocess the page first.</p><h2>Title tag vs H1: what is the difference?</h2><p>The title tag lives in the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> and appears in search results and browser tabs. The H1 is the main heading visitors see on the page itself.</p><p>They should describe the same topic, but they do not have to be identical. The title is written to win a click in a list of results, often with the brand added. The H1 is written for someone who has already arrived.</p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Title tag</th><th>H1</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Where it appears</td><td>Search results, browser tab</td><td>On the page</td></tr><tr><td>Audience</td><td>Someone choosing a result</td><td>Someone already reading</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>Wedding Cakes in Bristol: Prices and Flavors - Rosie''s Bakery</td><td>Our Wedding Cakes</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Because Google looks at the main visible heading when it builds title links, a vague H1 like "Welcome" next to a specific title sends mixed signals. For more on the on-page side, see our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/heading-tags-seo">structuring H1 and H2 headings</a>.</p><h2>Before and after title tag examples</h2><p>Here are rewrites for common page types. All are hypothetical, but each shows the same pattern: topic first, specific detail second, short brand last.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Page type</th><th>Before</th><th>After</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Home page</td><td>Home</td><td>Rosie''s Bakery: Custom Cakes and Fresh Bread in Bristol</td></tr><tr><td>Service page</td><td>Services - Smith Plumbing</td><td>Boiler Repair in Leeds: Same-Day Visits - Smith Plumbing</td></tr><tr><td>Product page</td><td>Product 4471</td><td>Waterproof Hiking Jacket for Women, Navy - TrailCo</td></tr><tr><td>Category page</td><td>Shop</td><td>Women''s Hiking Jackets: Waterproof and Insulated - TrailCo</td></tr><tr><td>Blog post</td><td>Some Thoughts on Sourdough</td><td>How to Feed a Sourdough Starter (Simple Daily Routine)</td></tr><tr><td>Contact page</td><td>Contact Us</td><td>Contact Rosie''s Bakery: Orders, Hours and Directions</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Your title and meta description appear together in results, so write them as a pair. Our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/meta-descriptions-that-get-clicks">meta descriptions that get clicks</a> shows how to write the snippet that sits underneath.</p><h2>How to audit the title tags you already have</h2><ol><li><strong>Collect every title.</strong> Crawl your site and export the URL and title of every page.</li><li><strong>Sort by length.</strong> Fix empty titles first, then titles under 30 or over 60 characters.</li><li><strong>Sort alphabetically to find duplicates.</strong> Identical titles sit next to each other.</li><li><strong>Check your top pages in search.</strong> In the Search Console Performance report, find pages with plenty of impressions but few clicks. A weak title is a common cause.</li><li><strong>Rewrite in priority order:</strong> pages that bring in leads or sales first, then high-impression pages, then everything else.</li></ol><p>A free desktop crawler like <a href="https://www.scaryspiderseo.com">Scary Spider SEO</a> flags missing, duplicate, too long, and too short titles (using both character count and pixel width), which turns steps 1 to 3 into a single sortable table.</p><h2>The bottom line</h2><p>A good title tag is specific, unique, front-loaded with the topic, and short enough to survive truncation. Google may still rewrite it, but it uses your title most of the time, and accurate titles that match the page''s main heading get rewritten far less.</p><p>Think of each title as the headline of a tiny advert in the search results. If it clearly says what the page offers and why it is worth a click, you have done the job.</p><p><strong>Your next step:</strong> open Search Console, find your five pages with the most impressions and the lowest click-through rate, and rewrite their titles using the topic, detail, brand pattern from this guide.</p>', '["The title tag is the main source of the clickable headline Google shows in search results.", "Google sets no maximum title length but truncates by pixel width, so aim for about 30 to 60 characters with the topic first.", "Put the main keyword near the start, add a specific detail, and keep any brand name short and at the end on inner pages.", "Google rewrites titles that are half-empty, outdated, inaccurate, boilerplate, or unclear, so keep the title accurate and in line with the main heading.", "The title tag and H1 should describe the same topic but can be worded differently for their different audiences."]', '[{"question": "How long should a title tag be?", "answer": "Google has no official limit, but it truncates titles to fit the device width. Around 30 to 60 characters is a reliable range for most pages. Put the most important words first so the title still makes sense if it is cut off."}, {"question": "Why is Google showing a different title for my page?", "answer": "Google generates title links automatically and may replace your title tag if it is missing, outdated, inaccurate, repeated across many pages, or does not match the main heading. Fixing those issues and making the title match the visible heading reduces how often it happens."}, {"question": "Should the title tag and H1 be the same?", "answer": "They should cover the same topic, but they do not need to be identical. The title is written to win a click in search results and often includes your brand, while the H1 welcomes someone already on the page."}, {"question": "Should I put my brand name in the title tag?", "answer": "Usually yes, but keep it short. Google suggests adding the site name at the start or end of the title with a delimiter. For most small business inner pages, placing it at the end keeps the topic in front."}, {"question": "How long does it take for a new title tag to show in Google?", "answer": "Google has to recrawl and reprocess the page before it notices the change, which Google says can take from a few days to a few weeks. You can request recrawling of an important page with the URL Inspection tool in Search Console."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'On-page SEO', 'How to Write Title Tags: Length, Format, and Examples', 'Learn how to write a title tag that ranks and gets clicks: ideal length in characters and pixels, keyword and brand placement, and why Google rewrites.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = 'how-to-write-title-tags');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT 'meta-descriptions-that-get-clicks', 'Meta Descriptions That Get Clicks: Length, Examples, Mistakes', 'How to write a meta description that earns the click: realistic length guidance, why Google rewrites snippets, examples by page type, and fixes at scale.', '<p>Search for your own business and look closely at the two lines of grey text under your result. On a lot of small sites, that text is a cookie notice, a footer address, or the first line of a navigation menu. On others, every page shows the exact same sentence.</p>
<p>That text is the snippet, and the main thing you control in it is the meta description. It will not move you up the rankings, but it can be the deciding factor in whether someone clicks your result or the one below it.</p>
<p>This guide covers what a meta description actually does, how long it should be, why Google sometimes ignores yours, a simple formula for writing one, examples for different page types, and how to fix missing or duplicate descriptions across a whole site. It is one item in a wider <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/on-page-seo-checklist">pre-publish on-page SEO checklist</a>, and it is worth getting right on every page that matters.</p>

<h2>What a meta description is (and what it is not)</h2>
<p>A meta description is an HTML tag in the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> of a page. Visitors never see it on the page itself. It looks like this:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;meta name="description" content="Custom birthday and wedding cakes baked to order in Leeds. Gluten-free options, free local delivery, order online up to 48 hours ahead."&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Google may use that text as the snippet under your title in search results. <strong>The meta description is a sales pitch for the click, not a ranking signal.</strong> Google has said directly that <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/blog/2009/09/google-does-not-use-keywords-meta-tag">it does not use the description meta tag for ranking</a>, even though it sometimes shows it as the snippet.</p>
<p>So the job is simple: convince the right searcher that this page has what they want. The title and the description work as a pair, so write them together. If you have not tightened up your titles yet, start with <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/how-to-write-title-tags">writing title tags that get clicked</a>, then write the description to add what the title could not fit.</p>
<p>What to do:</p>
<ul>
<li>Treat the description as ad copy for one specific page, not a summary of your whole business.</li>
<li>Do not stuff it with keywords hoping to rank. It will not help rankings and it makes the snippet less readable.</li>
<li>Read your title and description out loud together. If they repeat each other word for word, rewrite the description.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Meta description length: how long should it be?</h2>
<p>There is no official character limit. Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/snippet">snippet documentation</a> says there is no limit on how long a meta description can be, but the snippet is truncated as needed, typically to fit the device width.</p>
<p>In practice, a range of about 70 to 155 characters works well. Under 70 characters you are usually leaving useful space empty and saying too little. Over about 155, the end of your text is likely to be cut off with an ellipsis on many screens. <strong>Because truncation depends on display width, not an exact character count, put the most important information in the first sentence.</strong></p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Length</th><th>What usually happens</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Missing</td><td>Google builds a snippet from page text, which may be a menu, notice, or random paragraph</td><td>Write one for every important page</td></tr>
<tr><td>Under 70 characters</td><td>Shows in full but often says too little to win the click</td><td>Add a specific detail: price, location, format, or benefit</td></tr>
<tr><td>About 70 to 155 characters</td><td>Usually shows in full on most devices</td><td>Aim here, with the key point first</td></tr>
<tr><td>Over 155 characters</td><td>Often truncated mid-sentence</td><td>Cut filler or move the essential part to the start</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Say you sell handmade leather wallets. "Handmade wallets." is 17 characters and tells the searcher almost nothing. "Handmade full-grain leather wallets, stitched by hand in small batches. Free UK delivery and a 2-year guarantee on every wallet." is around 130 characters and answers the questions a buyer actually has.</p>

<h2>Why Google sometimes shows different text</h2>
<p>Writing a great description does not guarantee Google will show it. According to Google, snippets are created primarily from the page content, and the meta description is used when it describes the page better than other parts of the content. The snippet is also chosen per search, so the same page can show different text for different queries.</p>
<p>Take a hypothetical bakery page about custom cakes. Its description talks about birthday and wedding cakes. Someone searches for "gluten free celebration cake". Google may skip the description and show the paragraph on the page that mentions gluten-free options, because that matches the search better.</p>
<p>That is not a failure. <strong>Google is more likely to replace your description when it is vague, stuffed with keywords, duplicated across many pages, or out of date.</strong> Common triggers:</p>
<ul>
<li>A list of keywords instead of a sentence ("cakes, birthday cakes, wedding cakes, Leeds cakes").</li>
<li>The same description used on dozens of pages.</li>
<li>A description that does not match the page, for example one that still mentions a sale that ended months ago.</li>
<li>A description so short that it says nothing a searcher could use.</li>
</ul>
<p>Google also supports snippet controls such as the <code>nosnippet</code> and <code>max-snippet</code> robots rules and the <code>data-nosnippet</code> attribute. These limit or block snippets. They do not force Google to use your meta description, so most small sites do not need them.</p>

<h2>A simple formula for writing a meta description</h2>
<p>You do not need to be a copywriter. Most good descriptions answer three questions in one or two sentences:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>What is on this page?</strong> Match what the searcher is looking for, using the words they would use.</li>
<li><strong>What makes it worth clicking?</strong> One specific detail: price range, location, number of options, turnaround time, a free tool, or who it is for.</li>
<li><strong>What can they do next?</strong> Book, compare, download, order, or learn, stated plainly.</li>
</ol>
<p>A few writing rules make a big difference:</p>
<ul>
<li>Use the page''s main topic or keyword naturally, once. It confirms to the searcher that they are in the right place.</li>
<li>Be specific. "Fast delivery" is forgettable. "Delivered in 2 working days" is useful, as long as it is true.</li>
<li>Write in an active voice and talk to the reader.</li>
<li>Never promise anything the page does not deliver. A click that bounces straight back helps no one.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Meta description examples by page type</h2>
<p>All the businesses below are hypothetical. Use them as patterns, not as copy to paste.</p>
<h3>Home page</h3>
<p><em>Weak:</em> "Welcome to Smith Plumbing. We are a plumbing company. Call us today!"</p>
<p><em>Better:</em> "Family-run plumbers covering Bristol and Bath. Boiler repairs, leaks and bathroom installs, with upfront fixed prices and same-day callouts on weekdays."</p>
<p>The better version names the area, the main services, and two reasons to choose this business.</p>
<h3>Product page</h3>
<p><em>Weak:</em> "Buy now! Best prices on the best products. Shop our amazing range today."</p>
<p><em>Better:</em> "Waterproof 30L hiking backpack with a padded laptop sleeve and rain cover. Weighs 1.1 kg. Free returns within 30 days."</p>
<p>Google''s snippet guidance points out that a good description can bring together key facts scattered across a product page, such as price or manufacturer. Concrete details like size, weight, or materials are exactly what a shopper scans for. Prices and ratings can also appear in results through markup, which is covered in our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/structured-data-for-beginners">adding structured data to your pages</a>.</p>
<h3>Blog post</h3>
<p><em>Weak:</em> "In this post we talk about composting and everything you need to know about it."</p>
<p><em>Better:</em> "Start a compost bin in a small garden: what to add, what to leave out, and how to fix a smelly or slow heap in a few weeks."</p>
<p>The better version tells the reader exactly which problems the post solves.</p>
<h3>Local service page</h3>
<p><em>Weak:</em> "Dog grooming services. Contact us for more information."</p>
<p><em>Better:</em> "Dog grooming in Northampton for all breeds: bath, cut and nail trim from £35. Online booking with evening and Saturday slots available."</p>
<p>Location, price, and availability are the three things a local searcher compares, so lead with them.</p>

<h2>Common meta description mistakes</h2>
<p>Most problems fall into a handful of patterns. Check your key pages for each:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Missing descriptions.</strong> Common on blog archives, tag pages, and pages created years ago before anyone thought about SEO.</li>
<li><strong>Duplicates.</strong> Themes and plugins often reuse the site tagline on every page. Google''s documentation is clear that identical or similar descriptions on every page are not helpful. Duplicate descriptions often travel with duplicate titles, so it is worth reading about <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/duplicate-content-seo">what duplicate content actually hurts</a> while you fix them.</li>
<li><strong>Template leftovers.</strong> Default text like "Just another WordPress site" or a placeholder from a page builder.</li>
<li><strong>Keyword lists.</strong> Comma-separated terms instead of a sentence.</li>
<li><strong>Stale details.</strong> Old prices, last year''s date, discontinued products, or an offer that has ended.</li>
<li><strong>Out-of-range length.</strong> A five-word description or a 300-character paragraph.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to fix missing or duplicate meta descriptions at scale</h2>
<p>Rewriting descriptions one page at a time is fine for a 10-page site. For anything bigger, you need a list first and a priority order second.</p>
<h3>1. Get a complete list</h3>
<p>Crawl the site so you see every page, not just the ones you remember. A free desktop crawler like <a href="https://www.scaryspiderseo.com">Scary Spider SEO</a> flags pages with missing or duplicate meta descriptions, and ones outside 70 to 155 characters, and you can export the results to CSV to work through in a spreadsheet.</p>
<h3>2. Prioritize by impressions</h3>
<p>Not every page deserves the same effort. In Search Console, the <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/7576553">Performance report</a> shows impressions, clicks, and click-through rate for each page. Start with your home page and the pages that get the most impressions. A page with many impressions and a low click-through rate is the best candidate for a rewrite, because the snippet is clearly being seen.</p>
<h3>3. Use templates for large, similar page sets</h3>
<p>For shops or directories with hundreds of similar pages, hand-writing every description is not realistic. Google''s documentation supports generating descriptions programmatically from page data, as long as the result is readable and specific to each page. A hypothetical product template might look like this:</p>
<p><code>[Product name] in [colour], [one key spec]. [Price]. Free returns within 30 days.</code></p>
<p>Make sure every field is filled for every page. A template that outputs "in , . ." when data is missing is worse than no description at all.</p>
<h3>4. Re-check after Google recrawls</h3>
<p>Changes only show once Google has recrawled the page, which can take days or weeks. Compare click-through rate for the edited pages over the same length of time before and after the change, and keep in mind that seasonality and ranking changes also affect it.</p>

<h2>The bottom line</h2>
<p>A meta description is not a ranking factor, but it shapes the first impression of your page in search results. Write a unique, specific description of roughly 70 to 155 characters for every important page, lead with the key point, and keep it true to what is on the page.</p>
<p>Google will sometimes show other text instead, and that is normal. What you can control is giving it a description good enough to use, and making sure no important page is missing one or sharing one with a dozen others.</p>
<p><strong>Your next step:</strong> crawl your site, export every page with a missing or duplicate meta description, and rewrite the ten with the most impressions in Search Console this week.</p>', '["A meta description does not affect rankings, but it can influence whether searchers click your result.", "There is no official length limit, and about 70 to 155 characters with the key point first works well on most screens.", "Google often shows other page text when a description is vague, duplicated, stuffed with keywords, or does not match the search.", "A good description says what the page offers, gives one specific reason to click, and states the next step.", "Fix descriptions at scale by crawling for missing and duplicate ones, then prioritizing pages with the most impressions."]', '[{"question": "Do meta descriptions affect SEO rankings?", "answer": "No. Google has said it does not use the meta description for ranking. It can still affect how many people click your result, which is why it is worth writing well."}, {"question": "Why is Google not showing my meta description?", "answer": "Google picks the snippet for each search and primarily uses page content. If your description is vague, duplicated, outdated, or does not match what the person searched for, Google may show a more relevant passage from the page instead."}, {"question": "How many characters should a meta description be?", "answer": "Google sets no official limit, but snippets are truncated to fit the screen. Around 70 to 155 characters is a practical target, with the most important information in the first sentence."}, {"question": "Is it bad to have the same meta description on every page?", "answer": "It is not a penalty, but it wastes the snippet. Google''s documentation says identical or similar descriptions are not helpful, and Google is more likely to ignore them and generate its own text."}, {"question": "Should every page have a meta description?", "answer": "Every page you want people to find in search should have one, starting with your home page and most visited pages. For very large sites, generating descriptions from page data is acceptable if the result is readable and specific."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'On-page SEO', 'Meta Description Guide: Length, Examples, Mistakes', 'Write a meta description that earns clicks: ideal length, why Google shows other text, real examples by page type, and how to fix missing or duplicates.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = 'meta-descriptions-that-get-clicks');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT 'heading-tags-seo', 'Heading Tags for SEO: How to Structure H1, H2 and H3', 'How heading tags help SEO, readers and screen readers: one clear H1, H2 and H3 nesting without skipped levels, question-style headings and common theme mistakes.', '<p>A page can look perfectly organized in the browser and still be a mess underneath. Run a crawl on a typical small business site and you will often find the logo marked up as an H1 on every page, two or three competing H1s on the home page, a jump from H2 straight to H4, and "headings" that are really just bold paragraphs.</p>
<p>None of that is visible to a sighted visitor scrolling the page. It is very visible to someone using a screen reader, and it makes it harder for search engines to tell what the main topic of the page is.</p>
<p>This guide explains how heading tags affect SEO and readers, how to use one clear H1, how to nest H2 and H3 headings logically, how to write headings that answer real questions, and how to spot the heading mistakes that themes and page builders introduce. Headings are one item on a full <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/on-page-seo-checklist">on-page SEO checklist</a>, and one of the quickest to fix.</p>

<h2>What heading tags do for SEO, readers, and screen readers</h2>
<p>HTML has six heading levels, <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> to <code>&lt;h6&gt;</code>. They are not font sizes. They describe the outline of the page: the main topic, the sections under it, and the subsections under those.</p>
<p>That outline helps three audiences:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Readers who skim.</strong> Many people scan headings first to decide whether the page has what they need.</li>
<li><strong>Screen reader users.</strong> A common way to explore a page with a screen reader is to jump from heading to heading, as <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/Heading_Elements">MDN''s heading element reference</a> describes. Missing or out-of-order headings make that much harder.</li>
<li><strong>Search engines.</strong> Google lists heading elements, such as the H1, among the sources it uses to <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/title-link">generate title links in search results</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p>It is worth being honest about the SEO side. Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/seo-starter-guide">SEO Starter Guide</a> says that from a Google Search perspective it does not matter if headings are out of order, and that there is no ideal number of headings. <strong>Good heading tags help SEO mostly by making the page clear: clear to the reader, clear about its main topic, and easy to scan.</strong> They are not a ranking trick, and stuffing keywords into them will not help.</p>

<h2>Use one clear H1 per page</h2>
<p>The H1 is the visible main title of the page. It should say, in plain words, what the page is about.</p>
<p>HTML technically allows more than one H1, but MDN recommends using only one per page, and it is the most predictable choice for both screen readers and search engines. Google''s title link documentation also warns that when a page has several large, equally prominent headings, it can be unclear which one is the main title.</p>
<p><strong>Give every page exactly one H1 that names its specific topic, and make sure it is the first heading in the main content.</strong></p>
<p>The H1 and the title tag usually cover the same topic, but they do different jobs. The title tag is written for the search result, where space is short and the brand may be added. The H1 is written for the person already on the page. If you want the details on that difference, see our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/how-to-write-title-tags">title tags and how they differ from H1s</a>.</p>
<p>Example for a hypothetical dog grooming business:</p>
<ul>
<li>Title tag: "Dog Grooming in Northampton: Prices and Booking"</li>
<li>H1: "Dog Grooming in Northampton"</li>
</ul>
<p>What to do:</p>
<ul>
<li>Check that every template (home, page, post, product, category) outputs exactly one H1.</li>
<li>Make sure the H1 is the page''s own topic, not the site name repeated on every page.</li>
<li>Keep it short enough to read at a glance.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Build a logical heading structure (no skipped levels)</h2>
<p>Under the H1, use H2s for the main sections and H3s for subsections inside an H2. Go deeper only if the content genuinely has that many levels, which most blog posts and service pages do not.</p>
<p>The W3C''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/page-structure/headings/">headings tutorial</a> recommends nesting headings by rank and avoiding skipped levels, such as an H2 followed directly by an H4. Going back up is fine: an H2 that starts a new section can follow an H4 that ended the previous one.</p>
<p>Here is a heading outline for a hypothetical service page:</p>
<pre><code>H1  Boiler Repair in Bristol
  H2  Common boiler problems we fix
    H3  No heating or hot water
    H3  Low boiler pressure
    H3  Strange noises from the boiler
  H2  How much does a boiler repair cost?
  H2  How booking works
    H3  Same-day callouts
    H3  What to check before we arrive
  H2  Areas we cover</code></pre>
<p>Read only the headings and you know exactly what the page covers. <strong>That is the test: if the headings alone make sense as a table of contents, the structure is right.</strong></p>
<p>What to do:</p>
<ul>
<li>Pick the heading level by position in the outline, never by how big you want the text to look.</li>
<li>If a heading level looks too large or small, change the style with CSS. MDN specifically advises against using heading elements to resize text.</li>
<li>Keep sidebar and footer headings consistent across pages rather than renumbering them to fit each article.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Write headings that answer real questions</h2>
<p>A heading like "Overview", "Details", or "More info" tells a skimmer nothing. A heading that states the point of the section, or asks the question it answers, does much more work.</p>
<p>Compare these for a hypothetical bakery''s custom cake page:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Vague heading</th><th>Descriptive heading</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Pricing</td><td>How much does a custom cake cost?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Options</td><td>Gluten-free and vegan cake options</td></tr>
<tr><td>Info</td><td>How far in advance should you order?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Delivery</td><td>Delivery areas and collection times</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The descriptive versions use the words people actually type into search, and they let a reader jump straight to the part they care about. <strong>Write each heading so that it would still make sense if it were the only line someone read.</strong></p>
<p>What to do:</p>
<ul>
<li>Use question-style headings where the section genuinely answers a question people ask.</li>
<li>Answer the question in the first sentence or two under the heading, then add detail.</li>
<li>Use your main topic naturally in a heading or two. Do not repeat the same keyword in every heading.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Common CMS and theme heading mistakes</h2>
<p>Most heading problems are not written by the author. They come from the theme, a page builder, or a plugin, which means they repeat on every page. These are the ones to look for:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The logo or site name as the H1.</strong> Some themes wrap the logo in an H1 on every page, so each page''s real title becomes a second H1 or gets pushed down to an H2.</li>
<li><strong>Post titles as H2 on single posts.</strong> The blog index shows titles as H2s, which is fine, but some themes keep the same markup on the individual post page, leaving it without its own H1.</li>
<li><strong>Styled paragraphs instead of headings.</strong> Text made big and bold with the formatting toolbar looks like a heading but has no heading markup, so screen readers and crawlers do not treat it as one.</li>
<li><strong>Headings used for styling.</strong> A short promotional line set as an H2 because the author liked the large font.</li>
<li><strong>Widget and footer titles before the content.</strong> A sidebar that outputs "Recent Posts" and "Categories" as H2s ahead of the main content in the HTML.</li>
<li><strong>Empty headings.</strong> Leftover blocks from a page builder that contain no text at all.</li>
<li><strong>Image-only headings.</strong> When an H1 contains only a logo or banner image, its alt text becomes the heading text. Missing alt text leaves an empty heading, so it is worth knowing how to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/image-seo-alt-text">write alt text for images</a> properly.</li>
</ul>
<p>Say you run a 40-page site on a theme that puts the logo in an H1. That is 40 pages with a duplicate, meaningless H1, all caused by one line in the header template. Fixing the template fixes every page at once.</p>

<h2>How to audit heading tags across your site</h2>
<p>You can check one page quickly in the browser. Right-click the page, choose Inspect, and search the HTML for <code>&lt;h1</code>, <code>&lt;h2</code> and so on. That works for spot checks, but not for a whole site.</p>
<p>For every page at once, crawl the site. A free desktop crawler like <a href="https://www.scaryspiderseo.com">Scary Spider SEO</a> flags H1 issues, missing or duplicate H2s, and non-sequential headings for each page, with a short explanation of the fix. Heading checks fit naturally into a broader <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/technical-seo-audit-checklist">technical SEO audit</a>, so it is worth running them together.</p>
<p>Then work through the results in this order:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Template problems first.</strong> If the same issue appears on every page of one type, fix the theme or template, not the individual pages.</li>
<li><strong>Missing H1s.</strong> Every indexable page should have one that names its topic.</li>
<li><strong>Multiple H1s.</strong> Decide which is the real title and change the others to H2 or plain text.</li>
<li><strong>Skipped levels.</strong> Usually a sign that someone picked a heading level for its size. Change the level and adjust the styling in CSS.</li>
<li><strong>Vague headings on key pages.</strong> Rewrite them on your most visited pages so they describe the section.</li>
</ol>

<h2>The bottom line</h2>
<p>Heading tags for SEO are less about rankings and more about clarity. One clear H1, logically nested H2s and H3s, and descriptive wording make a page easier to skim, easier to use with a screen reader, and easier for search engines to understand.</p>
<p>The biggest problems usually live in templates, not in individual posts. Find them with a crawl, fix them once in the theme, and then write good headings into your publishing routine.</p>
<p><strong>Your next step:</strong> open your three most important pages, read only the headings from top to bottom, and fix any page where they do not work as a clear table of contents.</p>', '["Headings describe the outline of a page and help skimmers, screen reader users and search engines understand it.", "Give every page exactly one H1 that names its specific topic, not the site name or logo.", "Nest H2s and H3s by their place in the outline and avoid skipping levels, such as an H2 followed by an H4.", "Descriptive or question-style headings help readers find answers and naturally use the words people search for.", "Most heading problems come from themes and templates, so audit with a crawl and fix the template once."]', '[{"question": "Is it bad to have multiple H1 tags on a page?", "answer": "HTML allows it and Google can handle it, but one H1 per page is the clearer choice. MDN recommends a single H1, and several equally prominent headings can make it unclear which one is the main title."}, {"question": "Should the H1 be the same as the title tag?", "answer": "They should cover the same topic but do not need to match word for word. The title tag is written for the search result, while the H1 is written for the visitor already on the page."}, {"question": "Do heading tags help Google rankings?", "answer": "Not as a direct trick. Google says the order and number of headings do not matter for Search, but clear headings help Google and readers understand the page, and the H1 is one of the sources Google uses for title links."}, {"question": "Can I skip from H2 to H4?", "answer": "Avoid it. Skipped levels can confuse screen reader users who move through the page by heading. If an H3 looks too large, change its size with CSS instead of picking a lower level."}, {"question": "How do I check the headings on my page?", "answer": "For a single page, inspect the HTML in your browser or use a heading outline browser extension. For a whole site, run a crawl that reports missing, multiple and non-sequential headings for every page."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'On-page SEO', 'Heading Tags for SEO: H1, H2 and H3 Structure Guide', 'Learn how heading tags affect SEO and readers: one clear H1, H2 and H3 nesting without skipped levels, better headings, and fixes for common CMS mistakes.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = 'heading-tags-seo');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT 'image-seo-alt-text', 'Image SEO: Alt Text, File Size, and Dimensions Done Right', 'A practical image SEO guide: how to write useful alt text, name files, pick WebP or AVIF, compress and resize, set width and height, and lazy-load the right images.', '<p>Images are often the heaviest thing on a page and the easiest thing to get wrong. A typical example: a product photo uploaded straight from a phone at 4,000 pixels wide and several megabytes, a file name like <code>IMG_4021.jpg</code>, no alt text, and no width or height, so the text jumps down the screen as the image loads.</p>
<p>Each of those is a small problem. Together they make the page slower, harder to use with a screen reader, and harder for search engines to understand.</p>
<p>This image SEO guide walks through what to fix and in which order: writing useful alt text, naming files, choosing formats, compressing and resizing, setting dimensions, lazy loading, and when an image sitemap is worth it. It fits into the images step of a broader <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/on-page-seo-checklist">on-page SEO checklist</a> you can run before publishing.</p>

<h2>Why image SEO matters beyond Google Images</h2>
<p>Image SEO is not only about ranking in Google Images, although that can bring useful traffic for products, recipes, and local businesses. It affects three things on every page:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Understanding.</strong> Google uses alt text, file names, captions, and the text around an image to work out what the image shows and how it relates to the page.</li>
<li><strong>Accessibility.</strong> Screen readers read alt text aloud. Without it, a blind visitor may hear a file name or nothing at all.</li>
<li><strong>Speed and stability.</strong> Oversized images slow the page down, and images without dimensions make the layout shift as they load.</li>
</ul>
<p>One technical point matters before anything else. Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/google-images">image SEO best practices</a> state that Google finds images in the <code>src</code> of an <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> element and does not index CSS background images. <strong>If an image matters for search, put it in an <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> tag, not a CSS background.</strong></p>

<h2>How to write useful alt text</h2>
<p>Alt text is the <code>alt</code> attribute on an image. It should describe what the image shows in the context of the page, in a short phrase or sentence.</p>
<p>The question to ask is: if the image did not load, what would the reader need to know? The answer depends on why the image is there.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Image</th><th>Weak alt text</th><th>Better alt text</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Product photo</td><td><code>alt="backpack"</code></td><td><code>alt="Green 30L hiking backpack with front zip pocket and side bottle holders"</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Team photo on an About page</td><td><code>alt="image of people"</code></td><td><code>alt="The five-person bakery team standing outside the shop on opening day"</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Chart in a report</td><td><code>alt="chart"</code></td><td><code>alt="Bar chart showing online orders doubled between March and June"</code> (with the data also in the text)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Linked logo in the header</td><td><code>alt="logo"</code></td><td><code>alt="Smith Plumbing home"</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Decorative divider or background flourish</td><td><code>alt="decorative swirl"</code></td><td><code>alt=""</code></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>That last row matters. The W3C''s guidance on <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/images/decorative/">decorative images</a> is to give them an empty alt attribute (<code>alt=""</code>) so screen readers skip them. Leaving the attribute out entirely is different, and often worse, because some screen readers will then read the file name.</p>
<p>Images inside headings need extra care. If your H1 is a logo or banner image, its alt text effectively becomes the heading text, which ties into how you <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/heading-tags-seo">structure headings on the page</a>.</p>
<p>What to do:</p>
<ul>
<li>Describe the content and purpose of the image, not just its category.</li>
<li>Skip "image of" or "picture of". Screen readers already announce that it is an image.</li>
<li>Use a keyword only if it naturally describes the image. Google''s documentation gives keyword-stuffed alt text as a bad example and warns it can make a site look spammy.</li>
<li>For a linked image, describe where the link goes.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Use descriptive file names</h2>
<p>File names are a small signal, but a free one. Google''s documentation says file names can give it very light clues about the subject of an image, and gives the example that <code>my-new-black-kitten.jpg</code> is better than <code>IMG00023.JPG</code>.</p>
<p>Rename images before you upload them, because many CMSs bake the file name into the URL. Use lowercase words separated by hyphens, keep it short, and describe the image: <code>green-30l-hiking-backpack.jpg</code>, not <code>final-v2-NEW.jpg</code>.</p>

<h2>Choose the right image format</h2>
<p>Google Search supports BMP, GIF, JPEG, PNG, WebP, SVG, and AVIF images. Within that list, the right choice depends on what the image is:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Photos:</strong> WebP or AVIF usually give much smaller files than JPEG at similar visual quality. JPEG is the safe fallback.</li>
<li><strong>Logos and icons:</strong> SVG, because it stays sharp at any size and is usually tiny.</li>
<li><strong>Screenshots and graphics with text:</strong> PNG or lossless WebP, which keep text edges crisp.</li>
<li><strong>Animations:</strong> animated WebP or a short video rather than a large GIF.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you want to serve AVIF or WebP with a fallback for older browsers, the <code>&lt;picture&gt;</code> element handles it. The browser picks the first format it supports:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;picture&gt;
  &lt;source srcset="green-backpack.avif" type="image/avif"&gt;
  &lt;source srcset="green-backpack.webp" type="image/webp"&gt;
  &lt;img src="green-backpack.jpg" alt="Green 30L hiking backpack with front zip pocket"
       width="800" height="600"&gt;
&lt;/picture&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Many CMSs and image plugins can generate these versions automatically, so check your platform''s settings before doing it by hand.</p>

<h2>Compress and resize before you upload</h2>
<p><strong>The biggest image win on most small sites is simply not uploading images far larger than they are displayed.</strong> A photo shown 800 pixels wide does not need to be 4,000 pixels wide. Resizing it is often a bigger saving than any change of format.</p>
<p>A practical routine:</p>
<ol>
<li>Resize to the largest size the image is actually shown at. Up to about twice that width covers high-density phone and laptop screens.</li>
<li>Export in a modern format, or let your CMS convert it.</li>
<li>Compress with a quality setting you have checked by eye. Lower it until you can see a difference, then step back up.</li>
<li>For images shown at very different sizes on mobile and desktop, use <code>srcset</code> so phones get a smaller file.</li>
</ol>
<p>As a rule of thumb, treat any content image over about 100 KB as worth a second look. That is a prompt to review, not a hard limit: a large, full-width hero photo may reasonably be bigger, while a small thumbnail of that size almost certainly is not compressed.</p>

<h2>Always set width and height</h2>
<p>When an image has no dimensions in the HTML, the browser does not know how much space to reserve, so the content below jumps when the image arrives. That movement counts towards Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS), one of the Core Web Vitals.</p>
<p>web.dev''s guide to <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://web.dev/articles/optimize-cls">optimizing layout shift</a> lists images without dimensions as one of the most common causes of poor CLS, and recommends always including <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> attributes (or reserving the space with CSS <code>aspect-ratio</code>).</p>
<p>Set the attributes to the image''s real pixel size, without units, for example <code>width="800" height="600"</code>. Then add <code>height: auto</code> in your CSS so the image still scales down on small screens. The browser uses the ratio to reserve the right amount of space.</p>

<h2>Lazy-load images below the fold, never the hero</h2>
<p>Adding <code>loading="lazy"</code> to an image tells the browser to wait until the user scrolls near it before downloading it. It is supported natively in modern browsers and saves data on long pages with many images.</p>
<p>The catch is where you use it. web.dev''s article on <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://web.dev/articles/browser-level-image-lazy-loading">browser-level lazy loading</a> warns against lazy-loading images that are likely to be in the viewport when the page loads, especially the image that is the Largest Contentful Paint element. <strong>Lazy-loading your hero image makes the most important image on the page load later, not sooner.</strong></p>
<p>What to do:</p>
<ul>
<li>Leave the hero image and anything visible on first load without <code>loading="lazy"</code>.</li>
<li>Add <code>loading="lazy"</code> to images further down the page, such as gallery images, product grids, and blog post images below the intro.</li>
<li>Check what your CMS or plugin does by default. Some lazy-load every image, including the hero.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Image sitemaps: when you need one</h2>
<p>Most small sites do not need a separate image sitemap. If your images are in normal <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> tags on pages Google can crawl, it can find them.</p>
<p>Google describes <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/sitemaps/image-sitemaps">image sitemaps</a> as a way to tell it about images it might not otherwise find, such as images loaded with JavaScript. You can either create a separate image sitemap or add image tags to your existing sitemap. If you run a gallery or shop where images are loaded by a script, it is worth considering. Otherwise, focus on the basics above first.</p>

<h2>How to audit images across your site</h2>
<p>Checking images one page at a time does not scale past a handful of pages. A crawl gives you the full list. A free desktop crawler like <a href="https://www.scaryspiderseo.com">Scary Spider SEO</a> flags images missing alt text, images over 100 KB, images missing width or height attributes, and broken images, and lets you export the list to CSV.</p>
<p>Work through the results in this order: broken images, then missing alt text on meaningful images, then the largest files on your most visited pages, then missing dimensions. If you are running image checks as part of a wider review, our <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/technical-seo-audit-checklist">technical SEO audit checklist</a> shows where they fit alongside speed and crawlability.</p>

<h2>The bottom line</h2>
<p>Good image SEO comes down to a few habits: describe images with useful alt text (or <code>alt=""</code> if they are decorative), give files descriptive names, use modern formats at sensible sizes, always set width and height, and lazy-load only what sits below the fold.</p>
<p>None of these take long once they are part of your upload routine. The hard part is fixing years of old uploads, and a crawl turns that into a sorted to-do list instead of guesswork.</p>
<p><strong>Your next step:</strong> crawl your site, sort images by file size, and fix the five largest images on your most visited pages, adding alt text and dimensions as you go.</p>', '["Put important images in img tags, because Google does not index CSS background images.", "Alt text should describe what an image shows in context, and decorative images should use an empty alt attribute.", "Resizing images to their display size is usually the biggest saving, followed by modern formats like WebP or AVIF.", "Always set width and height attributes so the browser reserves space and the layout does not shift.", "Lazy-load images below the fold, but never the hero image or anything visible when the page first loads."]', '[{"question": "How long should alt text be?", "answer": "There is no fixed limit, but a short phrase or one sentence is usually enough. Describe what matters about the image in context and move longer explanations, such as chart data, into the page text."}, {"question": "Do decorative images need alt text?", "answer": "They need an alt attribute, but it should be empty, written as alt with two quote marks and nothing between them. That tells screen readers to skip the image instead of reading out its file name."}, {"question": "Is WebP or AVIF better for SEO?", "answer": "Google supports both, so neither has a direct ranking advantage. Both are usually smaller than JPEG at similar quality, which helps speed. Use whichever your CMS and audience support, with a JPEG fallback if needed."}, {"question": "What size should images be for a website?", "answer": "Resize each image to roughly the largest size it is displayed at, up to about twice that width for high-density screens. Many content images can be well under 100 KB, while large hero photos may need more."}, {"question": "Should I lazy load all images?", "answer": "No. Lazy-load images further down the page, but load the hero image and anything visible on first load normally. Lazy-loading the main image delays it and can hurt Largest Contentful Paint."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'On-page SEO', 'Image SEO: Alt Text, File Size and Dimensions Guide', 'Practical image SEO: write useful alt text, name files well, choose WebP or AVIF, compress images, set width and height, and lazy-load the right images.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = 'image-seo-alt-text');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT 'structured-data-for-beginners', 'Structured Data for Beginners: A Practical Schema Markup Guide', 'What structured data is, why JSON-LD is the format to use, which schema types still earn rich results for small sites, and how to write and test your markup.', '<p>Search for a recipe, a product, or a local event and some results look richer than others. They show a star rating, a price and stock status, a cooking time, or event dates right in the listing. Next to them, a plain blue link with two lines of text can look like the weaker option.</p>
<p>Most of those enhanced listings depend on structured data: a small block of code that describes the page to search engines in a format they can read reliably. It is less technical than it sounds, and most small sites only need a handful of types.</p>
<p>This guide explains what structured data is, why JSON-LD is the format to use, which schema types are worth adding for a small site (and which are no longer worth the effort), how a JSON-LD block is put together, and how to test it. Structured data is one item on our <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/on-page-seo-checklist">on-page SEO checklist</a>, usually done once per template rather than once per page.</p>

<h2>What structured data is</h2>
<p>Structured data is a standardized way of labeling the content on a page. Instead of Google having to guess that "4.6" is a rating or that "£24.99" is a price, you state it directly using a shared vocabulary from schema.org.</p>
<p>According to Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/intro-structured-data">introduction to structured data</a>, Google uses it to understand the content of a page and to show special search result features, called rich results. Recipes, products, events, and articles are common examples.</p>
<p><strong>Structured data makes a page eligible for rich results. It does not guarantee them, and it is not a shortcut to higher rankings.</strong> Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/sd-policies">structured data guidelines</a> say plainly that correct markup does not guarantee a rich result will appear, even if it passes the Rich Results Test.</p>
<p>Say you run a hypothetical cookery blog with 60 recipes. Adding Recipe markup lets Google show cooking times and ratings for those pages. It does not change whether Google considers your recipe the best answer for the search.</p>

<h2>JSON-LD vs microdata: which format to use</h2>
<p>Google supports three formats: JSON-LD, microdata, and RDFa. Google says all three are equally fine as long as they are valid, but recommends JSON-LD because it is the easiest to implement and maintain at scale.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>JSON-LD</strong> is a self-contained block of code inside a <code>&lt;script type="application/ld+json"&gt;</code> tag. It sits apart from your visible HTML, so you can add or change it without touching the page layout.</li>
<li><strong>Microdata</strong> adds attributes such as <code>itemprop</code> directly to your HTML elements. It works, but the markup gets tangled with your design, and a theme change can break it.</li>
<li><strong>RDFa</strong> works in a similar way to microdata and is less common on small sites.</li>
</ul>
<p>Google can also read JSON-LD that is added to the page by JavaScript, which is how many CMS plugins and tag managers insert it. <strong>For a small site, use JSON-LD unless your platform forces something else.</strong></p>

<h2>Which schema types are worth it for a small site</h2>
<p>Schema.org has hundreds of types. Google only uses a subset for rich results, listed in its <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/search-gallery">structured data search gallery</a>. For most small businesses and blogs, these are the ones that matter:</p>
<h3>Organization</h3>
<p>Describes your business: name, logo, website, social profiles, and contact details. Google says some of these properties can influence which logo appears in search results and in your knowledge panel. Add it once, usually on the home page or about page.</p>
<h3>Article</h3>
<p>For blog posts and news content, using <code>Article</code>, <code>BlogPosting</code>, or <code>NewsArticle</code>. Google lists no required properties, but recommended ones such as the headline, images, author, and publish and modified dates help it understand the article.</p>
<h3>Product</h3>
<p>For pages about a single product. Product snippets can show price, availability, and review ratings. Google requires the product name and at least one of <code>offers</code>, <code>review</code>, or <code>aggregateRating</code>. If customers can buy directly from you, Google also documents merchant listing markup with more shopping detail.</p>
<h3>LocalBusiness</h3>
<p>For businesses with a physical location or service area: address, opening hours, phone number, and geo coordinates. The name and address are required. For local visibility, your Google Business Profile usually matters more than this markup, which is covered in our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/offpage-seo/off-page-seo-guide">building authority off your site</a>.</p>
<h3>BreadcrumbList</h3>
<p>Shows where a page sits in your site structure, such as Home &gt; Guides &gt; Composting. Since January 2025, Google shows breadcrumbs only in desktop results, not on mobile, according to its <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/updates">Search documentation updates log</a>. It is still easy to add and useful on desktop.</p>
<h3>Types to skip</h3>
<p>Some markup that was popular a few years ago no longer produces rich results:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>FAQ rich results.</strong> Google limited them to well-known government and health sites in 2023, then stopped showing the FAQ rich result in Google Search from May 2026 and removed its documentation.</li>
<li><strong>How-to rich results.</strong> No longer shown on desktop or mobile since 2023.</li>
<li><strong>Several niche types</strong>, including course info, estimated salary, special announcement, and vehicle listing, were phased out in 2025.</li>
</ul>
<p>Before adding any type, check that it is still listed in Google''s search gallery. Rich result support changes over time, and plenty of older tutorials still recommend markup Google no longer displays.</p>

<h2>How a JSON-LD block is put together</h2>
<p>Here is a complete example for a hypothetical blog post. It goes anywhere in the page''s <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;script type="application/ld+json"&gt;
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BlogPosting",
  "headline": "How to Start a Compost Bin in a Small Garden",
  "image": "https://www.example.com/images/compost-bin.jpg",
  "datePublished": "2026-03-14",
  "dateModified": "2026-09-02",
  "author": {
    "@type": "Person",
    "name": "Sam Taylor",
    "url": "https://www.example.com/about/sam-taylor"
  }
}
&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Line by line:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>@context</code> says which vocabulary you are using. It is always <code>https://schema.org</code>.</li>
<li><code>@type</code> says what the thing is: here, a blog post.</li>
<li>The other lines are properties of that thing. Each one must match what is visible on the page: the same headline, the same dates, the same author.</li>
<li><code>author</code> is a nested object with its own <code>@type</code>. Nesting is how you describe related things, like a person who wrote the post or an offer attached to a product.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>In practice, most small sites should add structured data through their CMS or an SEO plugin, then check the output, rather than writing it by hand on every page.</strong> Hand-written JSON-LD is mainly useful in custom templates, where you write it once and fill it from page data.</p>

<h2>How to test structured data</h2>
<p>Use two free tools, because they check different things:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Google''s Rich Results Test</strong> shows which Google rich results the page is eligible for, and flags errors and warnings for those features. Errors on required properties make the page ineligible for that feature. Warnings usually mean a recommended property is missing.</li>
<li><strong>The <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://validator.schema.org/">Schema Markup Validator</a></strong> checks your markup against the whole schema.org vocabulary, including types Google does not use for rich results. It is useful for catching typos and wrong property names.</li>
</ul>
<p>After the page is live, use the URL Inspection tool in Search Console to confirm Google has seen the markup, and keep an eye on the rich result reports in Search Console. Google recommends this because markup that worked in testing can break after deployment due to templating or serving issues.</p>

<h2>Common structured data mistakes</h2>
<p>Most structured data problems are not syntax errors. They are markup that says something the page does not.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Markup that does not match the visible content.</strong> Google''s guidelines say structured data should represent the main content of the page and not describe content hidden from users. A price, rating, or review in the markup must be on the page too. The same rule applies to your snippet text, which is why it helps to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/meta-descriptions-that-get-clicks">write meta descriptions that match the page</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Stale values.</strong> The price or stock status in the markup no longer matches the page after a sale ends, because the markup is hard-coded.</li>
<li><strong>Wrong type for the page.</strong> Product markup on a category page listing 30 products, or Article markup on a contact page.</li>
<li><strong>Duplicate or conflicting blocks.</strong> A theme and an SEO plugin both output Organization markup with different names or logos.</li>
<li><strong>Missing required properties.</strong> Google says you must include all required properties for a page to be eligible for that rich result.</li>
<li><strong>Self-made reviews and ratings.</strong> Ratings you made up, or reviews not shown on the page, break Google''s guidelines and can lead to a manual action.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to audit structured data across a site</h2>
<p>Testing pages one at a time works for a few templates. To find pages where markup is missing or broken across the whole site, crawl it. A free desktop crawler like <a href="https://www.scaryspiderseo.com">Scary Spider SEO</a> flags pages with invalid or missing structured data alongside other issues, so you can see which templates need attention.</p>
<p>Because structured data is usually generated by templates, fix the template and then retest one page of each type in the Rich Results Test. If you are reviewing the whole site, add structured data as a step in your regular <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/technical-seo-audit-checklist">technical SEO site audit</a>.</p>

<h2>The bottom line</h2>
<p>Structured data helps search engines understand your pages and makes them eligible for rich results, but it does not guarantee them or boost rankings on its own. For most small sites, Organization, Article, Product, LocalBusiness, and BreadcrumbList cover everything that matters, written in JSON-LD.</p>
<p>Keep the markup honest and in sync with what is on the page, check Google''s search gallery before adding a type, and test every template with the Rich Results Test before and after it goes live.</p>
<p><strong>Your next step:</strong> run your home page and one blog post or product page through the Rich Results Test, and fix any errors or mismatches it shows before adding new types.</p>', '["Structured data labels page content with the schema.org vocabulary so search engines can understand it and show rich results.", "Google supports JSON-LD, microdata and RDFa, and recommends JSON-LD because it is easiest to maintain.", "Organization, Article, Product, LocalBusiness and BreadcrumbList cover what most small sites need.", "FAQ and How-to rich results are no longer shown in Google Search, so check the search gallery before adding any type.", "Markup must match the visible content of the page, and correct markup makes a page eligible for rich results but never guarantees them."]', '[{"question": "Does structured data improve rankings?", "answer": "Not directly. Structured data helps Google understand a page and makes it eligible for rich results, which can make a listing more useful to searchers. It does not make Google rank a page higher on its own."}, {"question": "What is the difference between schema markup and structured data?", "answer": "The terms are often used interchangeably. Structured data is the general idea of labeling content in a standard format, and schema markup usually means structured data written with the schema.org vocabulary, which is what Google uses."}, {"question": "Why are my rich results not showing?", "answer": "Valid markup only makes a page eligible. Google may not show a rich result if the markup does not match the visible content, required properties are missing, the feature is no longer supported, or Google decides a plain result fits the search better."}, {"question": "Should I still add FAQ schema?", "answer": "Not for Google rich results. Google stopped showing the FAQ rich result in Search in 2026 and removed its documentation, so the time is better spent on types that are still supported."}, {"question": "Can I add structured data without coding?", "answer": "Yes. Most CMS platforms and SEO plugins can output JSON-LD from fields you fill in. Test the output with the Rich Results Test, because plugins and themes can produce duplicate or incomplete markup."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'On-page SEO', 'Structured Data for Beginners: Schema Markup Guide', 'A beginner''s guide to structured data: JSON-LD vs microdata, which schema types still earn rich results, a worked example, and how to test your markup.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = 'structured-data-for-beginners');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT 'core-web-vitals-explained', 'Core Web Vitals Explained in Plain English: LCP, INP, CLS', 'What LCP, INP and CLS measure, the official thresholds, where to find your real numbers, the fixes that work, and how much Core Web Vitals affect rankings.', '<p>You paste your homepage into PageSpeed Insights and get back three acronyms, a score out of 100, and a list of suggestions that reads like a developer''s to-do list. Somewhere near the top, it says your page fails the Core Web Vitals assessment.</p><p>Is that an emergency or a footnote? <strong>Core Web Vitals</strong> are Google''s three measures of how a page feels to a real visitor: how fast the main content appears, how quickly the page reacts when someone taps or clicks, and whether things jump around while it loads. They matter, just not in the way most panicked forum threads suggest.</p><p>This guide explains each metric in plain English, gives you the official thresholds, shows where to find your real numbers, and covers the most common fix for each one. If you are working through a full <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/technical-seo-audit-checklist">technical SEO audit checklist</a>, this is the performance step.</p><h2>What Core Web Vitals actually measure</h2><p>There are three metrics. Each one has a "good" threshold that Google publishes, and each covers a different part of the experience.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it measures</th><th>Good</th><th>In plain English</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>LCP (Largest Contentful Paint)</td><td>Loading</td><td>2.5 seconds or less</td><td>How long until the biggest image or text block in view appears</td></tr><tr><td>INP (Interaction to Next Paint)</td><td>Responsiveness</td><td>200 milliseconds or less</td><td>How long the page takes to visibly react after a click, tap or key press</td></tr><tr><td>CLS (Cumulative Layout Shift)</td><td>Visual stability</td><td>0.1 or less</td><td>How much the layout moves around unexpectedly</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>A page passes when it meets all three "good" thresholds at the 75th percentile of real visits.</strong> In other words, at least three out of four page loads need to be at or better than each target. Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://web.dev/articles/vitals">Web Vitals overview on web.dev</a> sets out these thresholds and the 75th percentile rule, measured separately for mobile and desktop.</p><p>One update to be aware of: INP replaced First Input Delay (FID) as a Core Web Vital in March 2024. If an article or tool still talks about FID, it is out of date.</p><h2>Field data vs lab data: why your numbers disagree</h2><p>You will see two kinds of numbers, and they often disagree. That confuses a lot of site owners, so it is worth getting straight.</p><ul><li><strong>Field data</strong> comes from real Chrome users visiting your site, collected in the Chrome UX Report (CrUX) over a rolling 28-day window. It reflects real devices, real networks and real behavior.</li><li><strong>Lab data</strong> comes from a single simulated page load, usually run by Lighthouse on a throttled mid-range phone profile. It is repeatable, which makes it good for debugging.</li></ul><p><strong>For Search, the numbers that count are the field data, not the Lighthouse score out of 100.</strong> The Performance score is a lab diagnostic. It is useful, but it is not what Google''s Core Web Vitals report is built on.</p><p>Say you run a bakery site with 40 pages. PageSpeed Insights shows a lab LCP of 4.1 seconds, but the field LCP is 2.2 seconds because most of your customers browse on recent phones over good connections. You pass. The lab number still tells you where the slow parts are, so it is worth acting on, but it is not a failing grade.</p><p>Small sites often have no field data at all, because CrUX needs a minimum amount of traffic before it reports anything. In that case, use lab data as your guide and focus on the fixes below.</p><h2>Where to check your Core Web Vitals</h2><h3>Google Search Console</h3><p>The <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/9205520">Core Web Vitals report in Search Console</a> uses field data and groups similar URLs together, rated Poor, Need improvement or Good, separately for mobile and desktop. A group''s status is set by its worst metric, so a group with good INP but poor CLS shows as Poor. Only indexed URLs appear.</p><p>This is the best place to start because it shows patterns. If 300 blog posts are all in one "Poor CLS" group, the cause is almost always the shared template, not 300 separate problems.</p><h3>PageSpeed Insights</h3><p>PageSpeed Insights shows field data for the specific URL (and the whole origin) at the top when enough data exists, then a Lighthouse lab run underneath. Use the top section to judge, and the bottom section to diagnose. The lab report also names your LCP element and lists the elements that shifted, which saves a lot of guessing.</p><h3>Actions</h3><ul><li>Open the Search Console report and look at mobile first, since that is usually where problems are worst.</li><li>Note which metric is failing and which URL groups are affected.</li><li>Open one example URL from each group in PageSpeed Insights to find the cause.</li></ul><h2>LCP: get the main content on screen faster</h2><p>The LCP element is usually the hero image, a large banner, or the main headline. LCP gets slow for four common reasons: the server responds slowly, the image is huge, the browser discovers the image late, or render-blocking CSS and JavaScript hold everything up.</p><p>A typical example: a homepage hero is a 2.4 MB photo straight off a camera, it is set as a CSS background (so the browser finds it late), and a plugin has added lazy loading to it. All three slow down LCP.</p><p><strong>The fastest LCP wins usually come from the hero image itself.</strong> Here is what to do:</p><ul><li>Resize and compress the hero image, and serve a modern format such as WebP or AVIF. Our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/image-seo-alt-text">image file size and formats</a> covers this in detail.</li><li>Never lazy-load the LCP image. Lazy loading is for images below the fold.</li><li>Use a normal <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> element rather than a CSS background, and consider <code>fetchpriority="high"</code> on it so the browser requests it early.</li><li>Reduce server response time with page caching or a CDN, especially on shared hosting.</li><li>Cut or defer render-blocking scripts and stylesheets that are not needed for the first screen.</li></ul><p>Google''s guide to <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://web.dev/articles/optimize-lcp">optimizing Largest Contentful Paint</a> breaks LCP into stages so you can see which one is eating the time.</p><h2>INP: make the page respond quickly</h2><p>INP looks at the clicks, taps and key presses during a visit and reports roughly the slowest one. If someone taps your menu button and nothing visibly happens for half a second, that is a bad INP.</p><p>The usual cause is JavaScript keeping the browser''s main thread busy. Chat widgets, tag managers loaded with many tags, ad scripts, sliders and heavy page builders all compete for the same thread. While the browser is busy running them, it cannot respond to the visitor.</p><p>Say a local plumber''s site loads a chat widget, two analytics tools, a heatmap script and a booking plugin on every page. The "Call now" button feels sluggish on mid-range phones, even though the page looked loaded.</p><p><strong>Most INP problems on small business sites are fixed by running less JavaScript, not by tuning it.</strong></p><ul><li>List every third-party script on the site and remove the ones nobody uses any more.</li><li>Load non-essential scripts (chat, heatmaps) later or only on the pages that need them.</li><li>Show immediate visual feedback when a button is pressed, even if the work behind it takes longer.</li><li>Ask your developer to break up long JavaScript tasks and simplify very large pages with thousands of elements.</li></ul><p>Heavy JavaScript can also stop Google from seeing your content properly, which is a separate problem covered in our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/web-performance/javascript-seo-rendering">how JavaScript rendering affects SEO</a>.</p><h2>CLS: stop the page from jumping</h2><p>CLS measures unexpected movement. You go to tap "Book now", a banner loads above it, the button moves, and you tap "Call us" instead. Shifts that happen right after a user''s own input do not count; this metric is about movement nobody asked for.</p><p>Common causes:</p><ul><li>Images, videos and iframes without <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> attributes, so the browser does not know how much space to reserve.</li><li>Ads, embeds or review widgets injected into the page without a reserved slot.</li><li>Cookie or promo bars that push content down instead of overlaying it.</li><li>Web fonts that swap in late with very different sizing.</li></ul><p><strong>Reserve space for everything before it loads.</strong> Add width and height to every image and embed (CSS can still make them responsive), give ad and widget containers a minimum height, and show banners as overlays at the top or bottom of the screen.</p><h2>Mobile is where Core Web Vitals usually fail</h2><p>Phones have slower processors and often slower connections, so mobile scores are usually worse than desktop. Search Console reports mobile and desktop separately, so check both, but start with mobile, where problems tend to show up first. Our <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/web-performance/mobile-first-indexing-checklist">mobile-first indexing checklist</a> covers the rest of what needs to work on phones.</p><p>Test on a real mid-range phone once in a while, not just on your laptop. A page that feels instant on office Wi-Fi can feel very different on a three-year-old phone on mobile data.</p><h2>How much do Core Web Vitals matter for rankings?</h2><p>Google says Core Web Vitals are used by its ranking systems, but there is no single "page experience signal". Its <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/page-experience">page experience documentation</a> also says good scores do not guarantee top rankings, and that Google will still show the most relevant content even when its page experience is weak. Where many pages are similarly helpful, a better experience can contribute to success.</p><p><strong>Treat Core Web Vitals as something that stops you losing, not something that makes you win.</strong> A fast page with thin content will not outrank a genuinely useful one. But if you and a competitor are equally relevant, a page that loads slowly and jumps around is giving away an advantage, and it frustrates the visitors you worked to attract.</p><p>Page experience is also wider than speed. A site that works for everyone, including people using screen readers or keyboards, tends to be a better experience overall. See where <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/accessibility/accessibility-and-seo">accessibility and SEO overlap</a> for that side of it.</p><h2>A simple Core Web Vitals workflow</h2><ol><li>Open the Core Web Vitals report in Search Console and start with mobile.</li><li>Pick the Poor URL group that covers your most important pages (product, service or lead pages before old blog posts).</li><li>Run one example URL through PageSpeed Insights and identify the failing metric and its cause.</li><li>Fix the cause in the template or theme, so every page in the group benefits.</li><li>Click "Validate fix" in Search Console. Because field data covers a rolling 28 days, expect confirmation to take about four weeks.</li><li>Look for site-wide patterns with a crawl. A free desktop crawler like <a href="https://www.scaryspiderseo.com">Scary Spider SEO</a> flags images over 100 KB, images missing width and height, and pages with slow server responses across the whole site, which are common contributors to LCP and CLS problems.</li></ol><h2>The bottom line</h2><p>Core Web Vitals come down to three questions: does the main content show up quickly, does the page respond when someone uses it, and does it stay still while loading? The targets are LCP of 2.5 seconds or less, INP of 200 milliseconds or less, and CLS of 0.1 or less, measured on real visits at the 75th percentile.</p><p>They are a real ranking input, but a modest one next to relevance and content quality. Fix the template-level problems behind your Poor URL groups, starting with mobile and your most valuable pages, and do not chase a perfect lab score for its own sake.</p><p><strong>Your next step:</strong> open the Core Web Vitals report in Search Console today, note which metric fails on mobile, and run one affected URL through PageSpeed Insights to find its LCP element or shifting elements.</p>', '["Core Web Vitals measure loading (LCP), responsiveness (INP) and visual stability (CLS).", "A page passes when LCP is 2.5 seconds or less, INP is 200 milliseconds or less and CLS is 0.1 or less at the 75th percentile of real visits.", "Search Console''s Core Web Vitals report uses field data from real Chrome users, while the Lighthouse score is a lab diagnostic.", "Most fixes are template-level: optimize the hero image, run less JavaScript, and reserve space for images, ads and banners.", "Core Web Vitals are used in ranking, but good scores do not replace relevant, helpful content."]', '[{"question": "Are Core Web Vitals a Google ranking factor?", "answer": "Google says Core Web Vitals are used by its ranking systems as part of page experience. They do not override relevance, so a slower page with clearly better content can still rank above a faster one. They matter most when competing pages are similarly helpful."}, {"question": "Why does Search Console say no data is available for Core Web Vitals?", "answer": "The report relies on field data from the Chrome UX Report, which needs a minimum amount of real Chrome traffic per URL group. New or low-traffic sites often have none. In that case, use PageSpeed Insights lab results to find and fix problems."}, {"question": "Do I need a PageSpeed Insights score of 100?", "answer": "No. The score out of 100 is a lab measurement from a single simulated load and is not what the Core Web Vitals assessment uses. Aim to pass the three field thresholds instead of chasing a perfect lab score."}, {"question": "What replaced First Input Delay?", "answer": "Interaction to Next Paint (INP) replaced First Input Delay as a Core Web Vital in March 2024. INP looks at responsiveness across the whole visit rather than only the first interaction, so it gives a more complete picture."}, {"question": "How long does it take for Core Web Vitals fixes to show up?", "answer": "Lab tools show improvements immediately after you deploy a fix. Field data in Search Console is based on a rolling 28-day window, so expect around four weeks before the report fully reflects the change."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'Web Performance', 'Core Web Vitals Explained: LCP, INP and CLS', 'Core Web Vitals in plain English: what LCP, INP and CLS measure, Google''s good thresholds, field vs lab data, common fixes, and their real ranking impact.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = 'core-web-vitals-explained');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT 'javascript-seo-rendering', 'JavaScript SEO: When Rendering Hides Your Content From Google', 'How Google crawls, renders and indexes JavaScript pages, the mistakes that hide content and links, SSR vs CSR in plain English, and how to compare raw and rendered HTML.', '<p>Your new site looks great in the browser. The product pages load, the filters work, the reviews appear. Then you check Google a few weeks later and half the products are missing, or they show up with a generic snippet that has nothing to do with the product. It is a classic <strong>JavaScript SEO</strong> problem.</p><p>You right-click and choose View Source, and the page is nearly empty: a header, a <code>&lt;div id="root"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</code>, and a few script tags. Everything a visitor sees is built by JavaScript after the page loads.</p><p>Google can run JavaScript, but anything that depends on it is processed later and is easier to break. In this guide you will learn how Google handles JavaScript pages, the specific things that go wrong, the rendering options in plain English, and how to compare what you send with what Google sees. If pages are already missing from Google, start with the wider guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/why-google-is-not-indexing-your-pages">why pages are not indexed</a> as well.</p><h2>How Google crawls, renders and indexes JavaScript</h2><p>Google processes JavaScript pages in three phases: crawling, rendering and indexing. Its <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/javascript/javascript-seo-basics">JavaScript SEO basics guide</a> describes the flow:</p><ol><li><strong>Crawling.</strong> Googlebot checks robots.txt, fetches the URL, and reads the raw HTML the server sends. Links found in that HTML are added to the crawl queue.</li><li><strong>Rendering.</strong> Pages that return a 200 status are queued for rendering, unless a robots meta tag or header says not to index them. When resources allow, a headless version of Chromium runs the JavaScript and builds the final page. The page can sit in that queue for seconds or longer.</li><li><strong>Indexing.</strong> Google uses the rendered HTML to index the content and to find more links.</li></ol><p><strong>The raw HTML is read first and the rendered version arrives later, so anything only present after rendering is a delay and a dependency.</strong> If rendering fails, times out, or is blocked, Google only has the raw HTML to work with.</p><p>Google is also not the only system reading your pages. Many other crawlers and social media link preview tools read only the raw HTML, so a title or description set by JavaScript may never reach them.</p><h2>Raw HTML vs rendered HTML</h2><p>These two terms come up constantly, so it helps to be precise:</p><ul><li><strong>Raw HTML</strong> (also called source or initial HTML) is what the server sends. It is what you see with View Source.</li><li><strong>Rendered HTML</strong> is the page after the browser has run the JavaScript. It is what you see in the Elements panel of your browser''s developer tools.</li></ul><p>Here is a simplified example of a client-side rendered product page. The raw HTML looks like this:</p><pre><code>&lt;title&gt;Shop&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;div id="root"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;script src="/app.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre><p>After rendering, the same page has a product title, a description, a price, reviews and links to related products. Google can see all of that only if rendering succeeds. If the raw HTML already contains the important content, rendering becomes a bonus rather than a requirement.</p><h2>What goes wrong with JavaScript sites</h2><h3>Content that appears only after JavaScript or a click</h3><p>Content built entirely in the browser depends on rendering going well. Content that loads only when someone clicks a tab, presses "Load more" or scrolls in a particular way is riskier still, because Googlebot does not click buttons or type into boxes like a visitor does.</p><p>Say a recipe site loads the ingredient list only when the "Ingredients" tab is clicked, by fetching it from an API at that moment. Google may never see the ingredients at all. If the content is in the HTML and simply hidden in a tab, that is far safer.</p><p><strong>Put the content you want indexed in the HTML, or at least make sure it loads without any user interaction.</strong></p><h3>Links Google cannot follow</h3><p>Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/links-crawlable">link best practices</a> are clear: it can generally only follow links that are <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> elements with an <code>href</code> attribute. A <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> with a click handler, or an <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> without an <code>href</code>, may work for visitors but will not reliably be crawled.</p><p>Single-page apps that switch views with URL fragments (<code>/#/products</code>) have a similar problem. Google does not treat fragment URLs as separate pages, so use real paths (<code>/products</code>) with the History API instead.</p><p>Pages reachable only through those script-driven links end up with no crawlable internal links pointing at them, which is the same problem described in our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/orphan-pages-internal-linking">finding and fixing orphan pages</a>.</p><h3>JavaScript changing the title, canonical or robots tag</h3><p>It is fine to set the title and meta description with JavaScript, but changes to indexing signals need care:</p><ul><li><strong>Canonical tags.</strong> Google recommends setting the canonical in the HTML. If JavaScript changes it, make sure it matches the value in the original HTML rather than pointing somewhere else.</li><li><strong>Meta robots.</strong> If the raw HTML says <code>noindex</code>, Google may skip rendering entirely. JavaScript that removes the noindex later may never run for Googlebot, so the page stays out of the index.</li></ul><p>A common version of this bug: a single-page app ships a default <code>noindex</code> in its HTML shell "for safety" and relies on JavaScript to remove it on real pages. The result can be a site that never gets indexed.</p><h3>Blocked JavaScript, CSS or API files</h3><p>Google will not render JavaScript from files it is not allowed to fetch. If robots.txt disallows <code>/assets/</code>, <code>/js/</code> or the API path your pages pull content from, the rendered page Google builds may be blank or broken. Check robots.txt for any rule that touches the resources your pages need.</p><h3>Error pages that return a 200 status</h3><p>In single-page apps, a "Product not found" view often still returns a 200 status code, because the server just sends the app shell. Google may treat it as a soft 404, or worse, index it. Google''s guidance is to redirect to a URL that returns a real 404 or to add a <code>noindex</code> robots tag to the error view with JavaScript.</p><h2>SSR, SSG and CSR in plain English</h2><p>The fix for most JavaScript SEO problems is to change where the HTML gets built. These are the main options, using the terms from Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://web.dev/articles/rendering-on-the-web">rendering on the web overview</a>:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Where the HTML is built</th><th>SEO risk</th><th>Good fit for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Client-side rendering (CSR)</td><td>In the visitor''s browser, by JavaScript</td><td>Highest: content depends on rendering</td><td>Logged-in dashboards and app screens that do not need to rank</td></tr><tr><td>Server-side rendering (SSR)</td><td>On the server, for each request</td><td>Low: content is in the raw HTML</td><td>Pages with frequently changing content, such as stock levels</td></tr><tr><td>Static rendering (SSG)</td><td>Ahead of time, when the site is built</td><td>Low: content is in the raw HTML</td><td>Blogs, docs, service pages and marketing pages</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>For pages you want to rank, send the main content, links and metadata in the raw HTML through SSR or static rendering.</strong> Most modern frameworks support this, often with JavaScript taking over afterwards to make the page interactive.</p><p>You may also read about dynamic rendering, which serves pre-rendered HTML to bots and the normal app to users. Google now calls <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/javascript/dynamic-rendering">dynamic rendering a workaround</a> rather than a recommended solution, so treat it as a stopgap, not a plan.</p><p>Heavy client-side JavaScript has a performance cost too: it keeps the browser busy and can hurt responsiveness, which shows up in your <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/web-performance/core-web-vitals-explained">Core Web Vitals scores</a>.</p><h2>How to compare raw and rendered HTML</h2><p>You do not need to be a developer to spot the big problems. Work through these checks on one important page of each type (home, category, product or service, article):</p><ol><li><strong>View Source vs Inspect.</strong> Open View Source and search for a sentence from the main content. Then open the Elements panel in developer tools and search again. If the sentence only appears in the second, that content depends on JavaScript.</li><li><strong>Turn JavaScript off.</strong> Disable JavaScript in your browser settings and reload. Whatever disappears is what Google would miss if rendering failed.</li><li><strong>URL Inspection in Search Console.</strong> Run a live test and view the tested page''s HTML and screenshot. This shows what Google''s renderer actually produced, plus any resources it could not load.</li><li><strong>Compare key tags.</strong> Check that the title, canonical, meta robots and internal links are the same in both versions.</li><li><strong>Check at scale with a crawl.</strong> A free desktop crawler like <a href="https://www.scaryspiderseo.com">Scary Spider SEO</a> can crawl with optional headless Chrome rendering and flag pages where content or links are added only by JavaScript, and where JavaScript changes the title, canonical or meta robots.</li></ol><p>Also check the mobile version of key templates. Google indexes the mobile version of your pages, so the parity rules in our <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/web-performance/mobile-first-indexing-checklist">mobile-first indexing checklist</a> apply to rendered content too.</p><h2>A quick JavaScript SEO checklist</h2><ul><li>Main content is present in the raw HTML, or at least loads without any click, scroll or typing.</li><li>Every internal link is an <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code> pointing to a real URL, not a fragment or a click handler.</li><li>Title, meta description, canonical and meta robots are in the raw HTML and JavaScript does not contradict them.</li><li>No <code>noindex</code> in the HTML shell of pages you want indexed.</li><li>robots.txt does not block the scripts, styles or API endpoints your pages need.</li><li>Missing pages return a real 404 or carry a <code>noindex</code>.</li><li>Pages that need to rank use server-side or static rendering.</li></ul><h2>The bottom line</h2><p>JavaScript SEO is mostly about one question: what is in the HTML before any JavaScript runs? Google can render JavaScript, but rendering happens later, can fail, and is skipped for blocked resources and noindexed pages. The more your important content, links and tags rely on it, the more can go wrong.</p><p>You do not have to give up a JavaScript framework. Use server-side or static rendering for pages that need to rank, use real links, keep indexing signals in the raw HTML, and regularly compare raw and rendered versions so problems surface before your traffic drops.</p><p><strong>Your next step:</strong> pick your most important product or service page, compare View Source with the rendered page in developer tools, and run it through URL Inspection to confirm Google sees the same content your visitors do.</p>', '["Google crawls the raw HTML first and renders JavaScript later, so content that depends on rendering is processed with a delay and can be missed.", "Google can generally only follow links that are a elements with an href attribute pointing to a real URL.", "A noindex in the raw HTML can stop Google from rendering the page, so JavaScript that removes it may never take effect.", "Server-side or static rendering puts content, links and metadata in the raw HTML, which is the safest setup for pages that need to rank.", "Comparing View Source, the rendered DOM and Search Console''s URL Inspection live test reveals most JavaScript SEO problems."]', '[{"question": "Can Google index JavaScript websites?", "answer": "Yes. Google renders pages with a headless version of Chromium and indexes the rendered result. Rendering happens after crawling and can be delayed or fail, so content that only exists after JavaScript runs is less reliable than content in the raw HTML."}, {"question": "Is client-side rendering bad for SEO?", "answer": "Not automatically, but it carries the most risk because every piece of content and every link depends on rendering. For pages you want to rank, server-side or static rendering is the safer choice. Client-side rendering is fine for logged-in or app-like screens that do not need to appear in search."}, {"question": "How do I see what Googlebot sees on my page?", "answer": "Use the URL Inspection tool in Search Console and run a live test, then view the tested page''s HTML and screenshot. The Rich Results Test also shows rendered HTML for public URLs. Compare that with what you see in your own browser."}, {"question": "Does Google click buttons or scroll to load content?", "answer": "No. Google does not interact with pages the way a visitor does, so content that loads only after a click, swipe or typing may never be seen. Content that is already in the HTML but hidden in a tab or accordion is a much safer pattern."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'Web Performance', 'JavaScript SEO: How Rendering Can Hide Your Content', 'JavaScript SEO explained: how Google renders JS, why content and links go missing, SSR vs CSR in plain English, and how to check raw and rendered HTML.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = 'javascript-seo-rendering');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT 'mobile-first-indexing-checklist', 'Mobile-First Indexing: A Checklist for Small Business Sites', 'Google ranks the mobile version of your site. Use this checklist to match content, links and metadata across devices and fix viewport, tap target and pop-up issues.', '<p>On a laptop, your site looks complete: a full services list, customer reviews, an FAQ section and a footer full of useful links. On a phone, the theme quietly drops the reviews, cuts the FAQ, and tucks the navigation into a menu with half the links missing. Under mobile-first indexing, that stripped-down phone version is the one that counts.</p><p>Most owners never notice, because they edit and check the site on a desktop. But with <strong>mobile-first indexing</strong>, Google uses the mobile version of your pages for indexing and ranking. Whatever your phone layout leaves out is, as far as Google is concerned, not there.</p><p>This checklist walks through what mobile-first indexing means, what has to match between mobile and desktop, the mobile usability basics (viewport, tap targets, text size, pop-ups), and how to test it all now that some of Google''s older mobile tools are gone. It pairs well with the broader <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/on-page-seo-checklist">pre-publish on-page SEO checklist</a>.</p><h2>What mobile-first indexing means</h2><p>Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/mobile/mobile-sites-mobile-first-indexing">mobile-first indexing best practices</a> put it simply: Google uses the mobile version of a site''s content, crawled with its smartphone crawler, for indexing and ranking. There is no separate mobile index. The mobile version simply is the version Google looks at.</p><p>How much work this means depends on how your site is built:</p><ul><li><strong>Responsive design:</strong> the same HTML on the same URL, styled differently by screen size. Google recommends this setup as the easiest to implement and maintain, and content and metadata automatically match.</li><li><strong>Dynamic serving:</strong> the same URL, but the server sends different HTML depending on the device. Parity has to be checked.</li><li><strong>Separate mobile URLs:</strong> for example <code>m.example.com</code>. Parity has to be checked, and the two versions need correct <code>rel="canonical"</code> and <code>rel="alternate"</code> links between them.</li></ul><p><strong>If your site is responsive, most of the parity checks below pass by default, but themes and page builders can still hide content or links on small screens.</strong> If you run a separate m-dot site, it is worth asking whether moving to responsive design would save you ongoing work.</p><h2>The parity checklist: mobile must match desktop</h2><h3>1. Same main content</h3><p>Google is explicit that the mobile page should contain the same content as the desktop page, with the same clear headings. If you need to save space on mobile, Google suggests moving content into accordions or tabs instead of removing it, as long as the content stays equivalent.</p><p>Say a dentist''s desktop service page has 800 words explaining a treatment, and the mobile layout shows 150 words and a "Book now" button. Google ranks the 150-word version.</p><p>Action: open your five most important pages on a phone and compare them, section by section, with the desktop version.</p><h3>2. Same internal links</h3><p>Mobile menus are often trimmed, and footers are frequently collapsed or cut down. Since Google crawls the mobile version, the links missing on mobile are links Google may not follow. Check that every important page is still reachable from the mobile navigation or from links in the content.</p><h3>3. Same titles, meta descriptions and robots tags</h3><p>The title and meta description should be equivalent on both versions. Robots meta tags must match: a <code>noindex</code> or <code>nofollow</code> that exists only on mobile can stop pages from being crawled or indexed.</p><h3>4. Same structured data</h3><p>If your desktop pages carry structured data (Product, Breadcrumb, LocalBusiness and so on), it must be present on mobile too, with URLs pointing to the mobile versions if you use separate URLs.</p><h3>5. Same images, with the same alt text</h3><p>Use the same images, alt text, captions and file names on mobile, and avoid tiny or low-resolution images there. Our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/image-seo-alt-text">writing alt text and sizing images</a> covers the details.</p><h3>6. Content that loads without interaction</h3><p>Google will not load content that requires a swipe, click or typing to appear. Lazy loading is fine as long as the content loads when it comes into view, not only after a tap. If content on your mobile pages is assembled by JavaScript, read our guide on <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/web-performance/javascript-seo-rendering">how JavaScript rendering affects indexing</a>.</p><h3>7. Resources Google can crawl</h3><p>Mobile pages often load different images, scripts or stylesheets. Make sure robots.txt does not block any of them, and that it uses the same rules for both versions.</p><h2>Mobile usability: the basics that still matter</h2><h3>The viewport meta tag</h3><p>Without a viewport tag, mobile browsers lay the page out at a desktop width and shrink it, leaving visitors to pinch and zoom. One line in the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> fixes it:</p><pre><code>&lt;meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"&gt;</code></pre><p><strong>Every page needs a viewport tag, and it should not disable zooming.</strong> Avoid <code>user-scalable=no</code> or a low <code>maximum-scale</code>; people with low vision rely on zoom. MDN''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/meta/name/viewport">viewport meta tag reference</a> explains each value.</p><h3>Tap targets</h3><p>Buttons and links that are too small or too close together cause mis-taps, which is frustrating on a contact or checkout page. Google''s web.dev guidance on <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://web.dev/articles/accessible-tap-targets">accessible tap targets</a> recommends a minimum target of about 48 by 48 pixels with around 8 pixels of space between targets.</p><p>Typical offenders are footer links stacked tightly together, small social icons, and inline links packed into a short paragraph. Add padding rather than enlarging the visible icon if you want to keep the design.</p><p>Tap target size overlaps with accessibility requirements, and so do several other mobile basics. See our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/accessibility/accessibility-and-seo">accessibility and SEO</a> for the wider picture.</p><h3>Readable text</h3><p>Visitors should not need to zoom to read your content. A base font size around 16 pixels is a common, comfortable default for body text, with enough line height and contrast to read outdoors. Check long paragraphs on a real phone, not only in a browser window shrunk on your desktop.</p><h3>Pop-ups and interstitials</h3><p>A full-screen newsletter pop-up that appears the moment someone arrives from Google is the classic mobile annoyance. Google''s guidance on <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/avoid-intrusive-interstitials">intrusive interstitials and dialogs</a> says they frustrate users and can make it harder for Google to understand your content. It recommends not covering the entire page and not redirecting people to a separate page for consent or input.</p><p>Legally required notices, such as cookie consent or an age check, are treated differently, but they should still overlay the content rather than replace it.</p><p>Action: replace full-screen promotional pop-ups with a small banner, and delay them until the visitor has engaged with the page.</p><h2>Speed on mobile</h2><p>Phones have slower processors and often slower connections, so mobile performance is usually where sites struggle. Large hero images, heavy sliders and stacks of third-party scripts hurt most. Our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/web-performance/core-web-vitals-explained">LCP, INP and CLS</a> explains how to read your mobile scores and what to fix first.</p><h2>How to test mobile-first indexing readiness</h2><p>Google retired its standalone Mobile-Friendly Test and the Mobile Usability report in Search Console in late 2023, so testing now means combining a few tools:</p><ol><li><strong>URL Inspection in Search Console.</strong> Run a live test on a key URL. Google tests it with its smartphone crawler, so the screenshot and HTML show what Google indexes.</li><li><strong>Lighthouse in Chrome DevTools or PageSpeed Insights.</strong> Run the mobile audit for performance, a missing viewport tag, and touch target size (now checked in the accessibility section).</li><li><strong>Device mode in DevTools.</strong> Switch between phone and desktop views to compare content and links side by side.</li><li><strong>A real phone.</strong> Tap through your menu, forms and checkout. Nothing replaces it.</li><li><strong>A site-wide crawl.</strong> A free desktop crawler like <a href="https://www.scaryspiderseo.com">Scary Spider SEO</a> flags pages missing a viewport tag and other mobile usability issues across the whole site, so one broken template does not go unnoticed.</li></ol><h2>The quick checklist</h2><ul><li>Mobile pages have the same main content and headings as desktop.</li><li>Important internal links are reachable on mobile.</li><li>Titles, meta descriptions and robots tags match.</li><li>Structured data is present on mobile.</li><li>Images and alt text match, and images are not tiny.</li><li>No content requires a tap or swipe to load.</li><li>robots.txt does not block mobile resources.</li><li>Every page has a viewport tag that allows zooming.</li><li>Tap targets are about 48 by 48 pixels with space between them.</li><li>Body text is readable without zooming.</li><li>No full-screen promotional pop-ups on arrival.</li></ul><h2>The bottom line</h2><p>Mobile-first indexing means the phone version of your site is the version Google reads, indexes and ranks. If content, links, metadata or structured data exist only on desktop, Google is ranking a weaker version of your site than the one you see.</p><p>For most small business sites on a responsive theme, the work is a short audit: check that nothing important is hidden on small screens, add a proper viewport tag, make buttons easy to tap, and remove intrusive pop-ups. Then test with URL Inspection and a real phone.</p><p><strong>Your next step:</strong> open your three most important pages on your phone and on your desktop side by side, and list every section, link or image that is missing from the mobile version.</p>', '["Under mobile-first indexing, Google uses the mobile version of your pages, crawled with its smartphone crawler, for indexing and ranking.", "Content, headings, internal links, metadata, robots tags and structured data should be equivalent on mobile and desktop.", "Google will not load content that needs a click, swipe or typing to appear, so primary content must load without interaction.", "Every page needs a viewport meta tag that allows zooming, tap targets of about 48 by 48 pixels, and readable text.", "With the Mobile-Friendly Test retired, test with URL Inspection, Lighthouse, a real phone and a site-wide crawl."]', '[{"question": "Does mobile-first indexing mean desktop pages are ignored?", "answer": "Google uses the mobile version of your content for indexing and ranking, and there is no separate mobile index. Desktop searchers still see your pages in results, but the content Google ranks is what it found on the mobile version."}, {"question": "Is content in accordions and tabs indexed on mobile?", "answer": "Google''s mobile-first indexing guidance suggests moving content into accordions or tabs on mobile instead of removing it, as long as the content stays equivalent to desktop. The content should be in the page when it loads, not fetched only after someone taps."}, {"question": "What replaced Google''s Mobile-Friendly Test?", "answer": "Google retired the Mobile-Friendly Test and the Mobile Usability report in late 2023. You can use Lighthouse in Chrome DevTools or PageSpeed Insights for mobile audits, and the URL Inspection tool in Search Console to see how Google''s smartphone crawler sees a page."}, {"question": "Do I need a separate mobile site?", "answer": "No. Google recommends responsive design, where the same HTML works on every screen size, as the easiest setup to implement and maintain. Separate mobile URLs work but need extra care to keep content and tags in sync."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'Web Performance', 'Mobile-First Indexing Checklist for Small Sites', 'A mobile-first indexing checklist: match content, links, metadata and structured data across devices, and fix viewport, tap targets, text size and pop-ups.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = 'mobile-first-indexing-checklist');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT 'accessibility-and-seo', 'Accessibility and SEO: Where They Overlap and Why It Matters', 'Alt text, headings, link text and semantic HTML help both people and search engines. Learn where accessibility and SEO overlap, what WCAG is, and how to test properly.', '<p>Picture someone using a screen reader on a local plumber''s website. The images are announced as "IMG_4021 dot jpg". Every link says "click here". The headings are just bold text, so there is no way to jump to the section about emergency callouts. They give up and call someone else. This is where accessibility and SEO meet.</p><p>Now look at the same page as a search engine: images with no useful description, links that say nothing about where they go, and no clear structure. The problems are almost identical.</p><p><strong>Accessibility and SEO</strong> share a lot of ground like this. To be clear from the start: Google does not list accessibility as a ranking factor, so a perfect audit score will not move you up the results on its own. But many of the same fixes help both people and search engines, and accessibility shapes how visitors experience your site just as much as <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/web-performance/core-web-vitals-explained">Core Web Vitals and page speed</a> do. This guide covers where the two overlap, what WCAG is in plain English, and how to test properly.</p><h2>Why accessibility is worth your time</h2><p>Accessibility means people with disabilities can perceive, understand, navigate and use your site. That includes people who are blind or have low vision, people who are deaf or hard of hearing, people who cannot use a mouse, and people with cognitive differences. It also helps everyone on a small screen, in bright sunlight, or with a temporary injury.</p><p><strong>The business case does not depend on rankings: an inaccessible site turns away customers who wanted to buy from you.</strong> In many places, accessibility is also a legal requirement for some organizations, so it is worth checking what applies to you.</p><h2>What WCAG is, in plain English</h2><p>The Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) are the international standard for web accessibility, published by the W3C. The <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/">W3C''s WCAG overview</a> explains the structure:</p><ul><li><strong>Four principles:</strong> content must be perceivable, operable, understandable and robust.</li><li><strong>Guidelines</strong> under each principle, such as "provide text alternatives for non-text content".</li><li><strong>Testable success criteria</strong> at three levels: A (the minimum), AA and AAA (the strictest).</li></ul><p>The current versions are WCAG 2.0, 2.1 and 2.2. Each later version adds criteria without changing the earlier ones, with one exception: WCAG 2.2 marks the old 4.1.1 Parsing criterion as obsolete. Level AA is the target most organizations and policies aim for, so it is a sensible goal for a small business site.</p><h2>Where accessibility and SEO overlap</h2><h3>Alt text on images</h3><p>Alt text is read aloud by screen readers and shown when an image fails to load. Search engines use it to understand what an image shows. A good alt describes the image in context ("Plumber fitting a new kitchen tap under a sink"), while decorative images get an empty <code>alt=""</code> so screen readers skip them.</p><p><strong>Write alt text for the person who cannot see the image, and it will usually serve search engines well too.</strong> Do not stuff keywords into it. Our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/image-seo-alt-text">writing useful alt text</a> has good and bad examples.</p><h3>Headings and structure</h3><p>Screen reader users often move through a page by jumping from heading to heading, the way sighted visitors skim. A clear H1 followed by logically nested H2s and H3s makes that possible, and it gives search engines a clear outline of the page''s topics. See our guide on <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/heading-tags-seo">structuring H1, H2 and H3 headings</a> for an outline example.</p><h3>Descriptive link text</h3><p>Screen readers can list every link on a page out of context. A list of ten "click here" and "read more" links is useless. Search engines also use anchor text to understand what the linked page is about.</p><p>Compare "To book a boiler service, <em>click here</em>" with "<em>Book a boiler service</em> online". The second tells both the visitor and Google where the link goes. Our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/orphan-pages-internal-linking">internal linking and anchor text</a> covers the SEO side in more depth.</p><h3>Page titles</h3><p>WCAG requires pages to have titles that describe their topic or purpose. The title is the first thing a screen reader announces and the text that identifies the browser tab. It is also usually the headline of your search result. A unique, descriptive title per page serves all three.</p><h3>The lang attribute</h3><p>The <code>lang</code> attribute on the <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> element (for example <code>&lt;html lang="en"&gt;</code>) tells screen readers which language to pronounce the page in. Without it, a French page might be read with English pronunciation. Google says it uses the visible content to work out a page''s language and <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/specialty/international/managing-multi-regional-sites">does not use the lang attribute</a> for that, so this one is mainly for people. It is still a one-line fix.</p><h3>Captions and transcripts</h3><p>Captions make video usable for deaf and hard-of-hearing visitors, and for anyone watching with the sound off. A transcript on the page also gives search engines indexable text about what the video covers, which they cannot easily get from the audio.</p><h3>Semantic HTML and real links</h3><p>Native elements carry meaning that assistive technology understands: <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>, and links that are real <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code> elements. A <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> with a click handler cannot be reached with the keyboard by default and is not announced as a link or button by screen readers, and it is also a link Google cannot reliably follow. Our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/web-performance/javascript-seo-rendering">JavaScript SEO and crawlable links</a> explains the search side.</p><h3>Contrast and readability</h3><p>WCAG''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/contrast-minimum.html">minimum contrast criterion</a> asks for a contrast ratio of at least 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for large text. Low-contrast text is not a ranking problem, but pale grey text on white is hard for everyone to read, especially on a phone outdoors. People who cannot read your content will not stay to act on it.</p><h2>Automated testing vs manual testing</h2><p>Automated tools are the right place to start. They are fast, they cover many pages, and they catch common, objective problems such as missing alt attributes, missing form labels, empty links and insufficient contrast.</p><p>The most widely used engine is axe-core, an open-source library from Deque that runs rules against the rendered page. Even its makers are upfront about the limits: the <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core">axe-core project page</a> says it finds on average 57% of WCAG issues automatically, and flags some results as needing manual review.</p><p><strong>Automated tools catch part of the problem, not all of it.</strong> The W3C''s guidance on <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/test-evaluate/tools/selecting/">choosing evaluation tools</a> says tools cannot check all accessibility aspects automatically and that human judgment is required. A tool can confirm an image has alt text. It cannot tell whether "image1" is a useful description, whether your focus order makes sense, or whether your error messages are understandable.</p><h3>Run an automated check across the whole site</h3><p>Testing one page in a browser extension misses template problems that repeat across hundreds of pages. A free desktop crawler like <a href="https://www.scaryspiderseo.com">Scary Spider SEO</a> can run axe-core on rendered pages during a crawl, and also flags missing alt text, missing <code>lang</code> attributes, non-sequential headings and non-descriptive or empty anchor text, so you can see which issues are site-wide.</p><h3>Then test with a keyboard</h3><ol><li>Put the mouse aside and press Tab from the top of the page.</li><li>Check that every link, button and form field can be reached, in a logical order.</li><li>Check that you can always see where the focus is.</li><li>Open and close menus, dialogs and accordions with Enter, Space and Escape.</li><li>Make sure you never get stuck inside a component.</li></ol><h3>Then try a screen reader</h3><p>Free options are built in or easy to get: VoiceOver on macOS and iOS, TalkBack on Android, and NVDA on Windows. Spend ten minutes on your most important page. Jump through the headings, open the links list, and try to complete one task, such as sending a contact form. You will quickly hear what an automated report cannot tell you.</p><p>Finally, zoom the browser to 200% and check that the content still reads and nothing is cut off or overlapping.</p><h2>An accessible website checklist for SEO-minded owners</h2><ul><li>Every meaningful image has descriptive alt text; decorative images have empty alt.</li><li>One clear H1 per page, with headings nested in order.</li><li>Link text describes the destination; no bare "click here".</li><li>Every page has a unique, descriptive title.</li><li>The <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> element has the correct <code>lang</code> attribute.</li><li>Videos have captions, and important videos have transcripts.</li><li>Navigation, buttons and links use real HTML elements.</li><li>Text meets WCAG contrast ratios.</li><li>Everything works with a keyboard, with visible focus.</li><li>Forms have visible labels and clear error messages.</li></ul><h2>The bottom line</h2><p>Accessibility and SEO overlap because both depend on clear, well-structured pages: meaningful alt text, logical headings, descriptive links, good titles and real HTML. Google does not reward accessibility as a ranking factor in itself, but the same fixes make your content easier for search engines to understand and for every visitor to use.</p><p>Start with an automated scan to find the obvious, repeated problems, then test with a keyboard and a screen reader, because tools only catch part of what matters. Fix issues at the template level so every page benefits.</p><p><strong>Your next step:</strong> put your mouse away and try to complete your most important task, such as booking or contacting you, using only the keyboard, then note every point where you got stuck or lost focus.</p>', '["Accessibility is not a Google ranking factor in itself, but many accessibility fixes also help search engines understand your pages.", "Alt text, clear headings, descriptive link text, page titles and semantic HTML serve screen reader users and search engines at the same time.", "WCAG is the international accessibility standard, organized into four principles and success criteria at levels A, AA and AAA.", "Automated tools such as axe-core find common issues quickly but cannot check everything, so human judgment is still required.", "Testing with only a keyboard and a free screen reader reveals problems that automated reports miss."]', '[{"question": "Is accessibility a Google ranking factor?", "answer": "Google does not list accessibility as a ranking factor. Many accessibility improvements, such as descriptive alt text, clear headings and crawlable links, also help Google understand your content, so the two often improve together."}, {"question": "What WCAG level should a small business website aim for?", "answer": "Level AA is the target most organizations and policies use, and it is a sensible goal for most sites. It includes the Level A criteria plus requirements such as minimum text contrast. Check which laws apply in your country, since some set specific requirements."}, {"question": "Can an accessibility overlay or plugin make my site compliant?", "answer": "No tool can fix everything automatically. A script cannot know what your photos show, whether your link text makes sense, or whether a form is understandable. Use tools to find problems, then fix them in your site''s HTML and content."}, {"question": "Which free screen readers can I test with?", "answer": "VoiceOver is built into macOS and iOS, TalkBack is built into Android, and NVDA is a free screen reader for Windows. Spending a few minutes navigating your key pages with one of them shows problems that automated tests miss."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'Accessibility', 'Accessibility and SEO: Where They Overlap', 'Accessibility and SEO share many fixes: alt text, headings, link text and semantic HTML. Learn WCAG basics and why automated tests only catch part of it.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = 'accessibility-and-seo');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT 'eeat-checklist', 'E-E-A-T in Practice: How to Show Experience on Every Page', 'E-E-A-T is not a score you can raise with a badge. Here is what it really means and the on-page signals that show experience and earn trust on every page.', '<p>Say you run a small home renovation business and publish a guide to sealing a leaky shower. The advice is solid. But the page has no author name, no photos of an actual shower, no date, and the "About" link goes to a single paragraph of marketing copy. From the outside, it looks exactly like a page written by someone who has never held a caulking gun, and that is an E-E-A-T problem.</p><p>That gap between what you know and what the page <em>shows</em> is what E-E-A-T is about. Google uses the term to describe the kind of content its systems try to reward: content that demonstrates experience, expertise, authoritativeness and trust.</p><p>There is a lot of confusion around it, too. Some people treat E-E-A-T as a score you can raise with a few badges. Others dismiss it because it is "not a ranking factor".</p><p>This guide sits between those two views. You will learn what E-E-A-T actually is, where it comes from, and a practical set of on-page signals you can add to any page, from author bios and first-hand evidence to honest dates and sources.</p><h2>What E-E-A-T actually is (and what it is not)</h2><p>E-E-A-T stands for Experience, Expertise, Authoritativeness and Trustworthiness. The framework comes from Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://static.googleusercontent.com/media/guidelines.raterhub.com/en//searchqualityevaluatorguidelines.pdf">Search Quality Rater Guidelines</a>, a long document that human raters use to evaluate the quality of search results.</p><p>The first "E" is the newest. Google <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/blog/2022/12/google-raters-guidelines-e-e-a-t">added Experience to the framework in December 2022</a>, to recognize content created by someone who has actually used the product, visited the place or done the thing.</p><p><strong>The key point: E-E-A-T is not a single ranking factor you can measure, but Google''s ranking systems look for signals that line up with it.</strong> Google''s own page on <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/creating-helpful-content">creating helpful, reliable, people-first content</a> says this directly. It also says that search raters have no control over how pages rank, and that of the four parts, trust is the most important.</p><p>So think of E-E-A-T as a description of a good page, not a checklist that triggers a boost. If you are new to how Google evaluates sites in general, start with our <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/dont-let-your-website-turn-invisible-a-beginner-guide">beginner''s guide to how SEO works</a> and come back here.</p><p>In practice, the four parts break down like this:</p><ul><li><strong>Experience:</strong> the author has first-hand involvement with the topic (used it, tested it, lived it).</li><li><strong>Expertise:</strong> the author has the knowledge or skill the topic needs.</li><li><strong>Authoritativeness:</strong> the author or site is a known, go-to source for the topic.</li><li><strong>Trust:</strong> the page is accurate, honest, safe and transparent about who is behind it.</li></ul><h2>Why E-E-A-T matters more for some topics</h2><p>Not every page needs the same level of proof. A recipe for banana bread and an article about medication dosages are held to very different standards.</p><p>Google calls the high-stakes category "Your Money or Your Life" (YMYL): topics that could significantly affect people''s health, financial stability, safety, or the welfare of society. For these, Google says its systems give even more weight to content with strong E-E-A-T, and that the content should be highly accurate and consistent with established expert consensus.</p><p>Here is how that plays out for a hypothetical small business site:</p><ul><li>A bakery''s post about its favorite sourdough starter: experience matters most. Photos and honest notes from the bakers do the job.</li><li>An accountant''s post on small business tax deadlines: this is YMYL. It needs a named, qualified author, current dates, and links to the official tax authority.</li><li>A pet store''s guide to toxic foods for dogs: also YMYL (animal health). Cite veterinary sources and say who reviewed the advice.</li></ul><p><strong>Action:</strong> list your ten most important pages and mark which ones touch health, money, safety or legal matters. Those get the full treatment below first.</p><h2>Signal 1: Real bylines and author pages</h2><p>Google''s helpful content guidance asks a simple question: is it self-evident to visitors who created the content? It goes on to ask whether pages carry a byline where one might be expected, and whether bylines lead to more information about the author.</p><p><strong>A byline is cheap to add, and it answers the first question every skeptical reader has: who wrote this?</strong></p><p>A useful author page includes:</p><ul><li>The person''s real name and a real photo.</li><li>Their role and what they actually do day to day ("Licensed electrician, 12 years in residential wiring" beats "Passionate content creator").</li><li>Relevant qualifications, certifications or memberships, only if they exist.</li><li>Links to their professional profiles or other work.</li><li>A list of the articles they have written on the site.</li></ul><p>Say your plumbing company blog is written by the owner and reviewed by a senior technician. Show both: "Written by Sam Lee, owner. Reviewed by Priya Shah, master plumber." Each name links to a short author page.</p><p>Do not invent people. Google explicitly warns against deceptive authorship, such as fabricated creator profiles with made-up names or AI-generated headshots. A company byline ("The Acme Plumbing team") is far better than a fake expert.</p><p>If you use Article structured data, you can also mark up the author name and author URL so the byline is machine-readable. Our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/structured-data-for-beginners">adding schema markup with JSON-LD</a> shows how a block like that is put together.</p><h2>Signal 2: First-hand evidence on the page</h2><p>Experience is the easiest part of E-E-A-T to show and the hardest for a competitor to copy. If you have done the thing, prove it on the page.</p><p>Evidence that works:</p><ul><li><strong>Original photos:</strong> your team on the job, the product on your workbench, the before and after.</li><li><strong>Screenshots:</strong> the actual settings screen, the actual report, with your own data.</li><li><strong>Test results:</strong> what you measured, how, and what happened. Include the boring details (dates, conditions, versions).</li><li><strong>Specifics only a practitioner knows:</strong> the step that usually goes wrong, the tool you stopped using and why.</li></ul><p>Compare two sentences from a hypothetical lawn mower review. "This mower is powerful and easy to use" could have been written by anyone. "On our half-acre test lawn, the battery lasted 41 minutes with the grass at about 10 cm, and the bag filled twice" could only come from someone who mowed the lawn.</p><p>This matters even more if you draft with AI tools. An AI draft has no experience to share, so it is up to you to add it. Our walkthrough on <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/ai-seo/5-steps-to-humanize-ai-content-save-seo">turning a raw AI draft into human content</a> covers how to edit for exactly this.</p><h2>Signal 3: Sources you would stake your name on</h2><p>Trust is built on accuracy, and accuracy is easier to judge when readers can check your claims.</p><p><strong>Link every statistic, threshold or rule to the place it came from, and prefer the primary source.</strong> If you mention a legal deadline, link the government page, not a blog that summarized it. If you quote a study, link the study.</p><ul><li>Remove any statistic you cannot trace to a source. Unverifiable numbers hurt trust more than they help.</li><li>Name the source in the sentence ("according to the national weather service...") so readers do not have to click to know who said it.</li><li>Re-check sources when you update a page. Links die and guidance changes.</li></ul><h2>Signal 4: About, contact and policy pages</h2><p>Trust is also a site-level question. A reader who likes your article will often click "About" before buying from you or acting on your advice.</p><p>A trustworthy small business site usually has:</p><ul><li>An <strong>About page</strong> that names the people behind the business, how long it has operated and where it is based.</li><li>A <strong>Contact page</strong> with real ways to reach you: a physical address if you have one, a phone number, an email address.</li><li><strong>Policies</strong> that fit the site: returns and shipping for a shop, an editorial or review policy for a content site, a privacy policy for anyone collecting data.</li><li>Clear disclosure of affiliate links or sponsored content.</li></ul><p>The rater guidelines put particular weight on who is responsible for a website and how customers can get help, especially for sites that take payments. An online store with no contact details is a red flag for real shoppers too.</p><h2>Signal 5: Honest dates and real updates</h2><p>Showing when a page was published or last updated helps readers judge whether advice is current. It also helps Google, which looks at several factors to estimate a page''s date.</p><p>Google''s guidance on <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/publication-dates">showing byline dates in search</a> recommends a clear, visible date on the page, matched by <code>datePublished</code> and <code>dateModified</code> in your structured data.</p><p><strong>Only change the "updated" date when you have substantially changed the content.</strong> Google''s helpful content page specifically lists changing dates to make pages seem fresh, when the content has not substantially changed, as a warning sign.</p><p>A good update note looks like this: "Updated 3 March: added the new filing deadline and removed the section on the retired form." It tells the reader what changed and why it matters to them.</p><h2>An E-E-A-T checklist for any page</h2><p>Use this as a pass over each important page. It works alongside a broader <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/on-page-seo-checklist">pre-publish on-page SEO checklist</a>, which covers titles, headings and internal links.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>What good looks like</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Who</td><td>Visible byline linking to a real author page; reviewer named for YMYL topics</td></tr><tr><td>Experience</td><td>At least one piece of original evidence: photo, screenshot, measurement or worked example</td></tr><tr><td>Sources</td><td>Every number and rule linked to a primary source</td></tr><tr><td>Site trust</td><td>About and contact pages one click away; relevant policies in place</td></tr><tr><td>Dates</td><td>Visible published or updated date that matches structured data and reflects real changes</td></tr><tr><td>Purpose</td><td>The page exists to help a reader, not just to rank for a keyword</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The last row comes from Google''s "Who, How, and Why" questions. "Who" is the author, "How" is how the content was made (including any use of automation), and "Why" is whether the main reason the page exists is to help people.</p><h2>E-E-A-T and AI search</h2><p>The same signals carry over to AI features in search. Google''s guidance for those features emphasizes unique, first-hand content over summaries of what is already online, which is the "Experience" part of E-E-A-T in different words.</p><p>If you want to go further, our guide on <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/ai-seo/optimize-for-ai-overviews">appearing in Google AI Overviews</a> explains what Google has said about AI search and what you can safely ignore.</p><h2>The bottom line</h2><p>E-E-A-T is not a switch you flip or a score you can check. It is Google''s description of content that deserves trust, and the practical work is making the experience and care behind your pages visible: real authors, original evidence, cited sources, clear contact details and honest dates.</p><p>Start with the pages where accuracy matters most to your readers, especially anything touching health, money or safety, and work outward from there. Every signal you add helps real visitors as much as it helps search engines.</p><p><strong>Your next step:</strong> pick your single most important article today, add a real byline that links to an author page, and add one piece of first-hand evidence (a photo, screenshot or measurement) that only your business could have produced.</p>', '["E-E-A-T comes from Google''s Search Quality Rater Guidelines and is not a single ranking factor, but Google''s systems look for signals that align with it.", "Trust is the most important part of E-E-A-T, and experience, expertise and authority all contribute to it.", "Real bylines that link to author pages are one of the simplest ways to show who is behind your content.", "Original photos, screenshots and test results show first-hand experience that summaries and AI drafts cannot copy.", "Only change an updated date when the content has substantially changed, and keep visible dates consistent with your structured data."]', '[{"question": "Is E-E-A-T a Google ranking factor?", "answer": "Not as a single measurable factor. Google says E-E-A-T itself is not a specific ranking factor, but its systems use a mix of signals that help identify content with good experience, expertise, authoritativeness and trust. Improving those signals tends to make pages more useful to readers as well."}, {"question": "What does the extra E in E-E-A-T stand for?", "answer": "The first E stands for Experience. Google added it to its quality rater guidelines in December 2022 to recognize content created by people with first-hand experience of the topic, such as someone who has actually used a product or visited a place."}, {"question": "Do I need an author bio on every blog post?", "answer": "Google encourages accurate bylines wherever readers would expect them, and most blog posts fall into that category. A short byline linking to a fuller author page is usually enough. If several people work on a post, you can name both the writer and the reviewer."}, {"question": "Can AI-written content have good E-E-A-T?", "answer": "AI tools have no first-hand experience, so an unedited AI draft rarely shows it. If you use AI to help write, add your own evidence, check every fact against a source, and make sure a real, accountable person stands behind the page."}, {"question": "What are YMYL pages?", "answer": "YMYL stands for Your Money or Your Life. It covers topics that could significantly affect a person''s health, finances, safety or the welfare of society. Google holds these pages to a higher standard of accuracy and trust."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'AI SEO', 'E-E-A-T Checklist: How to Show Experience and Trust', 'What E-E-A-T really is, why it is not a single ranking factor, and a practical checklist of author bios, evidence, sources and dates that show trust.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = 'eeat-checklist');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT 'optimize-for-ai-overviews', 'AI Overviews SEO: How to Show Up in Google''s AI Search', 'What Google has actually said about getting cited in AI Overviews and AI Mode: the one technical requirement, practical steps that help, and the hacks to skip.', '<p>You search for a question your business answers every day, and an AI Overview appears at the top of the results. It cites three websites. None of them is yours, and at least one says something you know is out of date. Welcome to the newest corner of SEO: AI Overviews.</p><p>Then you go looking for advice on how to get cited and find a flood of new acronyms (AEO, GEO), "AI-ready" content formats, special files to upload, and services promising to get you cited by chatbots.</p><p>Most of that advice is guesswork. Google has published fairly direct guidance on how its AI features choose sources, and it is less exotic than the sales pitches suggest.</p><p>This guide sticks to what Google has said publicly. You will learn how AI Overviews find content, the one technical requirement that matters, the practical steps that improve your chances, and the "hacks" you can safely skip.</p><h2>How Google''s AI features choose their sources</h2><p>Google''s AI Overviews and AI Mode do not use a separate index of the web. According to Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/ai-optimization-guide">guide to optimizing for generative AI search</a>, these features are rooted in its core Search ranking and quality systems.</p><p>Two techniques do most of the work:</p><ul><li><strong>Grounding (retrieval-augmented generation):</strong> the system retrieves relevant pages from Google''s search index and uses them to build and support the answer, with links back to those pages.</li><li><strong>Query fan-out:</strong> the model runs several related searches behind the scenes. A question like "how to fix a lawn full of weeds" might also trigger searches about herbicides or chemical-free weeding.</li></ul><p><strong>In plain English: if a page does not do well in regular Google Search, it is unlikely to be used in an AI answer.</strong> Fan-out can surface pages for related sub-questions, though, which gives a thorough page more than one way in.</p><p>Google also notes that AI Overviews only appear when its systems decide they add something beyond the classic results, so many queries never show one at all.</p><h2>The one requirement: be indexed and snippet-eligible</h2><p>Google''s page on <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/ai-features">AI features and your website</a> is clear: to be shown as a supporting link in AI Overviews or AI Mode, a page must be indexed and eligible to be shown in Google Search with a snippet. There are no additional technical requirements.</p><p>That rules out pages that are:</p><ul><li>Blocked from crawling in robots.txt (or by a CDN or firewall that blocks Googlebot).</li><li>Marked <code>noindex</code>.</li><li>Restricted with <code>nosnippet</code>, or with a very low <code>max-snippet</code> value.</li><li>Not indexed for other reasons, such as duplication, weak content or a canonical that points elsewhere.</li></ul><p>So the first job is ordinary technical SEO. Check your key pages in Search Console''s URL Inspection tool, and if any of them show as excluded, work through our guide on <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/why-google-is-not-indexing-your-pages">fixing pages Google won''t index</a>.</p><p>To check a whole site at once, a free desktop crawler like <a href="https://www.scaryspiderseo.com">Scary Spider SEO</a> can flag noindex directives (including the <code>X-Robots-Tag</code> header), canonical problems and non-200 status codes across every page it crawls.</p><p>If your site relies on JavaScript to load its main content, make sure that content still reaches Google. Our walkthrough on <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/web-performance/javascript-seo-rendering">how rendering hides content from Google</a> explains how to compare raw and rendered HTML.</p><h2>AI overviews SEO in practice: what to do</h2><p>Once a page is eligible, the factors that decide whether it is used are the same ones that decide whether it ranks: relevance, quality and usefulness. Here is how to apply them.</p><h3>1. Answer the question clearly, early</h3><p>If a page targets a question, answer it in the first paragraph or two, in plain language, before the background and caveats.</p><p>Say you run a garden center and write about when to plant tulip bulbs. A strong opening is: "Plant tulip bulbs in autumn, roughly six to eight weeks before the ground freezes." Then explain soil temperature, regional timing and depth.</p><p>This is good writing for people first. A clear, direct answer is also easy for any system to find and quote accurately.</p><h3>2. Structure the page for readers</h3><p>Google says people appreciate pages organized into sections with clear headings. Use descriptive H2 and H3 headings, numbered lists for steps, and tables for comparisons.</p><p><strong>You do not need to "chunk" content into tiny pieces for AI.</strong> Google''s guide says its systems can understand multiple topics on a page and pull out the relevant part, and that there is no ideal page length.</p><h3>3. Add something that is not already everywhere</h3><p>Google''s AI guidance makes a distinction between commodity content, which repeats common knowledge, and non-commodity content, which adds a unique, expert or first-hand perspective.</p><p>A generic "7 tips for first-time homebuyers" list adds little. A page explaining exactly how your team handled a specific type of inspection problem, with photos and numbers, gives AI systems and people something they cannot get elsewhere.</p><p>This is the same idea behind experience in our <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/ai-seo/eeat-checklist">practical guide to E-E-A-T signals</a>. If you draft with AI tools, the step that matters most is adding your own knowledge back in, as covered in our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/ai-seo/5-steps-to-humanize-ai-content-save-seo">editing AI drafts into human content</a>.</p><h3>4. Keep facts verifiable</h3><p>AI answers are built from facts pulled from pages. Make yours easy to trust and easy to check:</p><ul><li>Put specific facts (prices, dates, opening hours, specs) in text, not only inside images.</li><li>Link claims to primary sources.</li><li>Show a real, visible updated date and keep it honest.</li><li>Fix outdated pages instead of publishing new ones next to them.</li></ul><h3>5. Use structured data that matches the page</h3><p>Structured data is not required for AI features, and Google says there is no special schema.org markup for them. It is still worth using for rich result eligibility, and it helps describe your content precisely.</p><p>The rule that matters: markup must describe what is visible on the page. Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/sd-policies">general structured data guidelines</a> say not to mark up content that readers cannot see. If you are new to it, start with our <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/structured-data-for-beginners">beginner''s guide to schema markup</a>.</p><h3>6. Earn real mentions</h3><p>AI features can reflect what is said about a business across the web, in blogs, videos and forum discussions. Google''s guidance also warns that seeking inauthentic mentions is not as helpful as it might seem, because its systems are built to favor quality and block spam.</p><p>The work here is the same as good off-page SEO: be genuinely useful in the places your customers already talk, and give journalists, partners and community members a reason to reference you.</p><h2>What you can safely skip</h2><p>Google''s AI optimization guide includes a short "mythbusting" list. For Google Search, you can ignore:</p><ul><li><strong>llms.txt and other AI text files.</strong> The <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://llmstxt.org/">llms.txt proposal</a> is a community convention for a Markdown file that summarizes a site for language models. Google says Google Search does not use these files. Creating one will neither help nor harm your visibility in Google, though other services may use it.</li><li><strong>Chunking content</strong> into small pieces specifically for AI.</li><li><strong>Rewriting pages</strong> in a special style for AI. Google says its systems understand synonyms and meaning, so you do not need every long-tail variation.</li><li><strong>Special schema</strong> for AI features. There is none.</li><li><strong>Mass-producing pages</strong> for every variation of a question. Google says doing this primarily to manipulate rankings or AI answers violates its scaled content abuse policy.</li></ul><p>Treat any service that promises guaranteed AI citations, or claims access to internal Google metrics, with suspicion. Google states that no third-party tool has access to its internal ranking or AI systems.</p><h2>How to measure and control AI visibility</h2><p>Traffic from AI Overviews and AI Mode is counted in Search Console''s Performance report, under the "Web" search type, alongside your other Google Search clicks. Google''s AI optimization guide also points to a Generative AI performance report in Search Console for a closer look.</p><p>A practical routine:</p><ol><li>In the Performance report, filter for your most important question-style queries and note impressions and clicks over time.</li><li>Search those queries yourself, and note whether an AI Overview appears and which sources it links to.</li><li>Compare your page with the cited sources. Is your answer as clear and specific? Is it more up to date?</li><li>Improve the page, then check again after Google has had time to recrawl it.</li></ol><p>If you want to limit what Google shows from a page, the controls are the same as for regular snippets: <code>nosnippet</code>, <code>data-nosnippet</code>, <code>max-snippet</code> or <code>noindex</code>. Use them deliberately, because they also reduce how the page appears in normal results.</p><h2>The bottom line</h2><p>AI overviews SEO is mostly SEO. Google''s AI features pull from the same index and lean on the same ranking systems, so a page has to be indexed, snippet-eligible and genuinely useful before it has any chance of being cited.</p><p>The edge comes from content others cannot copy: clear answers, real experience, verifiable facts and structured data that matches what is on the page. Skip the files and formats Google has said it does not use, and spend that time making your best pages better.</p><p><strong>Your next step:</strong> pick the five pages that answer your customers'' most common questions, confirm each one is indexed in Search Console, and rewrite each opening paragraph so it answers the question directly in one or two sentences.</p>', '["Google says there are no additional requirements to appear in AI Overviews or AI Mode beyond being indexed and eligible to show with a snippet.", "Google''s AI features are rooted in its core ranking systems, so ordinary SEO fundamentals still decide which pages get used.", "Clear early answers, helpful structure and first-hand, non-commodity content give a page the best chance of being cited.", "Google Search does not use llms.txt or other AI text files, and there is no special schema for AI features.", "AI feature traffic is counted in Search Console''s Performance report under the Web search type."]', '[{"question": "How do I get my website featured in Google AI Overviews?", "answer": "There is no form to apply and no special markup. Make sure the page is indexed and eligible to show with a snippet, then focus on clear answers, helpful structure and original content. Google chooses sources using its core ranking systems, so the same fundamentals apply."}, {"question": "Does Google use llms.txt?", "answer": "No. Google says Google Search does not use llms.txt or similar AI text files, so adding one will neither help nor hurt your visibility in Google. Other AI services may read the file, so creating one is a separate decision."}, {"question": "Can I stop my content from appearing in AI Overviews?", "answer": "You can use the same preview controls that apply to regular search snippets, such as nosnippet, data-nosnippet, max-snippet or noindex. These also affect how your page appears in normal search results, so use them carefully."}, {"question": "Is GEO different from SEO?", "answer": "Google''s view is that optimizing for its generative AI search features is still SEO. Terms like GEO and AEO describe the same goal, and Google recommends evaluating any advice sold under those labels against its official guidance."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'AI SEO', 'AI Overviews SEO: How to Get Cited in Google AI Search', 'AI overviews SEO grounded in Google''s own guidance: be indexed and snippet-eligible, answer clearly, add real experience, and skip llms.txt and hacks.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = 'optimize-for-ai-overviews');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT 'broken-link-building', 'Broken Link Building: A Step-by-Step Outreach Process', 'Find dead links on resource pages, learn what they covered, offer a better replacement and write an outreach email that gets read, without turning into spam.', '<p>Resource pages rot. A city council publishes a "home composting resources" page, links to a dozen helpful guides, and then nobody touches it for three years. Some of those guides move, some sites shut down, and the page quietly fills with dead links. For broken link building, that neglect is an opportunity.</p><p>Now say you run a site about home composting, and you have a thorough guide that covers the same ground as one of those dead links. The page owner has a problem (a broken link that frustrates their readers) and you have a fix. That is broken link building in one sentence.</p><p>Done well, it is one of the few outreach tactics where you lead with something useful instead of a request. Done badly, it is just another spam email.</p><p>This guide walks through the full process: finding the right pages, checking links at scale, working out what the dead page covered, building a replacement, writing an email people actually read, and tracking it all. If you are new to link building in general, our <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/offpage-seo/off-page-seo-guide">no-nonsense guide to off-page SEO</a> covers why links matter in the first place.</p><h2>Why broken link building works</h2><p>Most outreach asks a stranger for a favor. Broken link building starts by doing them one: you have found an error on their site and you are telling them about it.</p><p><strong>The link is a by-product of a genuinely helpful report, which is why it gets better responses than a cold "please link to us".</strong></p><p>It also stays on the right side of Google''s rules when you do it honestly. Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/essentials/spam-policies#link-spam">spam policies on link spam</a> cover things like buying or selling links for ranking, excessive link exchanges and automated link creation. Suggesting a relevant replacement that the editor is free to accept or ignore is none of those.</p><p>It is not a fast tactic. Many emails go unanswered, and some site owners just delete the dead link. Plan for steady, small wins rather than a sudden jump in links.</p><h2>Step 1: Pick a niche you can serve well</h2><p>Start with topics where you already have, or can realistically create, the best page on the web. Broken link building fails when your replacement is weaker than what was lost.</p><ul><li>List three to five topics your business knows deeply.</li><li>For each, note the page on your site that could replace a dead resource, or the page you would need to create.</li><li>Favor evergreen topics (how-to guides, checklists, glossaries, tools). Resource pages link to these far more often than to news or promotions.</li></ul><p>A hypothetical bike repair shop might pick "fixing a flat tire", "basic bike maintenance schedule" and "choosing a bike lock". All three are things a school, cycling club or city transport page might list as resources.</p><h2>Step 2: Find resource pages in your niche</h2><p>Resource pages are the best hunting ground because their whole job is linking out. Use Google search operators to find them:</p><ul><li><code>bike maintenance intitle:resources</code></li><li><code>cycling inurl:links</code></li><li><code>"useful links" bike repair</code></li><li><code>"recommended resources" cycling safety</code></li></ul><p>Look for pages on sites with a real audience: schools, libraries, clubs, local government, associations and established blogs. Skip link farms, pages with hundreds of unrelated links, and anything that looks built only to sell links.</p><p>Collect the URLs in a spreadsheet as you go. Fifty relevant resource pages is a good first batch.</p><h2>Step 3: Crawl those pages to find dead outbound links</h2><p>Clicking every link by hand does not scale. Instead, crawl the resource pages and check the status code of every outbound link.</p><p>Any link checker or crawler that reports the HTTP status code of each outbound link will do. Keep the check limited to the resource page itself rather than the whole site, and run it at a polite speed so you are not hammering someone else''s server.</p><p>When you review the results, sort dead links into groups:</p><ul><li><strong>Clear 404 or 410:</strong> the page is gone. Best candidates.</li><li><strong>Domain no longer resolves:</strong> the whole site is gone. Also strong candidates.</li><li><strong>Redirects to an unrelated homepage:</strong> technically not broken, but the original resource is lost. Worth checking manually. Our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/301-vs-302-redirects">301 vs 302 redirects</a> explains why these behave differently from a true 404.</li><li><strong>Timeouts or 5xx errors:</strong> could be temporary. Re-check a few days later before contacting anyone.</li></ul><p>Before you email anyone, make sure your own house is in order: a site with broken links of its own makes a weak case. A free desktop crawler like <a href="https://www.scaryspiderseo.com">Scary Spider SEO</a> flags 4xx and 5xx responses and broken links across your site, and our guide on how to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/technical-seo/find-and-fix-broken-links">find and fix broken links</a> walks through fixing them.</p><h2>Step 4: Find out what the dead page used to cover</h2><p>Before you pitch a replacement, you need to know what the original actually offered. The Internet Archive''s Wayback Machine is the standard tool for this: <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://help.archive.org/help/using-the-wayback-machine/">enter the dead URL into the Wayback Machine</a> and browse its saved snapshots.</p><p>For each dead link, note:</p><ul><li>The topic and the angle (beginner guide, checklist, tool, research).</li><li>Roughly how long and detailed it was.</li><li>Whether other resource pages also link to it. A dead page linked from many places is worth more effort, because one replacement can be pitched to all of them.</li></ul><p>Some URLs will have no archived copy. In that case, use the anchor text and the surrounding text on the resource page to work out what it covered.</p><h2>Step 5: Create or match a replacement</h2><p>Now compare the dead page with what you have.</p><ul><li><strong>You already have a close match:</strong> make sure it covers everything the original did, and update it if it is thin or out of date.</li><li><strong>You have something partly related:</strong> expand it, or add a section that covers the missing ground.</li><li><strong>You have nothing:</strong> only create a new page if the topic fits your site and you can make it genuinely better. If several dead links point to the same lost resource, the effort is easier to justify.</li></ul><p><strong>Do not copy the archived content.</strong> It belongs to someone else. Use it to understand the scope, then write your own page with your own examples and knowledge.</p><h2>Step 6: Write a short, honest outreach email</h2><p>The email has one job: report the broken link clearly and offer an option, without pressure. Keep it under 120 words. Use the person''s name if the site lists it, and send it from a real address at your own domain.</p><pre><code>Subject: Broken link on your cycling resources page

Hi Jordan,

I was reading the cycling resources page on the Riverside
Cycle Club site and noticed the "Fixing a flat tire" link
goes to a page that no longer exists (it returns a 404).

If you are looking for a replacement, we have a step-by-step
flat repair guide here: https://example.com/fix-a-flat

Either way, I thought you would want to know about the dead link.

Thanks,
Alex
Example Bike Workshop</code></pre><p>What makes this work:</p><ul><li>The subject line describes the problem, not your request.</li><li>It names the exact page and the exact dead link, so the reader can verify it in seconds.</li><li>The suggestion is optional, and the last line makes the email useful even if they ignore it.</li></ul><p>Never offer payment or a link swap in exchange. If anyone asks for money to place the link, that is a paid link, and Google asks that paid placements be <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/qualify-outbound-links">marked as sponsored links</a> using <code>rel="sponsored"</code>. That is no longer broken link building, so walk away.</p><h2>Step 7: Follow up once, then stop</h2><p>People are busy and emails get buried. One polite follow-up, about a week after the first email, is reasonable. Keep it to two sentences and reply in the same thread.</p><p>After that, stop. Repeated follow-ups turn a helpful note into spam, and a site owner who feels pestered will not link to you.</p><p>If someone replies asking you not to contact them, remove them from your list immediately.</p><h2>Step 8: Track everything in one sheet</h2><p>A simple spreadsheet keeps you from emailing the same person twice and shows you what is working.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>What to record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Resource page</td><td>URL of the page with the dead link</td></tr><tr><td>Dead link</td><td>The broken URL and its status (404, DNS error, and so on)</td></tr><tr><td>Your replacement</td><td>The page you suggested</td></tr><tr><td>Contact</td><td>Name and email, plus how you found it</td></tr><tr><td>Sent / follow-up</td><td>Dates for each</td></tr><tr><td>Outcome</td><td>Linked, removed dead link, declined, no reply</td></tr></tbody></table><p>After your first batch, look at the outcomes. If one type of site (say, school pages) responds much better than others, focus there next time. If nobody links, the replacement pages are usually the issue, not the email.</p><h2>Ethics: keep it helpful, not spammy</h2><p>A few rules keep this tactic clean:</p><ul><li>Only suggest pages that are genuinely relevant and at least as good as what was lost.</li><li>Send individual emails, not mass blasts with mail-merge placeholders.</li><li>Do not create fake personas or pretend to be a reader if you are the site owner.</li><li>Respect a "no" and any request not to be contacted.</li></ul><p>Broken link building works best as one part of a wider presence. Being active and useful in the communities your audience already uses, as covered in our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/offpage-seo/offpage-seo-with-social-group-strategies">earning mentions through online communities</a>, makes your name familiar before your email ever lands.</p><h2>The bottom line</h2><p>Broken link building turns outreach around: you start by reporting a real problem and offering a real fix. The process is simple (find resource pages, crawl them for dead links, check what was lost, build something better, send a short email), but it rewards patience and quality over volume.</p><p>Expect most emails to go unanswered and treat every link you do earn as a sign your content is genuinely useful. Keep it honest, keep it relevant, and never pay or trade for the link.</p><p><strong>Your next step:</strong> find ten resource pages in your niche with the search operators above, crawl them for 404s, and write down which dead links your existing content could already replace.</p>', '["Broken link building works because you start by reporting a real problem on someone else''s site and offering a useful fix.", "Resource pages on schools, clubs, associations and established blogs are the best places to look for dead outbound links.", "Crawling resource pages and checking outbound status codes finds 404s and dead domains far faster than clicking links by hand.", "The Wayback Machine shows what a dead page used to cover, so your replacement can match or beat it without copying it.", "Send short, individual emails, follow up only once, and never pay or trade for the link."]', '[{"question": "Is broken link building still effective?", "answer": "It can be, as long as your replacement content is genuinely relevant and useful. Many emails will not get a reply, so it works best as a steady, long-term tactic rather than a quick win. Quality of the replacement page matters more than the number of emails sent."}, {"question": "Is broken link building against Google''s guidelines?", "answer": "Suggesting a relevant replacement that the site owner is free to accept is not link spam. It becomes a problem if you pay for the link, set up link swaps, or use automated programs or services to create links. Paid placements should be marked with rel=sponsored."}, {"question": "How do I find broken links on other websites?", "answer": "Find resource pages with search operators such as intitle:resources or inurl:links plus your topic, then crawl those pages and check the status code of each outbound link. Links that return 404, 410 or point to domains that no longer exist are the strongest candidates."}, {"question": "How many follow-up emails should I send?", "answer": "One follow-up, about a week after the first email, is enough. More than that tends to annoy site owners and makes your outreach look like spam. Always stop if someone asks you not to contact them again."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'Offpage SEO', 'Broken Link Building: A Step-by-Step Outreach Guide', 'A practical broken link building process: find resource pages, crawl for dead links, check the Wayback Machine, build a replacement and email politely.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = 'broken-link-building');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, key_takeaways, faqs, author_id, category, meta_title, meta_description, status, created_at, updated_at) SELECT 'local-seo-basics', 'Local SEO Basics: Google Business Profile, Citations, Reviews', 'How Google picks local results, and how to set up your Business Profile, fix citations, earn reviews within the rules and build local pages that rank well.', '<p>Say you run a dog grooming salon. Someone two streets away searches "dog groomer near me" on their phone. Google shows a map with a handful of businesses, and the person taps one of those three or four names. If yours is not there, you did not lose a ranking battle. You were never in the room. That is the problem local SEO solves.</p><p>That map-and-listings view is where local SEO lives. It is driven less by your website alone and more by your Google Business Profile, how consistently your business details appear across the web, and what customers say about you.</p><p>This guide covers the basics a small business can handle itself: how Google decides which local results to show, how to set up and optimize a Business Profile, citations and NAP consistency, reviews within Google''s rules, local landing pages and LocalBusiness structured data.</p><p>Local listings and citations are one part of off-page SEO. Our <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/offpage-seo/off-page-seo-guide">guide to off-page SEO and authority</a> covers the wider picture, including how links and mentions build trust.</p><h2>How local SEO results are chosen</h2><p>Google''s help page on <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://support.google.com/business/answer/7091">improving your local ranking</a> says local results are mainly based on three things:</p><ul><li><strong>Relevance:</strong> how well a Business Profile matches what someone is searching for.</li><li><strong>Distance:</strong> how far the business is from the person searching. If they do not share their location, Google uses what it knows about it.</li><li><strong>Prominence:</strong> how well known the business is. Google says this is also based on information such as how many websites link to your business and how many reviews you have.</li></ul><p><strong>You cannot change your distance from a searcher, so local SEO is the work of improving relevance and prominence.</strong> Google also states there is no way to request or pay for a better local ranking.</p><p>That shapes your priorities. A complete, accurate profile improves relevance. Reviews, consistent citations and a well-known local reputation improve prominence.</p><h2>Set up and optimize your Google Business Profile</h2><p>Your Business Profile is the single most important local SEO asset. Claim it, verify it, and then make it complete.</p><h3>Get the basics exactly right</h3><ul><li><strong>Verify the profile.</strong> Google says verification makes the business more likely to show up in results.</li><li><strong>Name:</strong> use your real-world business name, exactly as it appears on your signage and stationery.</li><li><strong>Primary category:</strong> choose the most specific category that describes your core business ("Dog groomer", not just "Pet store"). Add secondary categories only if they are true.</li><li><strong>Address or service area:</strong> use your real location. If you visit customers rather than having them visit you, set up a service area.</li><li><strong>Hours:</strong> keep regular hours current and add special hours for holidays.</li><li><strong>Phone and website:</strong> use a local number you answer, and link to the most relevant page of your site.</li></ul><h3>Avoid the shortcuts that get profiles suspended</h3><p>Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://support.google.com/business/answer/3038177">guidelines for representing your business</a> are strict, and breaking them can lead to suspension. The most common mistakes:</p><ul><li>Adding keywords or a location to the name. "Paws &amp; Claws" is fine. "Paws &amp; Claws Best Dog Grooming Springfield" is not, unless that is genuinely your registered, signposted name.</li><li>Using a virtual office or a mailbox address you do not operate from.</li><li>Creating more than one profile for the same location.</li><li>Choosing categories as keywords rather than to describe what you actually are.</li></ul><h3>Fill in the rest</h3><p>Complete every relevant section: business description, services or products, attributes (such as wheelchair access or parking), and photos of your premises, team and work. Google''s help pages note that businesses with complete and accurate information are more likely to show up for relevant searches.</p><p><strong>Action:</strong> open your profile and fill in every empty field you can answer honestly. Then add at least five recent photos of real work.</p><h2>NAP consistency and local citations</h2><p>NAP stands for name, address and phone number. A citation is any mention of your business with those details: directories, industry listings, local chamber of commerce pages, review sites and social profiles.</p><p>Citations matter because they help Google and customers confirm that your business is real and where it is. The problem is inconsistency, which builds up over years as businesses move, change numbers or rebrand.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Where it appears</th><th>What it says</th><th>Problem</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Business Profile</td><td>Paws &amp; Claws, 12 High St, Unit 3</td><td>Correct (the reference)</td></tr><tr><td>Old directory listing</td><td>Paws and Claws Grooming, 40 Mill Rd</td><td>Old address from before a move</td></tr><tr><td>Website footer</td><td>Paws &amp; Claws, 12 High Street</td><td>Missing unit number</td></tr><tr><td>Social profile</td><td>Old mobile number</td><td>Wrong phone</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Small formatting differences ("St" vs "Street") are usually harmless. Wrong addresses, old phone numbers and outdated names are what confuse customers and dilute trust.</p><p>A simple cleanup process:</p><ol><li>Decide on one canonical version of your name, address and phone, matching your Business Profile.</li><li>Make sure your own website uses it exactly, in the footer and on the contact page.</li><li>Search for your business name, old addresses and old phone numbers to find existing listings.</li><li>Update or claim each listing, starting with the biggest directories and industry sites in your country and niche.</li><li>Keep a spreadsheet of every listing and its login, so the next change takes minutes rather than days.</li></ol><h2>Earn and respond to reviews, within Google''s rules</h2><p>Reviews feed prominence directly, and they are often the deciding factor for the person comparing three businesses on a map.</p><p><strong>Ask every happy customer, but never pay, bribe or filter.</strong> Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://support.google.com/contributionpolicy/answer/7400114">policy on fake engagement</a> prohibits businesses from:</p><ul><li>Offering incentives (discounts, free services, payment) in exchange for reviews, or for changing or removing a negative review.</li><li>Discouraging or prohibiting negative reviews.</li><li>Selectively asking only happy customers for reviews (often called review gating).</li></ul><p>What you can do is ask everyone, consistently. Google lets you create a review link or QR code from your profile. Put it on receipts, in follow-up emails and on a card at the counter.</p><p>Respond to reviews, too. Thank people specifically, and answer negative reviews calmly: acknowledge the issue, explain what you can do, and offer to continue the conversation privately. Future customers read your replies as much as the reviews themselves.</p><h2>Build local landing pages that deserve to rank</h2><p>Your website still matters for local SEO. Your Business Profile links to it, and it can rank in the regular results below the map.</p><ul><li><strong>One location:</strong> make your home page and contact page clearly local, with your full NAP, opening hours, an embedded map and the areas you serve.</li><li><strong>Several locations:</strong> give each real location its own page, with its own address, hours, staff, photos and details.</li><li><strong>Service areas:</strong> create area pages only where you genuinely serve customers and have something specific to say (local jobs, local regulations, travel times).</li></ul><p>Avoid near-identical pages that swap in a town name. Google''s spam policies treat pages created mainly to rank for slight variations of a search, like this, as doorway pages.</p><p>Each location page should also pass the basics in our <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/on-page-seo-checklist">on-page SEO checklist</a>: a clear title, one H1, descriptive headings and useful content.</p><h2>Add LocalBusiness structured data</h2><p>LocalBusiness structured data describes your business in a machine-readable format: name, address, phone, hours and more. Google''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/local-business">LocalBusiness structured data documentation</a> lists <code>name</code> and <code>address</code> as required, with properties like <code>telephone</code>, <code>url</code>, <code>geo</code> and <code>openingHoursSpecification</code> recommended.</p><p>A minimal example:</p><pre><code>&lt;script type="application/ld+json"&gt;
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "LocalBusiness",
  "name": "Paws &amp; Claws",
  "address": {
    "@type": "PostalAddress",
    "streetAddress": "12 High Street, Unit 3",
    "addressLocality": "Springfield",
    "postalCode": "12345",
    "addressCountry": "US"
  },
  "telephone": "+1-555-0100",
  "url": "https://example.com/"
}
&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre><p>Use the most specific subtype that fits, and keep the details identical to what is visible on the page and in your Business Profile. One common mistake: adding <code>aggregateRating</code> with your own reviews. Google recommends that property only for sites that capture reviews about other local businesses.</p><p>For a walkthrough of how JSON-LD works and how to test it, see our <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/on-page-seo/structured-data-for-beginners">beginner''s guide to structured data</a>.</p><h2>Show real people and real experience</h2><p>Local customers want to know who will turn up at their door or greet them at the counter. Name your team, show photos of real jobs, and explain how long you have served the area. Our guide to <a href="https://blog.scaryspiderseo.com/ai-seo/eeat-checklist">showing experience and trust on every page</a> covers these signals in more detail.</p><h2>The bottom line</h2><p>Local SEO comes down to three things you control: a complete and accurate Google Business Profile, consistent business details everywhere you are listed, and a steady flow of genuine reviews that you respond to. Distance is out of your hands, so relevance and prominence are where the work goes.</p><p>Your website backs it all up with clear local pages and structured data that matches what customers see. None of it is complicated, but it does need regular attention, because details go stale quietly.</p><p><strong>Your next step:</strong> open your Google Business Profile today, correct anything that does not match your signage and website exactly, fill every empty field, and set up a review link to share with your next ten customers.</p>', '["Google says local results are mainly based on relevance, distance and prominence, and there is no way to pay for a better local ranking.", "A verified, complete and accurate Google Business Profile is the most important local SEO asset for a small business.", "Your business name, address and phone number should match exactly across your profile, website and directory listings.", "Google prohibits offering incentives for reviews, discouraging negative reviews, and asking only happy customers for reviews.", "LocalBusiness structured data requires a name and address, and should match what customers see on the page and in your profile."]', '[{"question": "How long does local SEO take to work?", "answer": "Fixing errors in your Business Profile can make a difference fairly quickly once Google processes the change, but building prominence through reviews and citations takes months of steady effort. There is no fixed timeline, because it depends on your competition and how established your business already is."}, {"question": "Can I add keywords to my Google Business Profile name?", "answer": "No. Google''s guidelines say your profile name must match your real-world business name as used on your signage and stationery. Adding keywords or locations that are not part of that name can lead to suspension."}, {"question": "Is it allowed to ask customers for Google reviews?", "answer": "Yes. Google allows businesses to ask customers to share genuine experiences. What is not allowed is offering incentives, discouraging negative reviews, or only asking customers you expect to be happy."}, {"question": "Do I need a website for local SEO?", "answer": "You can appear in local results with only a Business Profile, but a website helps. It gives your profile a page to link to, can rank in the regular results below the map, and lets you add LocalBusiness structured data and detailed location pages."}]', (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = 'lthung_0412'), 'Offpage SEO', 'Local SEO Basics: Business Profile, Citations, Reviews', 'Local SEO basics for small businesses: how Google ranks local results, optimizing your Business Profile, NAP consistency, reviews and LocalBusiness schema.', 'draft', 1791244662, 1791244662 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM posts WHERE slug = 'local-seo-basics');
